Gutach: Even more safety for pedestrians
They are not 100 meters apart: On Tuesday, the new B 33 pedestrian tamper at the village parking lot was put into operation - within sight of the older town hall traffic lights.
Two facilities so close to each other: intention or shield civic coup
Gutach's mayor clearly answered this question yesterday.
"The town hall traffic light was installed at that time because it secures the way to school," Eckert explained yesterday.
The Kluser traffic lights secure both cyclists and bus passengers and Bergle residents.
The plant, which was officially put into operation yesterday, is important for the Sulzbachweg/Kirchstraße intersection area.
We have the museum, two churches, spa park, the bus stop, a doctor and a bank as well as the flow of traffic from the ›Grub‹ residential area.
"With the high volume of traffic and pedestrians, there had to be another traffic light for their safety," says Eckert.
This is also confirmed by Peter Arnold from the district office of Offenburg.
"According to current measurements, about 12 000 vehicles pass through the municipality of Gutach every day on the B 33, of which about ten percent are heavy-duty traffic," Arnold emphasizes.
Therefore, the construction of another traffic light is more than necessary: "Security is easy here," says Arnold.
A total of four traffic shows had been carried out, also a roundabout was considered, but discarded again because of the narrowness in the Sulzbachweg/Kirchstraße intersection area.
According to Arnold, everything has been tested beforehand during the selection of traffic light poles: "By means of a transporter specially loaded for us with particularly long wooden trunks, we tested whether these vehicles can extend from the Sulzbachweg to the B 33 without bending over the traffic light poles".
The traffic light system itself, which costs around 15 000 euros, is the "most modernal thing currently available on the market," Arnold explained.
The system is equipped with colored LEDs, which shine so strong that the lights can be easily recognized by the motorists, for example, even in the case of low sun.
And it is also economical: the older lighting systems consume about 100 watts, the new just eight watts.
There are three lighting systems per direction of travel.
Arnold explained the technology of the new system: This is equipped with two radar sensors.
If the pedestrian pushes the traffic light button, the upper radar sensor tests the traffic situation.
If the roast is cleared, there is immediate green for the pedestrian, if not, it takes about 15 seconds.
Another radar sensor checks whether the green phase for the pedestrian can be terminated.
"Should a group or disabled people walk across the road, the green phase will be extended, so everyone will come safely across the carriageway," Arnold explained.
Of course, the motorist has to think along here as a partner and observe the road.
This was not the case yesterday: Hardly showed the traffic light for pedestrians green, an upper-class vehicle rushed through - with bright red.
Josef Winkler has been writing the needs of his childhood and adolescence from the soul for more than 30 years.
The Carinthian poet has often described the catastrophes of his Catholic village childhood - the lack of speech, the penchant for bruising force and blunt sexuality, narrowness and joylessness.
The Büchner Prize winner is known primarily as a prose writer, theatre texts are rare in his work.
Collage from prose texts Gerhard Fresacher therefore puts together a collage of prose texts for his performance "Weather lights on the tongue tip", which can now be seen in Garage X at Petersplatz.
For example, the theatre maker combines elements from the autobiographical novel "Der Leibeigene" (1987) with pro-saminatures from "Leichnam, his family beluering" (2003).
On the largely vacant stage - important prop: a crumpled sofa, on which is coupulated and masturbated - the eight-member ensemble hangs through the text material.
Director Fresacher seems to trust the lyrics little.
The 70-minute performance whitewashes the template with an abundance of directing ideas, known from the repertoire of post-dramatic game forms.
Above all, the actresses arrive at the sometimes somewhat questionable scenic implementations.
They are touched hard, submerged with their heads under water, stapled to the wall with their evening robes.
Forced into cellophane or bodices, they stalk on dangerously high stalks through the staging, either monologizing them loudly or lying completely silent on the stage floor.
However, the text is hardly conveyed in this strained manner.
The best moments have the evening, when sung - the range ranges from Deep Purple to popular song material.
Only towards the end does the overaged performance come to rest, and Winkler's absurd humour flashes.
A black box in the car?
U.S. road planners are looking for a source of money to fix the decaying highway system, and believe they have found the solution in a small black box that finds space in the dashboard of every car.
The devices, which record every kilometre driven and report the information to the authorities, are the key point of a controversial attempt by Washington and the state's planning offices to revise the obsolete system for financing U.S. roads.
The usually rather dull area of road planning suddenly sparked an intense debate with colorful alliances.
Libertarians have allied themselves with environmental groups and advocate that the government use the small boxes to record the kilometers driven – and possibly also where they were driven – and can then use the information to calculate tax assessments.
The tea party is horrified.
The American Civil Rights Association (ACLU) is also deeply concerned and expresses a number of privacy concerns.
However, while one cannot agree on action in Congress, several states are no longer waiting.
They are currently examining how they can switch over the next ten years to a system in which drivers pay per mile driven.
Thousands of motorists have already tested the tachographers, some of whom are equipped with GPS surveillance.
This is really a must for our country.
“It’s nothing we’ll only potentially use,” said Hasan Ikhrata, managing director of the Southern California Assn. of Governments, which is planning a record of the miles driven with all California motorists in the state from 2025.
The way we pay those taxes will change.
The technology for that is there.
The initiative comes at a time, as the Highway Trust Fund, which is funded from the taxes that U.S. citizens pay at the dispenser, is broke.
But there isn’t as much refueling in America as it used to.
Cars consume less gasoline.
The state petroleum tax of 18.4 cents per gallon (less than 4 eurocent per litre) hasn't risen for 20 years.
Politicians, at high fuel prices, do not dare to raise the tax even by a penny.
“Gasoline tax is simply not fit for the future,” said Lee Munnich, an expert in traffic legislation at the University of Minnesota.
His state recently equipped 500 cars with tachographs to test a mile-based payment system.
“That’s the most long-term alternative,” he said.
Bureaucrats refer to it as a mile-based user fee.
It is not surprising that the idea is well received by urban liberals, because the tax could be used, for example, to influence driving behavior in such a way that congestion and climate-damaging exhaust gases are reduced.
The Californian planners rely on the system in the development of strategies aimed at achieving the state’s ambitious legally enshrined goals on climate change.
But Republican Bill Shuster of Pennsylvania, chairman of the House Transportation Committee, has also stated that he sees it as the most viable long-term alternative.
The free marketers of the Reason Foundation are also inspired by the idea of having drivers paid after the distance travelled.
“That’s not a tax that disappears into a black hole,” explains Adrian Moore, vice president of guidelines at Reason.
People pay directly for what they get.
The movement is also backed by two former U.S. transportation secretaries who, in a report in 2011, urged Congress to move toward mile-based reckoning.
The U.S. Senate last year approved a 90-million-dollar pilot project that would have included 10,000 cars.
Yet the majority in the House of Representatives prevented the push, responding to the concerns raised by deputies from rural areas representing people who often have to travel many miles on their way to work or into the city.
Nevertheless, several states and major cities are moving on their own in this direction.
Most engaged is Oregon, which currently recruits 5,000 drivers for the country's largest experiment.
These drivers will soon pay the mileage fees to the state instead of the petroleum tax.
Nevada has already completed a pilot project.
New York City is also considering one of these.
Illinois tests it to a limited extent with trucks.
And the I-95 coalition, which includes the transport ministries of 17 states on the East Coast (including Maryland, Pennsylvania, Virginia and Florida), is currently looking at how to introduce the change.
The concept isn't a universal hit.
In Nevada, where 50 volunteers were recently outfitted with the equipment, motorists were skeptical at the thought that the government could track any of its movements.
“Concerns about Big Brother and such things have been a big problem,” said Alauddin Khan, head of strategy and results management at the Nevada Department of Transportation.
People didn't want it.
As the test went on, Nevada’s ACLU warned on its website, “It would be relatively easy to turn tachographs into full-blown monitoring devices.”
There is no need to build a gigantic, bulky technological infrastructure that would inevitably be used to capture data about the daily movements of individuals.
Nevada is part of a number of states that are now looking out for affordable technology by which the state can capture the kilometers driven, but not exactly when and where.
With that, Khan says, the public would also be reassured.
The hunt for this technology has led some authorities to a small start-up company called True Mileage in California.
The company originally failed to help states with the taxation of motorists.
Rather, their goal was to gain a foothold in an up-and-coming market for car insurance, where drivers are supposed to pay based on the miles driven.
But the devices she tested are also interesting for the street planners, because they do not work with GPS and provide only limited information, which is regularly uploaded by modem.
“People are more willing to get involved if their speed and locations aren’t recorded,” said Ryan Morrison, managing director of True Mileage.
Big mistakes have been made in some of these public pilot programs.
There are considerably cheaper and less intrusive ways to implement this.
In Oregon, planners are experimenting with giving motorists a number of choices.
You can opt for a device with or without GPS.
Or they don’t choose any equipment at all and instead pay a flat fee based on the miles driven on average by all residents of the state.
Other bodies hope to be able to sell the concept of a suspicious public by equipping the devices with more features than with fewer.
In New York City, traffic officers want to develop a device for taxation, with which parking fees can also be paid, an insurance only has to be paid for kilometers driven and speed data from other vehicles are collected in real time, thanks to which motorists can dodge traffic jams.
“Motorists would be motivated to participate by the added value of the benefits offered by the system,” according to a city planning document.
Some traffic planners, however, are wondering whether all the talk about paying per mile isn't just a huge distraction.
At the Metropolitan Transportation Commission for the area of San Francisco Bay, officials declared that Congress could solve the problem of the bankrupt Highway Trust Fund simply by increasing the fuel tax.
An additional one-time fee or annual levy could be imposed on motorists with hybrid vehicles or other vehicles that consume little gasoline, so that they also pay a fair share.
"There is no reason for an operation, even if an aspirin is sufficient," explains Randy Rentschler, head of legislation and public affairs at the commission.
When we do, hundreds of millions of motorists worry about their privacy and numerous other things.
Königsfeld: Small team beats well
The volunteer fire brigade handled its autumn main rehearsal with flying colors despite staff shortages.
Only eleven men took part in the exercise.
Department commander Hans Kammerer had chosen the Feder estate in Burgberger Straße in his exercise acceptance.
A short circuit is said to have occurred there during sawing.
Due to the resulting fire and the smoke development, two people were injured, but one of them was still able to send an emergency call.
Two respirators of the weir got ready to enter the building after the first examination of the situation by Hans Kammerer.
After a short time, it was possible to find the first person and escort him outdoors.
The second person had to be worn.
This was not so easy, because there was also a narrow staircase to overcome.
The building, a workshop with an integrated stable for two horses, was not easy to secure.
It stored a lot of wood and also straw bales in it.
There were also electric machines for woodworking.
The first firefighting attack took place via the tank in the vehicle.
A further line took place via an overfloor hydrant at a distance of about 100 meters.
Now it was possible to design three extinguishing attacks.
There was also a hollow jet tube used in the building.
Hans Kammerer was also interested in pointing out what can be achieved with little available personnel.
In case of emergency, support is provided by the day-to-day action group Königsfeld.
The commander was satisfied with the course of the exercise.
Networking of universities and companies important
On his visit to the Breisgau-Hochschwarzwald district, state parliament president Guido Wolf was also educated in Titisee-Neustadt about where tomorrow’s workers come from.
Together with the managing principals of the vocational schools as well as the Kreisgymnasium and the parent advisory board chairmen, he investigated this important question at the Hochschwarzwald support centre.
"Education is an important location factor," emphasized Claudia Stehle, director of the Hans-Thoma-Schule, which presented the networked school project Bildungszentrum Hochschwarzwald.
Wolf was impressed by the model projects for training.
Since 2011, there has been a successful cooperation between Förderzentrum, Kreisgymnasium and vocational school centre in the forest city.
In addition, he praised the family-friendliness in the county.
"It is important not only to keep an eye on the family phase, but also due to demographic change, the phase of caring for the care of relatives," Wolf explained.
In addition, he cited that more and more employees took care of the care and care of their relatives.
"Without the support of employers, the resulting burden on employees can lead to a termination of the employment relationship," Wolf said.
But no one can afford to lose skilled workers, he said.
Another particularly important factor is the networking of universities and companies.
"Because those who have already learned about the advantages of the region during their studies are often preserved with their acquired knowledge and skills to the regional economy, which contributes to the strengthening of the region," the state parliament president was convinced.
Only if sufficient training places are offered, the need for skilled workers can also be met.
In his opinion, the location advantage of the district is its advantageous location in the southwest of Germany, in the immediate vicinity of France and Switzerland.
In addition, on his train journey from Titisee-Neustadt to Freiburg, he experienced the efforts already made for the expansion of local public transport.
"Remain stubborn and ensure that public transport gets better," he explained to the address of the local politicians present.
Experienced an entertaining afternoon with music
For 15 years, the Kaul family has been inviting the Dietingen seniors for coffee, cakes and thereafter for a vesper.
In the past, the older citizens were entertained in the rest area.
In the meantime, this afternoon will take place in the nursing home St. Josef
The nursing home residents looked forward to the delicious cakes and pies.
Later there was sausage salad.
Silvia Kimmich-Bantle and her father Karl Kimmich entertained with common melodies.
The more than 100-year-old natural stone wall as the front demarcation of the old schoolyard on Kirchstraße, which is located before the renovation, is to be relegated again to the renovation cost plan of the city of Bräunlingen.
In the days leading up to Kilbig, a small angular wall was installed during the first work of the renovation of the anterior schoolyard, to stabilize and static relief of the more than one-century-year-old natural stone wall with balustrade.
According to Alexander Misok from the Bräunlinger Stadtbauamt, the further procedure envisages including the Landesdenkmalamt in the plans and to create a renovation concept for the old wall.
Thereafter, a cost calculation is to be carried out by experts, which will then be placed on the table for the local council to decide and determine the further procedure.
A decision on this is expected to be made as early as next year.
After the renovation, paving and tree planting of the old school courtyard within the two side wings of the school built in 1912, it is necessary in the foreseeable time as a follow-up measure to renovate the boundary wall in need of renovation from scratch and placed on a new substructure.
The historic one-hundred-year-old demarcation wall of the old schoolyard to Kirchstraße, is mainly due to water and frost damage, since there is no drainage, in need of remediation and has a tendency forward.
Deep cracks in individual stones testify to weather damage, but the wall is currently not at risk of collapse.
A stable wall is a prerequisite for one schoolyard used by pupils, which has been done through the current fortification work.
Originally, the schoolyard renovation was even planned in 2008/2009, but high unplanned expenses brought a postponement.
An important word in the historical renovation of school walls, which has even already been used for film recordings, will be voiced by the state monument office.
If requirements come from this side, then the city of Bräunlingen hopes for grants from the preservationists.
The pupils of Bräunlinger Elementary School, which are now being taught again in a well-renovated old school building with a new auditorium, will soon be able to use the old schoolyard.
Özdemir wants to receive jazz education in Stuttgart
Cem Özdemir, Federal Chairman of the Greens, has now also intervened in the ongoing discussions about the future of the music academies in Baden-Württemberg.
"I think it's wrong to give up holistic education at the Musikhochschule Stuttgart," Özdemir told the "Stuttgarter Nachrichten."
Jazz and classical music are part of the jazz location Stuttgart.
Özdemir, who is considered a favorite for a direct mandate in the federal election on 22 September in Stuttgart, contradicts the Green-Red state government.
On the basis of an expert study, this pushes for a reorganization of the five music academies in Baden-Württemberg.
Afterwards, among other things, the degree program jazz and pop is to be relocated from the Musikhochschule Stuttgart to the Musikhochschule Mannheim.
U.S. border: 'Super-tunnel' discovered for drug smuggling
On the Mexico-U.S. border, investigators have spotted a "super-tunnel" on drug smuggling and decommissioned.
According to the U.S. customs authorities, the more than 500-meter-long tube between Tijuana and San Diego was equipped with electricity, railway tracks and a ventilation system.
Three suspects were arrested, eight tons of marijuana and nearly 150 kilograms of cocaine seized.
The tunnel had not yet been put into use.
"These cartels are stupid if they think they could die under the radar," U.S District Attorney General Laura Duffy said at a news conference outside a warehouse in San Diego, where an end of the tunnel had been discovered.
Addressing the drug gangs, Duffy said, “If you continue to build such tunnels and try to use them, we’re determined to turn that into a huge waste of your dirty money.”
Both U.S. authorities and Mexican security forces are in a permanent fight against the drug cartels.
Since 2006, more than 77, 000 people have been killed in Mexico in connection with drug crime.
Designed by experts
Due to the elaborate equipment of the tunnel, the investigators assume that it was constructed by architects and engineers and that the construction took about a year.
The track system was equipped in such a way that electrically operated wagons could be used there.
On the Mexican side there is access in a building that is 80 meters from the border.
According to the authorities, a ladder leads 20 meters into the depths to the actual tunnel entrance.
The tunnel has a cross-section of 1.20 meters height and 90 centimeters in width.
It would also have been suitable to promote illegal immigration towards the USA.
Two of the suspects have been arrested in connection with the cocaine find.
The third, a Mexican, was arrested for the seized marijuana.
All three face life imprison deprivation as a maximum sentence, officials said.
Eight such tunnels had been discovered since 2006, according to the press conference in San Diego.
However, it is the first time that cocaine has been found in such a tunnel.
Cocaine is usually smuggled in smaller quantities and not through tunnels.
This shows the "desperation" of the drug gangs, whose traditional routes are now cut off, said Bill Sherman from the DEA drug manhunt in San Diego.
They'd do anything to get to the U.S
No details of those arrested were disclosed, at least one was Mexican, it said.
You have to expect prison sentences of up to ten years.
2014 becomes Geisinger Festjahr
The jubilee of the city of Geisingen on the occasion of 1250.
Recurrence of the first documentary mention draws ever closer.
If the year comes to an end in a good eight weeks, the anniversary year will take place.
Geisingen and Kirchen-Hausen were first mentioned together in 794.
In churches, a certificate has been produced in which both places are mentioned.
On March 15 there will be an official kick-off to the anniversary year, and since Geisingen currently has no hall, this kick-off event takes place in the place, where 1250 years ago both were first mentioned, in Kirchen-Hausen.
As part of the jubilee, a number of events are planned both in Geisingen, as well as in Kirchen-Hausen.
Kirchen-Hausen becomes the 1250 in July on a festive weekend from 18 to 20 July.
Celebrate your return.
On Friday with a ceremony, on Saturday and Sunday with a party around the Kirchtalhalle.
On this festive weekend there is also the Kirchen-Hausen church festival.
On the occasion of the jubilee, city music director Rudolf Barth has written a commissioned composition for the city, which is performed on three dates.
On 21 June together with the solstice celebration of the Black Forest Association, on 12 July in the arena with fireworks and on 13 December as the first concert of city music in the neue Festhalle.
This will be completed and inaugurated in the jubilee year, the official date is on the last weekend of September, 27 and 28.
On the occasion of the Geising city anniversary, a new chronicle will also be published.
"But it is supposed to be a chronicle not only of Geisingen itself, but a printed work about today's Geisingen, which consists of Geisingen, Kirchen-Hausen, Leipferdingen, Aulfingen and Gutmadingen," says mayor Walter Hengstler.
And also the Geisingen of today celebrates its birthday in 2014, in 1974 today's Raumschaft was carried out with the incorporations.
The new chronicle is to be unveiled on 21 or 22 November in the new Festhalle in Geisingen.
However, 2014 is another year with many anniversaries.
The guild Strohglonki begins on February 8 with a custom evening on the occasion of its 60th anniversary, Harmonie Gutmadingen turns 90 and has the festive banquet on March 29 and then celebrates the district music festival from May 1 to 4.
The Altenwerk Leipferdingen is 40 years old, the Geisinger School has been at the new location for 50 years and celebrates this on May 10, the Musikverein Polyhymnia Leipferdingen turns 150 years old and celebrates this as part of the fountain festivation from 4 to 7 July.
The town of Geisingen was first mentioned in a donation certificate, which is owned by the monastery of St. Gallen, in 764.
The certificate was made in churches (Hausen), which at that time was a court place.
Both municipalities are therefore mentioned in the same document.
Reason to celebrate this 1250-year-old in 2014 accordingly, in addition, the municipal reform dates back 40 years ago.
Council targets track facilities
Should the railway crossing "Am Hirschen" be extensively rebuilt in order to increase road safety?
Yes, the local councils decided.
Schiltach has to take 220 000 euros in hand for this.
Deutsche Bahn intends to improve the Kinzigtal railway line next year.
In particular, the safety at level crossings is to be increased - there are regular collisions of trains with other vehicles nationwide.
If the municipalities agree to the reconstruction measures, they have to participate in the costs with a third, the rest are shared by the railway and the federal government.
Schiltach's city master builder Roland Grießhaber explained to the local council the peculiarities of the "Am Hirschen" railway crossing.
There, the city plans to build a bulge at the height of the tunnel mouth west of the railway tracks.
There, even larger vehicles, for example those transporting longwood, could stand in oncoming traffic without having to block the tracks.
Grießhaber therefore proposed to construct a required retaining wall in such a way that the city could widen the ensuing road in due course in order to ensure a smooth flow of traffic.
This was debated controversially in the local council, as some councillors questioned the need for road proliferation: Thomas Kipp summed up the mood of the critics with his statement: "Do we have to take so much money into our hands because of so few vehicles?"
Mayor Thomas Haas disagreed: The railway crossing "Hirschen" is regularly used for long-timbered transports.
Even wood from the "Kuhbach" area is partly transported over this route, because the vehicles up to 20 meters long could not use the route at the Häberles Bridge, because they could not turn into the main road there.
In addition, the route serves as a diversion for those vehicles that are not allowed to use the tunnel when the main road is closed, Haas argued.
Since it can be foreseeabled that Kirchberg and Schlossberg tunnels would have to be rebuilt and renovated, as is currently the case in Wolfach, an important traffic meaning could also be given to the railway crossing "Am "Hirschen" for a longer period of time.
The councillors agreed to investigate the costs of the planned widening of the road.
Notwithstanding this, the majority agreed to the conversion of the level crossing with the proviso that the required retaining wall will be built in such a way that the city can extend the subsequent street in the direction of "Bahnhofstraße" if necessary for encounter traffic to around 5.5 meters.
The conversion of the level crossing at the Trautwein tannery, on the other hand, could lead to a completely different problem, which concerns the access to the sharply ascending "Geroltzhäuser Weg".
The administration had the conversion plans of the railway examined by the engineering office Breinlinger, because it had doubts about whether the planned connection of the Geroltzhäuser Weg was then immediately possible.
The investigation came to the conclusion that it could not be ruled out that the inclination conditions of the road could cause longer vehicles to rest.
In turn, the city bears the responsibility for this as a road construction load carrier.
The conversion could therefore lead to liability disputes.
Since both the removal of wood and the turning in the current state of expansion functioned, one should not agree to the conversion of the level crossing, Haas argued.
At this point, too, the risk of a collision of the train with a vehicle standing on the transition is significantly lower than at the other level crossings, because there is directly next to the central stop, to which there is a good visual connection.
Even trains from the central station would have to be slowed down at this point to such an extent that there was no risk of a collision.
The majority of the reconstruction of this level crossing was denied approval, since the city of Schiltach would have to "bathe out" the problem with sitting-on vehicles, according to the councillors, since the railway could withdraw from responsibility after completion of the construction work.
The panel considers the conversions at the "Vor Heubach" and "Vor Kuhbach" railway crossings to be unproblematic, for which crossing agreements have already been made with the railway.
Esa Decides on Large Space Missions: Missions for Billions
Distant planets, gravitational waves or black holes - experts from the European Space Agency must now agree on two large-scale projects they want to launch in the next few years.
There were 30 proposals to choose from, five are still in the running.
Farning worlds.
Astronomers have already found more than 1000 planets in other stars.
Whether there is life, at least on some of them, nobody knows.
It’s hard to choose: Are you supposed to look for gravitational waves?
Or rather explore the origin of the cosmos?
Or search for Earth-like planets and thus perhaps discover extraterrestrial life?
These questions are just asked by those in charge of the European Space Agency Esa.
For their "Cosmic Vision" program, they are looking for ideas for two major space projects, which are intended to decisively advance research.
The budget for such "L-missions" is about one billion euros each.
The launch is expected to be 2028 and 2034.
At the beginning of September, the elite of European space exploration met in Paris to discuss proposals for such missions.
30 ideas were up for debate, five of whom have now been shortlisted.
Numerous committees are reviewing the proposals and catching up with the researchers.
In the next few days, a final decision is to be made by the scientific program committee.
A total of four major questions have been selected by Esa, to which the missions of the "Cosmic Vision" programme, which was initiated in 2007, will find answers.
What are the conditions for the formation of planets and life?
The exact processes in the formation of stars from large gas clouds - and thus also the formation of planets orbiting these stars - are still in the proverbial darkness.
A large infrared telescope in space could permeate this darkness.
And if a star possesses planets, under what circumstances can life arise there?
In order to investigate this question, the "Cheops" space telescope is to be launched in 2017, with which 500 already known planetary systems will be examined in more detail in our vicinity.
But the possibilities are limited, Cheops is a small mission with a budget of 150 million euros.
Using larger instruments, astronomers could map Earth-like planets and even analyze the composition of their atmospheres, thus looking for "biomarkers": gases that are seen as an indication of biological activity.
How Does the Solar System Work?
This question, too, should ultimately provide information about the prerequisites there are for the emergence of life.
An important role is played by the magnetic activity of a star and the interaction of its magnetic field as well as the particle radiation emanating from it with the planetary system.
In our solar system, these processes can be explored as an example.
With the "Solar Orbiter", Esa has already approved a mission of the M-Class (where the budget is about half a billion euros) for this question.
The probe is scheduled to launch in 2017 and closely monitor the sun's surface and activity.
Jupiter and its moons are also of great interest to the researchers.
They want to find out what role the giant planet has played in the development of the solar system.
Esa envisages the first of the three L missions of the "Cosmic Vision" programme.
"Juice" (Jupiter Icy Moons Explorer) is expected to begin its 2022 journey.
Eight years later, it reaches Jupiter and is expected to study the planet's atmosphere as well as the icy moons Europa, Kallisto and Ganymed.
What are the fundamental physical laws of the universe?
The universe offers many opportunities to study matter under extreme conditions and thus to investigate whether the laws of nature known to us are still valid there.
Perhaps there are also deviations that could show the physicists the way to a new theory, under the umbrella of which all known natural laws can be combined.
How did the universe emerge and what does it consist of?
13.7 billion years ago, our cosmos originated through the Big Bang.
In addition to the common matter, from which stars, planets and living beings such as us consist, there is dark matter, the gravity of which galaxies and galaxy clusters stick together, and dark energy, through whose action the expansion of the universe accelerates.
So far, the researchers do not know which physical laws were valid in the first fractions of the Big Bang, nor what dark matter and dark energy consist of.
With "Euclid" the Esa has already approved an M-mission here, the launch of which is planned for 2020.
With a special telescope, the probe is to investigate the distribution of matter in the universe for the first time in the entire sky and thus enable conclusions about the nature of dark matter and dark energy.
Who could do the race
Of the 30 proposals, only two will remain at the end of the selection process.
According to many experts, the best chance of obtaining a permit as an L-mission has "E-Lisa", a sophisticated concept for detecting gravitational waves.
By this, physicists mean changes in the structure of space-time that Albert Einstein predicted almost 100 years ago.
They have not been proven until today.
Elisa could do that, the scientists behind the projection hopes.
The mission envisages a detector consisting of two or three probes, which is stationed in space.
In contrast to installations on Earth, it could also detect gravitational waves, which arose during the Big Bang, and thus provide new insights into the formation of the cosmos.
Four other proposals are competing for the second L mission: a large X-ray telescope called "Athena", which is intended to investigate black holes, "Icy Planets", another mission to the outer planets of the solar system, "Prism", a mission to measure the cosmic background radiation, and the "Exoplanet Finder", which is supposed to detect and investigate Earth-like planets.
Proposals for sample retransmission from Mars, the exploration of moon, Venus and asteroids as well as solar physics have already been eliminated.
Snowden ready to “cooperate” with Germany in relation to US surveillance
Edward Snowden, the U.S. secret service whistleblower, has said he's ready to travel to Berlin and testify in front of the German Bundestag if the American National Security Agency and its director Keith Alexander don't provide answers about their activities.
German MP Hans-Christian Ströbele met Snowden on Thursday in Russia, where he has been granted asylum to discuss the statement in Germany with him.
In a letter from Snowden presented to the media in Berlin by Ströbele on Friday, he writes: “Even if the result of my efforts was demonstrably positive, my government continues to treat a dissenting opinion as a breach of allegiance and tries to criminalize political expression with a threat of punishment in which no defense is possible.”
But telling the truth isn’t a crime.
In the letter, Snowden writes that he is convinced that the support of the international community can convince the American government to drop the charges against him.
The indictation filed by the U.S. Department of Justice includes espionage and theft of state property.
German Interior Minister Hans-Peter Friedrich told Zeit Online: “If Mr Snowden is willing to talk to German representatives, we will find ways to make this possible.”
U.S.-Germany relations are strained by claims that the NSA has wiretapped Chancellor Angela Merkel's mobile phone.
Thomas Oppermann, the deputy who chairs the parliamentary committee responsible for the intelligence service, said it should seize the opportunity to listen to Snowden as a witness if this was possible “without endangering him and completely ruining relations with the US.”
Ströbele, a Green MP, posted a picture of himself and Snowden in his Twitter feed.
He was accompanied by two German journalists during his visit to Russia.
Ströbele explained that according to the lawyer of former NSA employee Snowden could not return to Russia if he leaved the country.
If Snowden testifies in Germany, he needs an assurance that he is “safe” there, according to the deputy.
Snowden explains in his letter that he would face a “serious and ongoing” persecution campaign that drove him out of his homeland.
However, he was touched by the worldwide reaction to “my act of political expression.”
Citizens around the world, as well as high-ranking politicians – including in the United States – have called the revelations about an uncontrolled system of comprehensive surveillance a merit to the general public.
In his letter, Snowden gives the German authorities an offer of cooperation “when the difficulties around the humanitarian situation have been resolved.”
Frontier Airlines levies charges for carry-on baggage
Frontier Airlines plans to charge up to US$100 from passengers who want to stoward carry-on baggage on their flight.
Frontier Airlines intends to charge up to US$100 for a piece of hand luggage and 2 dollars for coffee or lemonade, even if it could be readed in Wednesday's announcement that passengers are likely to keep the whole can.
The new fee applies to carry-on luggage in the luggage compartment above the seat, while small bags under the seat are still free
Frontier said it would charge US$25 if the fee was paid in advance and US$100 for travelers who only paid at the gate.
Frontier spokeswoman Kate O'Malley said the US$100 fee was aimed at moving travelers to take care of the payment in advance.
"We don't want to calculate that sum," she said.
Airlines began claiming fees for the first and second checked baggage in 2008.
Passengers who tried to circumvent them have therefore stuffed as much as possible into their hand luggage, which has the consequence that the luggage compartments above the seat are often overcrowded.
Fees are one way to persuade passengers to take less on board.
O'Malley said the new fee wasn't really about making money.
It is about the most loyal customers of Frontier, who have clearly expressed that it is becoming increasingly difficult to find space in the compartment above the seat.
Passengers who purchase your tickets on the airline’s website do not have to pay.
This means that a passenger may be allowed to take a piece of luggage free of charge at Frontier’s gate, but the next person may have to pay 100 dollars for a comparable piece of luggage.
O'Malley explained Frontier's website and check-in procedures would be changed so that passengers were aware of the fee before coming to the gate.
Frontier's new carry-of-wair fee doesn't come into effect until the summer, there's no exact date yet.
Passengers often scold about baggage surcharges and other fees, but airlines are happy to resort to it.
They argue that the transport of the luggage costs money and passengers should pay for this service if they wanted it.
Many on Wall Street consider the new bailout fees as a sign that the airlines are taking enough money to cover the cost of air travel after years of losses.
So far, however, most of them keep their fingers off charges for carry-on baggage.
Three years ago, Spirit Airlines Inc. was the first to apply for carry-on baggage, followed by Allegiant Air, also a low-cost airline.
The only other airline with such a fee is the Hungarian Wizz Air, explained airline consultant Jay Sorensen, who is closely following the additional fees.
He estimated in a December 2011 reportation that Spirit's carry-on fee brings in US$50 million annually.
Sorensen, who used to hold a leadership position at Midwest Airlines, has recently flown with Spirit and was curious about what to expect at the gate if passengers were confronted with the company's unusual carry-on fee.
“The boarding process was one of the smoothest I’ve witnessed in my career in aviation,” he said.
I was expecting to see gnashing my teeth and palpable discussions at the gate.
“The plane was full,” he explained, “and the boarding ran like lubricated.”
Frontier also succeeds Spirit at the 2 dollars required for coffee, tea, lemonade or juice.
The company explained that passengers who bought lemonade or sape should be allowed to keep the whole can and refill coffee for free.
Water is still free of charge.
U.S. Airways had briefly tried to demand money for soft drinks in 2008, but reversed it seven months later after passengers complained and no other big company had followed suit.
Frontier's move to levy fees for carry-on baggage if passengers don't purchase directly from the airline is the latest initiative designed to steer customers to their own website.
Airlines pay online travel agencies such as Orbitz 10 to US$25 per ticket sold.
This gives all companies an incentive to move passengers to buy directly from them instead of via an online travel agency.
Frontier, however, has gone furthest in this area.
In September, the company began issuing only half as many frequent flyer miles when booking via an online travel agency.
On Wednesday, miles were slashed once again to 25 percent of the flight route.
An air trip booked by a third-party provider with Frontier over 1000 miles therefore only earns customers 250 miles.
Passengers can also reserve seats exclusively for direct purchases on Frontier's website.
Frontier has a loyal customer base at its headquarters in Denver, but business is shrinking and the company is losing money.
Proceeds declined by 9 per cent and flight capacity shranked nearly 13 per cent in the first quarter, as can be read in the financial report released on Wednesday by the parent company, Republic Airways Holdings Inc.
Republic has tried to get a grip on Frontier's finances as part of the company's sale.
Proverbial words come out of the
A good 40 women attended this year's last women's breakfast in the evangelical parish of Bisingen.
Theme of the day in the parish hall were "Proverbs from the Bible" and "idioms from the Middle Ages".
Zita Köhler, the chairwoman of the parish council, addressed the biblical proverbs after breakfast in a lecture.
"Proverbs contain life wisdom, regulations or warnings," she explained.
She compared several proverbs with the corresponding Bible passages and explained the meaning.
She cited proverbs such as: Holzauge be vigilant, as scales fall from the eyes, keep an eye on someone, the Lord gives his own in his sleep, washing his hands in innocence.
Several times, the speaker also let her listeners guess what the proverb was.
The idioms from the Middle Ages were accepted by the parish councillor Christel Dehner: All good things are three, make blue, a bit.
She declared the importance and origin of the idioms she called "bridges into the past."
Finally, a raffle was raffled.
The prizes were covered on a table and humorously circumscribed before being handed over to the winners.
Among the visitors of the breakfast round was mayoral candidate Roman Waizenegger.
Learning instead of unemployment: Piolit project for unskilled people in tourism
Today, a pilot project for the tourism industry is starting on the Mecklenburg Lake District.
Under the leadership of the Federal Employment Agency, 49 women and men, who already work as unskilled workers in the industry, are to be qualifiered as specialists for hotels and restaurants over three winters.
"What's new is that the six-month courses end with degrees," said Carmen Wiechert of the Neubrandenburg employment agency.
The benefits are diverse: the participants would not be unemployed, the companies would get trained specialists, which were increasingly lacking in hotels and restaurants, and the agency did not have to finance unemployment.
Also involved in this project are the Chamber of Industry and Commerce in Neubrandenburg as well as the German Hotel and Restaurant Association (Dehoga) Mecklenburg-Vorpommern.
NSA declares an “internal error” and not hackers are to blame for the site’s crash
The shady National Security Agency declared late Friday that an error had paralyzed its public website for a few hours rather than hackers, as has been claimed online.
“NSA.gov was unreachable for a few hours tonight because an internal error occurred during a planned update,” the spying agency explained in an e-mail.
The problem will be fixed this evening.
The claim that the failure was caused by a Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attack was not true.
In the early evening hours, online server trackers noticed that the NSA website was unreachable for at least six hours and this is still not for some users.
Earlier, a spokesperson for the NSA told ABC News that the agency’s sensitive internal network was “in no way” compromised.
Secret information is not in danger, according to the spokesman.
At least one group of hacktivists claimed to have paralyzed the NSA website with a DDoS attack.
In such attacks, the target website is flooded with page views until the servers are overloaded and the website collapses.
Cybertactics are relatively simple, and the attacks do not serve to break into the target system’s internal network.
The formerly super-secret NSA, whose nickname was once No Such Agency, now finds itself in the bright light of the public and faces sharp criticism after the revelations about its extensive surveillance program at home and abroad – a result of the secret NSA data stolen and published by the disillusioned former NSA employee Edward Snowden.
The growing controversy surrounding the secret service fueled initial speculation that the incident today was the result of targeted cyper surgery.
Slightly the number of people without a job fell by 22 to 1,307 in October.
Although the rate is 3.1 percent better than last year and also better than in September, "we would have hoped for more," says Monika Felder-Bauer, deputy office manager of the employment agency in Sonthofen.
Because many companies have so far reacted subduedly in attitudes.
One reason for this, according to Felder-Bauer: "We hardly have any specialists in crafts, healthcare and elderly care".
Due to the season, job offers for staff in the hotel and restaurant industry have also been emerging since September.
In mid-December, the winter season begins there.
The whole background report can be found in the Allgäu display sheet from 31.10.2013 (page 33).
Loss of profits at Bombardier after decline in delivery figures and orders
Canadian aircraft and railroad maker Bombardier Inc reported a 15 percent drop in net gain on Thursday after coming under pressure from declining orders and shipments on aircraft in the third quarter, as well as contract problems in the rail division.
Bombardier, based in Montreal, also did not release flight testing data for its all-new CSeries aircraft and did not provide any new information on whether the aircraft could go into commercial use according to its ambitious schedule next September.
The maidening flight of the test aircraft one and a half months ago was followed by only three more take-offs so far, which makes it questionable whether the test phase proceeds as planned.
The results fell short of forecasts, leading to a price plunge of over eight percent on the Toronto stock market.
Cameron Doerksen, an analyst at National Bank Financial, lowered his valuation from “outperform” to “sector perform” on Thursday in sight that the stock possesses limited price potential over the next one to two quarters.
“While weaker delivery figures for aircraft were largely expected, we are clearly disappointed by the margin development in the transport sector,” Doerksen said in a customer letter.
We believe Bombardier will receive new orders for the CSeries as the test programme for the aircraft advance.
However, if no new orders are announced in the coming months, we expect the market to become more skeptical of the program.
Bombardier hopes the CSeries aircraft series can catapult the company into the lower market segment currently dominated by Boeing and Airbus.
The first test plane was unveiled to the public in March and flew for the first time in September after months of delays.
But so far binding orders for the CSeries are moderate at 177, as potential buyers are waiting for the results of flight tests and would like to see if the company’s claims regarding fuel efficiency and cost-saving potential of the new jet aircraft are accurate.
There are currently a total of 403 orders and commitments from 15 customers and companies.
CEO Pierre Beaudoin is confident that Bombardier will reach its target of 300 binding orders at the start of commercial use of the jet.
The company's management also assured analysts and the media on Thursday that the program was on schedule.
"The test plane hasn't stayed on the ground any longer than expected," Beaudoin said during a conference call, adding that ground tests and software updates had been scheduled during the plane's service life.
Every manufacturer plans differently.
We've decided to make an initial flight and then attach an overhaul period, which we've done.
This is done throughout the flight program.
The second of five test aircraft is expected to launch in the next few weeks, the rest shortly thereafter, it said from the company side.
Nevertheless, analysts remain skeptical about whether twelve months after the maiden flight the first customer can commission a CSeries aircraft.
Bombardier stated that it is reviewing planning for commissioning (EIS) and will update it in the next few months.
“The slow progress of the flight test – even if they obviously correspond to Bombardier’s internal planning – reinforces our view that the commissioning shifts to the first quarter of 2015,” Doerksen said.
In the third quarter at the end of September 30, Bombardier's net profit fell to 147 million dollars, or 8 cents per share, from 172 million dollars or 9 cents per share the year earlier.
Adjusted earnings per share are unchanged at 9 cents.
Sales declined slightly, falling from 4.2 to 4.1 billion dollars.
Analysts had expected a profit of 10 cents per share and revenue of 4.56 billion dollars, according to Thomson Reuters I/B/E/S.
The world's fourth-largest aircraft maker says it has delivered 45 aircraft this quarter, down 57 last year.
Net orders fell from 83 aircraft to 26.
The order backlog in the aerospace division amounted to 32.9 billion dollars as of September 30, unchanged from December 31.
“In the aerospace sector, the results were within our specifications, but the low order intake and overall market conditions were disappointing,” said Beaudoin.
Sales in the aerospace sector fell 13 percent to two billion dollars.
Bombardier, the world’s largest railway manufacturer, announced that sales in this division have risen by almost eleven percent to 2.1 billion dollars.
The order backlog in the transport division amounted to 32.6 billion dollars on September 30 and was thus slightly increased compared to December 31.
The profit margin in the field of transport has been affected by settlement problems with some major contracts.
According to management, there will be new guidelines for the fourth quarter.
In afternoon trading on Thursday, Bombardier's share yielded 8.5 percent and stood at 4.83 Canadian dollars, after it was also announced that Google Chief Financial Officer Patrick Pichette will join the board.
Brazilian Embraer SA, the world's third-largest commercial aircraft maker and Bombardier's closest rival, reported a ten-percent drop in quarter-term profit on Thursday.
U.S.: Shots fired at Los Angeles airport
An unidentified man fired shots at LAX airport on Friday morning (local time).
While the airport line said on Twitter that the shooter was in custody, the Los Angeles Times reported that the man was dead.
A security guard was also killed.
Mediaries also reported several injured people.
U.S. President Barack Obama is constantly being briefed on the state, White House spokesman Jay Carney said.
The incident took place in Terminal 3. Eyewitnesses claimed to have seen a gunman with a rifle in one of the departure halls, several media outlets reported.
One traveler told broadcaster CNN that many people were looking for protection in panic.
Travelers would have screamed and crying children.
Panic broke out among those waiting, many had hidden behind chairs out of fear.
He had heard two gunshots, a traveler told the Los Angeles Times.
An employee of the security agency TSA suffered a gunshot wound to the foot.
The scene was "like being in a movie."
After that, he himself was taken to a security area, the man reported.
The shots are said to have fallen close to a security control area.
Airport building evacuated
Television pictures showed numerous ambulances and police cars in front of a terminal.
Hundreds of people were evacuated from the building to safety.
Users on Twitter posted photos of a special operations squad and a rifle lying on the ground.
According to the airport management via Twitter, the incident began at 9.30 a.m. (local time).
The access roads were closed, causing long backlogs, according to CNN.
With the exception of landings, air traffic had been temporarily suspended, it said.
However, there were also several fliers on CNN at the launch.
Terminal 3 is mainly served by smaller U.S. airlines.
Los Angeles LAX Airport is one of the largest airports in the United States.
Cocaine-addicted lawyer, who warned drug lord against police investigation, must go to jail
Basharat Ditta, 42, passed on information to underword size Neil Scarborough
The lawyer was afraid that his secret drug addiction could come to light
He was sentenced to three years in prison by Liverpool Crown Court
A well-known criminal defense lawyer who warned a drug lord of a major police investigation because he was afraid his secret drug addiction could be uncovered was sentenced to three years in prison.
Basharat Ditta, 42, provided underworth size Neil Scarborough with sensitive information on investigations into his activities in drug trafficking after being compromised by his own cocaine addiction.
The lawyer nicknamed “Bash”, who was praised by criminalists as “first-class”, was arrested at his home in 2011 after police monitored Scarborough, whom he had represented in an earlier drug trial.
Officers watched Scarboorg (32) deliver three bags of cocaine at the lawyer's house in Blackburn, Lancashire, while the latter was with colleagues at a board of directors.
The investigation revealed that Ditta regularly used Class A drugs after traces of cocaine had been found in his hair, on his wallet and on credit cards during tests.
Over an eight-month period between January and August 2011, he attempted unauthorised information about the arrest of two men on behalf of Scarborough and one of his partners.
All four suspects at the time were monitored by police as part of a major investigative trial on the heroin and cocaine trade in Lancashire, Cumbria, Merseyside, Berkshire and West Yorkshire.
Together with 32 other people, they were later arrested after police secured heroin and cocaine worth 1.5 million pounds as well as 200,000 pounds of cash during a series of house searches.
Ditta, 42, passed on information to the criminals because he feared his drug addiction could become public.
Today Ditta, who works for Forbes Solicitors in Blackburn, was found guilty and convicted at Liverpool's Crown Court following a three-week trial on two counts of obstruction of justice and convicted.
He admitted cocaine possession during an earlier interrogation.
The lawyer was convicted after the police found out in their investigation against Scarborough that he had been in regular telephone contact with Ditta in February 2011.
Two investigation officers followed the suspect and seen him going to Ditta's house and put the drugs there with a purity of 60 per cent in a black golf glove under the lawyer's garbage can.
Shortly after delivering the drugs, Scarborough regularly telephoned Ditta, who found himself at dinner at Blackburn Rovers football stadium in Ewood Park.
The lawyer returned home, retrieved the drugs and then nine communications were exchanged between the two.
It was revealed in court that Ditta was a “regular consumer” of cocaine after tests had been discovered in Class A drugs in his hair, his wallet and on his credit cards.
Ditta was later arrested, but denied taking cocaine and said he had spoken to the alleged dealer because he was his client, claiming that her discussion was subject to “secret of lawyers.”
During his arrest, Ditta took his wallet and tried to remove a number of credit cards, but they were secured and a hair sample was taken from him.
During the police interrogation, he stated that he was running an office both at his home address and at the workplace and clients would call him at home for legal matters.
In court, it was revealed that he had called important actors in drug trafficking, some of whom he had previously represented, after significant arrests and informed about what investigators knew about them.
Prosecutor Anne Whyte said, “A criminal lawyer should know better than everyone else that you don’t break the law.”
Ditta is accused of abusing his position as a criminal defense lawyer, as he has engaged too much with certain clients.
The relationship in question is not simply that of a drug trafficker, but of a drug trafficker supplying his lawyer with drugs.
Part of his communications was undoubtedly legitimate, because he was the advocate of these individuals.
But this went far beyond the limits of a normal relationship between lawyer and client.
He countered the police investigation as far as possible, so that they could continue to investigate their criminal activities.
Mr. Ditta brought his profession into disrepute.
He was too close to certain clients, particularly Scarborough, and he allowed his independence to be compromised.
Ditta dismissed any misconduct, claiming, “If I was a corrupt lawyer, what I’m not, and wanted to pass information to Scarborough, then I wouldn’t wait 15 hours, but do that right away.”
But after the hearing, Superintendent Lee Halstead of police in Lancashire said, “Mr Ditta has taken the step from a criminal lawyer to a criminal the moment he started acquiring drugs from organized criminals.”
His cocaine addiction resulted in hopeless compromisation and made him receptive to the motives of leading members of organized crime who instructed him to obtain valuable information about police investigations.
Lawyers must maintain the highest standards of integrity and provide public confidence and confidence.
Ditta betrayed this trust and tried to entrench himself behind the façade of his profession.
Lancashire's Serious and Organised Crime Unit led the investigation into Ditta, which also resulted in a conviction in three cases for possession of cocaine and obstruction of justice, underscoring our determination to bring criminals to justice.
This case should be a warning to criminals and shows that no one can evade the arm of the law.
We'll find you and take you to court.
Scarborough himself was sentenced to 14 years in prison after pleading guilty to conspiracy on the heroin, cocaine and cannabis trade.
Thirty-five other ́persons involved in drug trafficking have been sentenced to a total of 153 years for drug offenses.
On his website, Ditta published an area with questions and answers about himself, in which he stated that his dream job was to represent clients on death row in America as a lawyer, his ultimate guest at the dinner was Mohammed Ali and opportunity inequality was the motivation for his work.
Revolutionary Work of Church Music
At the end of the festivities on the occasion of his 160th anniversary, Liederkranz Dunningen will frame two services with the "German Mass" by Franz Schubert.
After the great success of the musical "Rock my Life", which thrilled the audience in the spring of this year, at the end of the jubilee year, the song wreath will enrich two services with the performance of the German Mass by Franz Schubert.
On Saturday, October 26, this extraordinary Mass will be heard from 19 during the evening Mass in the St. Martin’s Church Dunningen and on Sunday, October 27, from 10.15 a.m. at the church service in the St. John Baptist Church in Lackendorf.
The so-called Deutsche Messe with the original title "Chants for the Celebration of the Sacrifice of the Mass", D 872, is a spiritual musical work of the composer Franz Schubert from 1826 and was considered almost revolutionary at the time of creation.
The Mass will be performed as part of the church services, as the client Johann Philipp Neumann had in mind.
Under the direction of Hermann Schneider, the choir is accompanied by the brass group of the Musikverein Frohsinn Tennenbronn and Noemi Lokodi on the organ.
Haslach: Skepticism gives way to confidence
With the elections to the Parish Council in 2015, for example, fundamental things will change in the Haslach (SE).
There is then only one joint parish council for all six parishes.
In a joint meeting on Saturday, the committees from Haslach, Hofstetten, Mühlenbach, Fischerbach, Steinach and Welschensteinach prepared for the changes.
Because there is nothing to change about the fundamental decisions of the diocese and it is up to the parish councils to implement them as satisfactorily as possible.
In the course of the day it became particularly clear: the skepticism towards the newer gives hesitant but increasing confidence.
From the ideas and expectations for the innovations in 2015 to concrete proposals about the future composition and the size of the parish council, the extremely constructive discussions of the six committees with the full-timers of the pastoral care team ranged.
On the basis of a presentation, regional dean Georg Schmitt explained the newly drafted guidelines for chaplaincy units in the Archdiocese of Freiburg.
In the future, SE Haslach, as a church community, will then form a public corporation that enters into the rights and obligations of the six individual parishes so far.
There will only be a joint parish council and a board of trustees in which the pastor Kraft Amtes is represented.
At least two elected members are to be represented from each parish, and so-called community teams are formed in the six parishes on site.
In the future, their task will be to promote church life in the place.
When asked about the financies of the individual parish, there was a considerable need for discussion.
The innovations provide that accumulated credits in the parishes are earmarked.
Liabilities, on the other hand, are transferred to the municipality as a whole.
Especially with regard to the large planned investments, whose commitment all parishes meet, there were great concerns.
"Even in the past was only invested in projects that could be mastered alone by the individual parishes," Haslach's parish council chairman Bruno Prinzbach relativized.
Barbar Ritter, the chairwoman of the committee in the pastoral care unit Schutterwald-Neuried, described practical experience with a joint committee for five parishes.
They had set themselves the task in 2006, "Become one, stay five" and had to realize that this is not so easy at all.
The committees of the Haslach pastoral unit then set out to answer the questions about the future composition and size of the joint parish council as well as the practical work of the parish teams.
Elaborations will be concretised in further meetings and decided in a further joint meeting of all six committees next spring.
In the end, parish councillor Michael Schöner from Steinach presented the new logo of the chaplaincy unit, which was approved by a clear majority.
In Pfullendorf, the electricity becomes more expensive
The citizens of Pfullendorf have to adjust to higher electricity costs.
As the managing director of the municipal utilities, Jörg-Arne Bias, confirmed to the SÜDKURIER, a four-member Pfullendorfer family 70 to 90 euros additional costs per year.
The main reason for the upcoming price increase is the increase in the so-called EEG levy from 5.277 to 6.3 cents per kilowatt hour.
This levy is redesigned every year in October by the four operators of the major power lines.
These quantify the additional costs resulting from the energy transition, which they can levy through a levy on the price of electricity.
Stadtwerke Pfullendorf forms the last link in this chain.
In its forecasts, the supervisory board of the energy company had even assumed a higher levy, as Jörg-Arne Bias reports.
Now, the concrete numbers would have to be calculated before the municipal utilities could inform their customers about the exact price increase next week.
"We're assuming an increase of 1 cent plus x," according to Bias, with the number more likely to be close to the 2 cents.
Traffic lights on perpetual red: pay attention to waiting time
Sometimes a traffic light just doesn’t turn green.
The contact loop in the street does not react, or the light sign is defective.
Then what to do - drive by red?
If an affected person has waited at a red traffic light long enough and the intersection is free, he can continue, explains the Stuttgart lawyer Ralf Becker in the magazine "Motorrad".
However, the waiting period must be "appropriate", which can be interpreted differently on a case-by-case basis.
Becker advises to endure at least five minutes before starting a malfunction of the traffic lights.
However, those who continue the journey despite red lights must be aware that they bear the full blame for an accident caused by it.
If you tolerate less than five minutes, you may not wait long enough, warns Becker and refers to a decision of the Hamm Higher Regional Court.
In the negotiated case, a driver had ignored the traffic light after about three minutes of permanent red and had to answer for a negligent red light violation.
The traffic light was not defective, but jumped to green a little later.
He was spared a driving ban because of the special situation - but not the fine.
Local council is happy about sound budget
Whenever it comes to the budget in municipal council meetings, Mayor Ralph Gerster laughs well.
The municipal sack in Herdwangen-Schönach is well filled and the administration has not known debts since 2005.
In this sense, the financial year 2012 can also be recorded as a complete success.
This became clear at the presentation of the annual accounts for 2012.
As Andrea Rothmund explained, the administrative budget of around 7.6 million euros over one million euros was higher than planned.
The surplus of the administrative budget, with about 1.8 million euros, is also around one million euros above the approach.
The surplus is mainly due to higher business tax revenues and the municipal share of income tax.
The financial budget was again well above the planned values.
Instead of the envisaged 1.5 million euros, it amounted to 2.5 million euros last year.
"This is mainly due to the higher contribution from the administrative budget," Rothmund said.
Since fewer investments were made than planned in the 2012 financial year, the levieage has also been higher.
In total, the general reserve increased by around 2.1 million euros to a total of 4.9 million euros.
Mayor Ralph Gerster thanked Rothmund and her team for good work.
"You don't just shake such a work out of your sleeve," Gerster praised with regard to the annual accounts.
He and the local councils were visibly happy about the good numbers.
It may sound paradoxical, but it is precisely these good numbers that citizens could soon be expensive.
Due to the good financial situation, the municipality was able to do without high lifting rates in the past.
As a result, according to Gerster, grants have often been slashed by the country.
There are prerequisites for these grants from various compensating sticks.
Among other things, the cities and municipalities that apply for grants must present certain lifting rates.
"We have to look at the topic of lifting rates, because in the coming years there will be investments for which we would like to have grants," Gerster announced in this context.
Nagold: Hugo Wermter in the choir for 60 years
At the age of 60 as a singer Hubert Wermter’s history at the men’s singing club Cäcilia is almost twice as long as that of the autumn festival, in which he was solemnly honored.
Hubert Wermter was on stage as usual in the colors of his men's singing club.
Even after 60 years, he still enjoys singing with the choir, which has now become more than beloved.
Wermter was also active in the association, including 28 years as a member of the committee.
For his commitment and his loyalty to the association, he received several awards: Jörg Kohr, Pastoral Officer on behalf of the Rottenburg-Stuttgart Cäcilian Association, handed him - since the choir is also a church choir - a certificate from the association and an honorary letter signed by Bishop Gebhard Fürst.
His fellow singers presented him with the certificate of the German choral association as well as an engraved tin plate.
In addition to the award ceremony, the 31st autumn festival took place in the usual courses: With the MGV Betra, the Liederkranz Salzstetten, the singing department Baisingen and the local Musikverein, the Vollmaringer had invited a colorful mixture of different choirs and singing styles, which entertained the approximately 400 visitors.
The MGV Vollmaringen opened with atmospheric songs such as "Im Weinparadies" and "Lustig, ihr Brüder".
Their honorary singer Hubert Wermter prepared a small serenade - Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart's "Bundeslied".
The men's singing club Betra also sang classical choral songs such as "Jägerwerben" by Julius Wengert, "Far, far away" by Hubert Goisern as well as the folk song "Wann du durch durchs Tal".
With its mixed choir and its women's group - the "Impulschor" - Liederkranz Salzstetten provided the women's quota of the festival.
The mixed chorus hijacked "Santa Lucia" and "Eviva Espana" by Antos Gus into foreign lands with the Neapolitan folk style, while the "Impuls Choir" also resorted to foreign languages: "Liberatio" and "Hey Jude" were part of their program.
The singing department of the Baisinger Sportverein came back with "Was isch der Schwob?" by Hans Süssmuth and Robert Pappert's "Bierlied", where the Vollmaringer Musikverein offered the crowning musical conclusion.
For the children there was a small side program - "Grandma Hanne" aka Hannelore Stollsteimer performed a Kasperltheater and read a few stories, which kept the children busy for lunch.
Children's dreams come true
The kindergartens and nurseries in the Zollernalbkreis receive mail these days.
In cooperation with Bauhaus and Sparkasse Zollernalb, the Schwarzwälder Bote would like to support the facilities in the realization of their building and beautification wishes.
Does kindergarten need a new sandbox?
All kindergartens, nurseries and crèches from the entire Zollernalb district can participate in the campaign.
If you have a building or beautification request for your facility, you can apply it to the Black Forest messenger.
Be creative with your children and surprise the jury of Aktion Kindergarten-Baustelle.
A jury, consisting of representatives of Schwarzwälder Bote, Bauhaus and Sparkasse Zollernalb, selects three wishes from all submissions.
What are the prerequisites for participation?
The desire should be realized with building material worth a maximum of 2000 euros and on one (action) day.
In addition, the winner himself has to provide for many diligent construction workers.
What's up with the day of action and the helpers?
Bauhaus, with the support of Sparkasse Zollernalb, provides the material.
It is delivered to the kindergarten on the agreed day and should then be processed directly by the helpers.
Helpers can be parents, grandparents, friends, clubs and, of course, the educators and children themselves.
Kenyan press outraged over controversial media law
“We are in a frightening situation and one may ask oneself: what stops Parliament from simply abolishing judicial independence tomorrow?” was to be read in the newspaper, which considers the draft law to be unconstitutional.
“The law is draconic and very punitive and we reject it,” said Cyrus Kamau, managing director of Capital Group, which also includes CapitalFM, one of Kenya’s most respected independent radio stations and news websites.
He said the new mediary tribunal “will always be biased because it’s an extended arm of the government,” and restrictions on content and advertising would damage Kenya’s position on the global economy.
“I hope the president listens to us and we’re appealing to him to reject this bill and send it back to parliament,” he said.
According to The Star newspaper, through the new law, the government “effectively puts the media in a stranglehold,” while The Standard writes that democracy and freedom of speech in Kenya have suffered “a serious blow” and described the draft as “draconian.”
The adoption of the law belongs to a series of measures aimed at strengthening national security in the aftermath of the attacks by armed Islamists on the Westgate shopping center in September.
Kenyan media drew the ire of the authorities because they released footage from security cameras showing the units sent to the crime scene allegedly robbing the luxurious shopping centre.
Police chief David Kimaiyo responded by summoning two journalists and a leading media representative for interrogations, even if the subpoena was withdrawn after an outcry from the media.
According to the new law, mediary houses can receive a penalty of up to 20 million Kenyan shillings and individual journalists with up to one million as well as the further risk of losing their professional recognition or be excluded from official press accreditation.
The tribunal also has the right to confiscate the possession of the persons concerned if a fine is not paid.
According to Daily Nation, “even a single penalty can bankrupt most radio stations.”
In addition, the newspaper believes that these measures could have a scathing effect on Kenya’s “lively blogosphere” according to its description.
If they silence the media, politicians could do unpunished whatever they want.
“No one will ever know,” wrote Mutuma Mathiu, a journalist at the nation who describes the Kenyan media as a crucial control factor in public life.
“Leave politicians to themselves, lead the country into bankruptcy and us back to the time of hunter-gatherers,” he said.
Kenyan legislators have caused public annoyance in the past.
In May, they agreed to undo the reductions ordered by the national remuneration commission and re-enacted their hefty salaries of about 532,000 shilling tax-free per month – they are among the world’s highest.
Bad-Dürrheim: A dream come true for FC
In spite of the cold weather and the rain, the official inauguration ceremony for the artificial turf sports field lost the importance for the city and the football club.
Everyone wished for a more pleasant ceremony last Saturday, but Peter didn’t want to play along.
So all those who came without an umbrella or could not hold one for any reason.
This was for the musicians, who framed the ceremony, as well as many speakers.
That’s whate why FC chairman Albrecht Schlenker, who spoke of fulfilling a dream.
Above all, he thanked Mayor Walter Klumpp, who was strongly committed to the implementation of the project, the local council, the participating companies and members of the association who got involved.
Lothar Held, Paul Weizenegger, Heiner Gail and Peter Graf in particular.
The sprightly pensioners laid 400 square meters of paving stones for the new paths.
Karl Rombach, a member of the state parliament, also paid tribute to this remarkable voluntary commitment.
Further greetings were given for the municipal sports committee Hubert Baier, the district chairman of the South Baden Football Association, Kuno Kayan, and Friedrich Knorr of the planning office, who announced a donation for the youth department of the club.
Mayor Klumpp remembered that recurring weather-related influences in spring and autumn added to the original lawn in such a way that it could not be used.
15 years ago there were the first considerations to solve the problem, when the construction of a third place on the grounds of the football club or at the Realschule am Salinensee was considered.
Both have been discarded again.
Six years ago, thoughts arose about creating an artificial turf sports field, which was generally regarded as the ultimate solution.
Concrete plans were started two years ago, when the FC took over the Schabelstube in Erbpacht, pledged a cost contribution of 100 000 euros for the construction of the square and approved the country's funding of 104 000 euros.
In October 2012, the local council decided to take over the remaining 356 000 euros in the total cost of 560 000 euros, Klumpp summarized.
The start of construction of the 68 by 108-meter artificial turf pitch took place on 4 June.
During the construction phase lasting more than three months, 3000 cubic meters of earth were removed, half of which were used for the creation of a dirt bike track.
Union and SPD have continued their coalition negotiations with the issues of home affairs and the judiciary.
For this purpose, the responsible working group met in Berlin in the morning.
Among other things, it is about direct democracy, deputy bribery and the federal police.
In the morning, the migration and integration working group also wanted to continue its discussions.
Among others, it should be about the issue of dual citizenship, in which both sides have opposing ideas.
The SPD wants to end the compulsion for immigrant children born in Germany to opt for one - the Union rejects a double passport.
On the subject of traffic, which is not on the conference regulations this Friday, the SPD underpained its no to a car toll demanded by the CSU.
A vignette is a "flat rate for frequent driving and thus ecologically counterproductive," the SPD negotiator for the topic of traffic, Florian Pronold, explained in a letter to his group.
Evasive traffic on country roads threatened, which are already accident focal points.
The introduction of a toll for all passenger cars would also only be a first step from Pronold’s point of view.
"There is a risk that the compensation for Germans will be cancelled at a later date," he said.
