Motorist seriously injured in accident
In an accident on Thursday evening, a 37-year-old car driver from Aachen was seriously injured.
According to the police, a 41-year-old from Müsch was traveling with her car on the Kempener outer ring in the direction of Grefrath around 21.15 clock.
As she tried to turn left onto St. Töniser Straße, she overlooked the 37-year-old's oncoming car.
The vehicles collided.
The Aachener suffered serious injuries and had to be taken to the hospital for treatment.
Constance: Closure after accident at Schänzlekreisel
According to police, the accident occurred when a 51-year-old driver of a Swiss Seat Ibiza drove out of town on Thursday evening, around 8 p.m
Shortly before the roundabout at Schänzle, he noticed that he was on the left-turning lane in the direction of Stromeyersdorfstraße and switched to the right lane.
This resulted in a collision with the BMW of a 23-year-old Constancer driving there.
Also damaged was the VW Passat of a 19-year-old driving behind BMW.
In total, about 15000 property damage was incurred, the police said.
The carriageway had to be closed during the accident recording, it said.
Vienna airport abolishes passenger calls
Mr Max Maier, please come to Gate 24.
Announcements like this will no longer exist at Wien-Schwechat Airport in the future.
A majority of loudspeaker announcements are thus omitted - the noise level is to be significantly lowered.
Creating a more relaxed atmosphere is the goal of this innovation.
As airport spokesman Peter Kleemann told Radio Vienna, Vienna Airport is following an international trend with the hiring of announcements affecting individuals.
Airports in Copenhagen, Frankfurt or Dubai, for example, are already refraining from the noisy appeals.
Due to the elimination of these announcements - on average around 200 personal calls through the loudspeakers sound in one day - it should be quieter at the airport in the future.
"It has also been shown in experience that these individual announcements were perceived relatively little, but at the same time all other information on changed boardinges or departure times goes down here a bit," Kleemann said in an interview with the radio station.
Highlander-Games at Kaltenhof
In addition to the brake car pulling with tractors, there will be a second competition at the agricultural autumn festival at the Kaltenhof from 6 to 8 September.
On Saturday, September 7, the Highlander Games will start at 13.30 p.m.
Although they have a Scottish tradition, they are Swabian on the Kaltenhof.
Teams and lone fighters compete in the disciplines of tree trunk throwing, horseshoe throwing and water bucket carriers.
Four and a half meters, the tree trunk is long and weighs 25 to 30 kilograms.
"It doesn't depend on the vastness," explains organizer Peter Saile.
The trunk must be discharged vertically, should turn over once and then come straight to rest.
This is valued with the most points.
When throwing horseshoes, it depends on goal accuracy.
From a distance of eight meters, it is throwned onto a stake.
Skilfulness and strength must be demonstrated by the participants in water bucket carrying over 50 meters.
Time and amount of water in a vat are measured at the end.
From each "clan" or team, two are then allowed to participate in the individual competition.
The winners of the team and individual competition will receive prizes.
You can train from 10 a.m.
Desired is a suitable clothing, for example, the competitors can compete in tarnish skirt.
Swabian costume is also allowed.
For the "outfit", the jury awards a few more bonus points.
You can register up to one hour before the start of the competition.
With the Scottish-Swabian Highlander games and the tractor-pulling, which starts half an hour earlier, there is already a lot of action on the fairgrounds on Saturday.
In the evening, the brass music friends get their money's worth.
At 7 p.m., the birabohmic wind music from Schömberg designs the big entertainment evening.
The chapel plays brisk polkas and marches.
Their repertoire also includes soulful waltzes and a whopping big-band sound.
Heinz Koch, known as a singing innkeeper and Schwarzwurstbaron, will be present from Weilen under the gutters.
In Dornhan he has already appeared at the Christmas market and the guild of fools.
On Sunday the Musikverein Leinstetten to breakfast, from 14 o'clock the Bauernkapelle Böffingen is a guest, and the "Oldies" then end the festival.
The stubbling field party with DJ Ralf will start on Friday.
Other programme items on Saturday and Sunday include the classic car and smuggling meeting, exhibitions of companies on the subject of agriculture and forestry.
Other attractions on Sunday include the maize maze, the artisan market and scissors.
Especially for children there is a petting zoo, straw bouncy castle, tractor driving (under guidance) and tractor surfing.
Organizers are the Förderverein Multizweckhalle, the ski club and the Traktorfreunde "Zündkäpsele".
Milk and egg alternatives at a glance
Vegan diet requires plant-based alternatives to eggs, milk and dairy products.
Piured tofu with plant cream, for example, is a quarker substitute.
The Vegetarian Federation will name others on World Vegan Day on 1 November.
On the occasion of World Vegan Day on 1 November, the German Vegetarian Federation proposes a number of vegan alternatives.
Pure plant margarine is a good alternative to butter, yoghurt can be replaced by soy yogurt.
Instead of milk, soy, oat, almond or rice milk could be used.
Servable plant cream replaces conventional whipped cream.
There are plant-based alternatives to eggs, too.
For example, a half crushed, ripe banana can replace an egg as a binder in a cake.
50 grams of applesauce or a tablespoon ground flaxseed plus three tablespoons of water are also suitable for this.
Doughs become loose instead of egg, for example with a mixture of a teaspoon of baking powder, a tablespoon of cornstarch and three tablespoons of mineral water.
But 50 grams of soy yogurt or silk tofu can also serve this purpose.
