Sleepless in New York
On the way to their host families in Weymouth, Massachusetts, the students of the Schwarzwald-Gymnasium got to know the American city of New York.
After eight hours of flight, we went straight to Brooklyn Bridge, over which the heart of the city, Manhattan, was reached on foot.
There was already a fascinating view of the world-famous skyline, which is a special charm in the evening light.
Due to the time difference, the students were on their feet for almost 24 hours, but according to the motto "The city doesn't sleep," the day was decided at Times Square.
Despite shutdown, on the second day the Statue of Liberty could be reached by ferry and climbed on foot.
Afterwards we went over the financial centre around Wall Street to the memorial of 9/11.
On the third day, the students were able to gain an overview of the intriguingly large city from the 373-meter-high viewing platform of the Empire-State-Building and enjoy the views.
Afterwards we went through the hustle and bustle of the New York streets over the Rockefeller Center to Central Park, which provided an ideal opportunity to linger briefly in the fall sun.
After a short flight from New York to Boston, the students have now arrived in their host families and are experiencing everyday school life at Weymouth High School.
Also this year, on the name day of St. Hubertu in the parish church of St. Marien on Hülsbergstraße, a Hubertus Mass will be celebrated.
According to St. Hubertus was a rather ruthless hunter who was converted through a vision and was later appointed bishop of Liège.
The Jagdhornbläsergruppe der Jagdreiter Westfalen, led by Brigitte Kluth, performs on parforce horns (natural horns in the mood Es) according to old, originally French melodies.
The winds wear riding clothes, because they mainly accompany riders, horses and dog crowds on tow hunts musically.
The solemn Mass will take place on Sunday, November 3, at 11.30 a.m.
The redesigned Opschlag shines with a new cafe.
One that invites you to linger and return.
One who wants to make the name the program.
Déjà Vu, according to Lexikon, is a psychological phenomenon that manifests itself in the feeling of having experienced or seen a new situation before.
In the case of a visit to the café of the same name at Opschlag in the district town of Kleve within sight of the Rhein-Waal University of Applied Sciences, it does not have to stick with the pure Déjà Vu feeling.
After all, the café with the beautiful name has exactly this goal: to make an actual stay not only mental, but always tangible.
Because it's fun and it's just beautiful.
A look into the inner life at Opschlag 8 gives the viewer a good feeling right away.
The state-of-the-art but non-cool café ambience looks like a place to feel good.
Here, the team with the founders Mirjam van der Stelt and Daniel Büttner at the helm spoils the guest with various coffee or cocktail variants.
There's delicious cakes or individually sandwiched fresh baguettes.
The owners, both 33 years old, have already made a name for themselves as restaurateurs before the opening of the pretty café on Opschlag: In Kranenburg, they ran the "Art Lounge".
From the move to Kleve to Opschlag, the sympathetic café lovers are hoping for more audiences.
Last but not least through the university.
"Whether we don't have a specific target group," emphasizes Daniel Büttner.
Rather, the two want to address young students here in the same way as seniors and all age groups in between.
Speaking of cafe lovers.
The sympathetic owner couple stands behind the coffee enjoyment.
Enjoys the fragrant hot brew in a variety of variations itself.
Expressofan
Daniel Büttner is more the Expresso fan and Mirjam van der Stelt the cappuccino drinker.
"A baguette for that - that's my thing," she reveals to the NRZ.
There were also two events in the bright café.
Better said one.
Because the harbour festival took place rather outside - the Café Déjà Vu did not benefit from it.
But there was also the ringer night.
"Niceous, successful and witty," found the 33-year-olds.
But it's not everyone's business.
Maybe that's why Daniel Büttner isn't an outspoken fan of event evenings: "You can't do it all right."
And with an event, I’ll only address those who are interested in the special event.
That’s why events are going to be an exception.
"We want to disrupt normal operations as little as possible."
Déjà Vu also stands for reliability.
In terms of the quality of the products offered here, as well as to the team and the beautiful environment at the Opschlag in Kleve.
The street, which is developing more and more into a gastronomy mile.
To the delight of many clovers, the guests from near and far and the students who discover the gastromeile and thus also the Café Déjà Vu for themselves.
