A day of thanks at the MGV in Dinker
It was the 165th foundation festival to which the MGV "Friedrich Wilhelm Dinker" had invited this year and 52 guests, active singers and their wives, were happy to come.
In the club restaurant "Witteborg" in Dinker, everyone enjoyed the harmonious togetherness completely in the sign of music.
Traditionally, the foundation festival is also always the right festivity to carry out honors.
This time secretary Rolf Wagener was pleased about the special award "Singer of the Year".
"Not only have you written down a lot for us, but you have also made many rounds for us, but our sincere thank-you," says the chairman of the choir Erich Schlotmann.
The awards for participation in the performances and choir rehearsals by means of activity bouquets went this year to the choir director Dieter Schulze with 44 participations, followed by the honorary chairman Horst Pier-Ribbert, the chairman himself, and last year's "singer of the year" Friedrich Winkler with 42 participations each.
Fifth place was scored by Artur Brückner.
The board addressed a special thank you to the team of club owner Ange Pier-Ribbert, who once again conjured up a top-class menu and Gerda Pier-Ribbert for table decoration.
The MGV "Friedrich-Wilhelm" meets for his regular choir rehearsals every Tuesday at 19.45 clock in the club restaurant.
"New singing voices are always welcome," says Schlotmann.
UN Announces New Goals on Poverty Reduction
The United Nations is now beginning to develop a series of new targets aimed at replacing the Millennium Development Goals announced twelve years ago.
In the run-up to the U.N. Summit on Sustainable Development, which began in Rio de Janeiro in the evening, Australian diplomats played a key role in the push for “sustainable development goals” designed to replace the Millennium Goals that expire in 2015.
They have been included in the final draft of the document, which will be adopted by world political leaders, including Ms Gillard, at the summit.
U.N. Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon declared at the summit in the evening that the time had come to "think beyond national interests."
“I am pleased that the member states have agreed to start a process with which universal, sustainable development goals are to be agreed,” he said.
“These objectives will build on our progress from the Millennium Development Goals and form an integral part of the framework for post-2015 development.
I will spare no effort to implement this mandate granted to me by the member states in order to achieve our vision of the Sustainable Development Goals, which are based on the success of the Millennium Goals.”
Even if SG Achim/Baden have been without a win for four matchdays, there is still a good mood for the handball top league.
Coach Tomasz Malmon leaves no doubts about this at all.
Everyone continues to be fully motivated.
Nevertheless, I hope that we finally win again.
"Because I don't even know how such a win tastes anymore," Malmon expects a sense of achievement from his team at VfL Fredenbeck II.
Even if the third-league reserve from Fredenbeck has not yet ripped out trees, Malmon raises his forefinger in advance.
I still coached a lot of players myself in the A-Youth.
That's why they're sure to be particularly motivated against their ex-coach.
"In addition, it has to be seen whether there will still be reinforcements from the first," explains the SG coach.
In order to finally get back on track for success, the Malmon team definitely has to step up compared to previous performances.
Especially in the coverage work and the changeover game, it lacked tremendously recently.
Opportunity exploitation also definitely has to get better.
Therefore, I hope that my players finally got their head clear during the short break.
Mozambique security concerns grow in the face of clashes between influential personalities
Thousands of people gathered in the centre of Maputo under the watchful eyes of the statue of Samora Machel, the founder of the state and first President of Mozambique, in order to voice peace calls at a rare public demonstration.
“We want peace again, we want stability,” said Vanessa de Sousa, managing director of an investment company.
Fearing about the country’s future, she traded her business clothes for a T-shirt reading “We demand security” in Portuguese and joined the crowd in the capital’s Independence Square on Thursday.
For two weeks, there have been almost daily reports of clashes between government forces and Renamo, which are among the worst battles since the peace agreement over 20 years ago.
Renamo was once a notorious rebel movement initially backed by white-ruled Rhodesia and later South Africa's apartheid government in an effort to destabilize the country's independent government.
According to the 1992 peace treaty, she became an opposition party.
Analysts consider it unlikely that the country will fall back into a full-blown conflict, yet recent events have alarmed foreign investors and locals.
For the fast-growing economy, much is at stake, because the discovery of huge gas deposits off the coast and coal warehouses in the north-west could bring investments of over US$50 billion from firms such as Rio Tinto, Vale from Brazil, Eni from Italy and Anadarko from the U.S. into the country over the next few years.
The ruling Frelimo party, the dominant force since 1975, and Renamo blame each other for the tensions.
Renamo claims the government provoked the latest clashes by attacking its members in Sofala province, a traditional stronghold of Renamos, on October 17.
The attacks on the former rebels escalated when government forces attacked Renamo bases and tried to kill Afonso Dhlakama, the leader of the group, Renamo's spokesman explained to the Financial Times.
The government blames Renamo for the clashes and accuses the organization of attacking soldiers.
President Armando Guebuza has tried to downplay concerns about instability.
Speaking to AFP, the French news agency, he said on Wednesday that Dhlakama considered himself a “loser” who “wants to use all his remaining forces to prove that he can impose his decisions on the government.”
Both Frelimo and Renamo stress that they want to avoid a war.
But concerns have increased since Mazanga was quoted as saying Renamo is abandoning the 1992 peace deal.
He told the FT that he meant that Frelimo no longer respected the agreement.
"Our vision is to start negotiations again and seriously," Mazanga said.
Earlier talks between the parties have contributed little to resolving the tensures fuelled by a series of clashes this year.
“We have two grown-up men (Guebuza and Dhlakama) who clash their heads,” says Joseph Hanlon, a lecturer at the Open University and a Mozambique expert.
None of them are good at negotiation and no one is willing to make the necessary concessions.
Renamo, pushing for electoral reform, has already said she will boycott the local elections in November.
Presidential and parliamentary elections are scheduled for next year.
Some commentators have interpreted the use of force as an attempt by an ailing movement to obtain concessions and financial benefits from the government.
Renamo's electoral successes have been shrinking since 1992, while a newer party founded by a former Renamo member, Movimento Democrático de Moçambique (MDM), is likely to improve in elections.
Mazanga maintains that Guebuza – whose resignation is pending when his second term ends next year – wants to destroy the country’s democracy.
"He doesn't want multi-party democracy, he doesn't want transparent elections, he doesn't want peace because he doesn't want to give up the presidency," Mazanga said.
It's clear what capacity Renamo has, but she has made unrestorious attacks on police stations and vehicles on an important north-south connection.
Most battles took place in Sofala province, located a few hundred kilometres north of Maputo, where Beira is located, use the port, the mining companies such as Rio Tinto and Vale to export coal.
In June, Rio interrupted the use of the railway for a month after Renamo announced an attack on the track.
Mazanga responded cautiously when asked whether Renamo would repeat that threat.
Renamo wanted "to warn the international community that things in Mozambique don't stand well," Mazanga said.
Instability has further heightened frustration over the government, says Fernando Lima, head of Mediacoop, an independent media company, because many people are already worried about corruption, sluggish progress in development and a recent wave of kidnapping.
“People think that the government and the president are responsible for the future of the country and he should be the one who finds solutions to the problems,” he said.
Omar Sultane, a protester, said people simply wanted stability.
“None are interested in Renamo and Frelimo, they just want peace and usable roads again,” he explained.
Children should be taught myths and legends as “role models for life,” says an author.
Thor’s stories showed that “brutal power has no chance against clever tricks,” and Arthur’s legend demonstrates the importance of having a dream.
However, many of the myths are “by far too wild, too offensive, and in some cases too dirty to be taught in schools, so Crossley-Holland advocated a “careful selection” of age-appropriate works.
“I think it’s wonderful that myths and folklore are already part of education in America,” he said.
I've proposed this as a plan for twenty years now.
He added that writers and teachers who were “excessively didactic” would “completely shut down” children, because in entertaining stories the messages would be conveyed “subliminally.”
Crossley-Holland, who translated Beowulf from Anglo-Saxon and is author of the Penguin Book of Norse Myths and the British Folk Tales, explained: “You can pursue certain intentions, but you should keep them in the background if possible.”
Perhaps the biggest difference between an adult author who writes for adults, and an author who writes for children, lies in the need to impart hope.
Not everything has to be simplified or lead to the happy ending, but there is an immanent sense of good and evil.
This must be subliminally represented, revealed by history and not explained.
This is the old story of showing instead of storytelling
Hansjakob moves once again in Freihof
100 years ago Heinrich Hansjakob moved into his retirement home in Haslach, on Sunday his last years came alive in the "Freihof".
The many spectators were thrilled by the extremely entertaining scene game.
From the arrival to the awarding of honorary citizenship to his death, screenwriter and Hansjakob performer Alois Kraftzcyk had designed an entertaining piece that received much acclaimation under the direction of Cornelia Volk.
Marcus Zagermann took the audience as a speaker through the ten different scenes, explaining connections and bridged time jumps from the youth to the approaching retirement.
On 22 October 1913 Hansjakob comes home to his children's heaven, to his Freihof.
And then began the scene game in which Alois Krafczyk shined once again in his parade role as the city's great son.
He quietly drove with the two-in-hander and "Schwarzwälder Füchsen" by Erich Becherer from Mühlenbach.
He was not only received by the audience with much applause, his sister Philippine also welcomed him.
Billy Sum-Hermann managed it incredibly well to put herself in the role of the sister and gave her tremendous life in facial expressions and gestures.
Between the individual game scenes, the Mühlenbacher Bauernkapelle played suitably selected pieces of music and rounded off the performance.
In order to award the honorary bourgeoisie, Haslach's mayor Heinz Winkler gave himself the honor, who, on behalf of the then incumbent Hättich, together with a part of the city council, paid his respects.
Their attendance was subsequently made by the Dreikönig-singer, who prompted Hansjakob to proclaim: "Oh how beautiful, youth memories of my own three-king time come alive again".
With their song "O Jesselein" they delighted the audience in Freihof as well as the stork day children with their loud "Get out".
Hansjakob had expressed his criticism of the official church at that time as well as his concerns about the excesses in agriculture, the consequences of industrialization or the effects of the war.
In his homeland, behind his burial chapel on the fire at Hofstetten, he found his resting place.
"A troubled ghost had finally found his rest and had gone home forever," it said at the end of the scene game.
There was plenty of applause and a lot of praising words from the audience for the performance.
Basketball: The hope of the Neckar giants grows
The chances of remaining the league of the relegated Ludwigsburg Bundesliga basketball players have increased.
Because if there is a wild card procedure, then there is now one fewer competitor for the Neckar giants.
The basketball projecture Hamburg Towers waives an application for a possible successor place.
