On the last weekend of August, the Schwarzwälder open-aid museum Vogtsbauernhof in Gutach near Hornberg is once again firmly in children's hands.
Traditionally, the museum welcomes visitors on Saturdays and Sundays 24 and 25 August as part of the summer holiday programme for a colourful children's and family celebration with numerous hands-on activities and events.
"Big and old can learn different old crafts on both days," the press release reads.
Whether making soap, turning candles, felting or crafting ropes, for every age there is something suitable.
The children are helped by regional craftsmen from 11 a.m. to 5 p.m.
There will also be arguing in the museum workshop.
When building cuckoo pipes, water wheels or gyroscopes, the young guests can prove their craftsmanship.
The many old games, such as wheelbarrow races or stimulus running, also promise a lot of fun.
Speed and coordination are required here.
For all small detectives, the open-air museum on Saturday and Sunday, at 12 and 2 p.m. respectively, offers a puzzle tour through the museum.
On an off-road tour, the children have to guess which stories correspond to the truth or are lies.
In addition, visitors have the special opportunity to get to know the open-air museum during a carriage ride with Black Forest foxes.
Not only horses, but also many other animals such as sheep, goats, cows and chicken can be found and marveled at on the premises.
In addition, all children can look forward to the Clown Otsch on Sunday, which drives his Schabernack from 11 a.m. with the museum guests.
Cosy and fairytale, meanwhile, it goes with Hermann Büttner.
The story narrator takes the younger guests into the wonderful world of fairy tales at 11, 13 and 3 p.m.
In addition, the children's costume dance group from Gutach performs traditional dances at 11.30 a.m.
Furthermore, numerous craftsmen such as the broom binder, the seamstress, the spinner, the weaver and the baker can look over the shoulder during their traditional work on two days from 11 a.m. to 5 p.m.
Until the end of the summer holidays in Baden-Württemberg, visitors to the open-air museum can look forward to a varied holiday programme.
In a small town in New Zealand, Burt Muro is considered an adorable owl, because he decided to take part in a race in America with his motorcycle, built in 1920.
Burt, despite a heart defect, isn't dissuaded from his dream.
With his savings, a few donations and a mortgage on his old workshop shed, he transfers by ship to Los Angeles.
There's coffee, tea and cake after the movie.
Shrill beeping bothers residents
Rhenus Midgard has also invested heavily on land.
They distribute the coal coming from the ships to the heap.
The company has several irons in the fire at the site.
In addition to coal imports, Rhenus Midgard relies, among other things, on wind turbine logistics.
Black shimmers the coal on the storage area in front of the dike.
Two enormous blue dump and return chargers distribute or load the coal here as needed.
With every movement of the transshipment devices, a shrill warning noise sounds over the terrain.
"The signals serve for safety and are required by law," says Matthias Schrell, managing director of Rhenus Midgard in Wilhelmshaven.
Unfortunately, this can be heard from afar in unfavorable wind - and so there have already been some complaints from people from the north of the city.
He takes the lawsuits very seriously and has therefore contacted those affected, says the 40-year-old.
In close consultation with the authorities, it is now necessary to keep the warning signs quieter with technical measures.
He continues to rely on open dialogue with neighbors.
Because Matthias Schrell still has a lot going on at the location.
With the expansion of the Niedersachsenbrücke, Rhenus Midgard continues to focus on coal import for power plants and is one of the big ones in Europe in this sector.
Thanks to the sunbathing tub in front of the terminal, which is recessed at 18.5 metres, capesize bulk carriers have long been handled here.
"This year we want to break the 3-million-tonne mark when it comes to an envelope," Schrell said.
In addition to the Eon power plant on site, import coal goes to inland power plants.
If the GDF-Suez power plant is connected to the grid, a good 5 million tons of coal import per year will be realistic.
The three ship unloaders on the bridge, as well as the second conveyor belt, could create up to 10 million.
Bayern's basketball players optimistic despite first defeat
Even the first defeat in the Euroleague could not diminish the huge optimism among the basketball players of Bayern Munich.
"No one can forbid us to believe in winning against the best team in Europe of the past two years," Bayern coach Svetislav Pesic accounted after the unfortunate 83:88 (39:47) on Thursday night at defending champion Olympiakos Piraeus.
We sent the message: Basketball also exists in Munich!
In their only third appearance in the European premier class, the Munich sold very well for a long time, but were simply not settled enough in the decisive phases.
The guests first managed the feat of converting a 15-point deficit 13 minutes before the end, before Piraeus gave the game a change again.
"We struggled incredibly back against the two-time Euroleague champion, but then made reckless mistakes again," Bayern professional Yassin Idbihi said.
We're proud of our performance, but we want to win every game
Malcolm Delaney and Nihad Djedovic were Munich's most successful throwers, who celebrated clear victories in the first two matches against Italian series champion Montepaschi Siena and Polish title holder Zielona Góra.
Ben Greenman: Ten Years of New York Comedy Festival
It could be said that New York City is the birthplace of stand-up comedy in America: almost a hundred years ago, variety artist Frank Fay, who worked as a conférencier at the Palaces Theatre on Broadway, began to directly tell the crowd jokes in the form of a conversation.
Over the years, Fay’s innovation has been expanded, just as recently as the New York Comedy Festival.
Launched and directed by Caroline Hirsch, the founder of the stand-up institution Carolines, this year the festival celebrates its 10th anniversary with over sixty shows in small clubs and big theatres.
“Most of these headliners started at Carolines and then became increasingly successful until at some point they were too well-known to perform at a club,” Hirsch said.
We designed this festival so that we can continue to work with them.
At this year’s event there will be performances by Wanda Sykes, Kathy Griffin and Bill Maher, as well as by “Stand Up for Heroes”, an annual music and comedy benefit event for army veterans at Madison Square Garden, which includes Bruce Springsteen, Jon Stewart, Roger Waters and Bill Cosby.
With the festival, the world of comedy has also grown.
A number of comedians taking part in the festival this year have become known through rather unconventional ways, such as broadcasts on smaller television channels such as Comedy Central, FX and Spike.
Nick Kroll became famous with a sitcom on cable television (the ironic-slippery fantasy football series “The League” on FXX) and now has his own sketch show on Comedy Central.
Jenny Slate was part of "Saturday Night Live" and "Parks and Recreation," even though she's best-knowned for her viral video series "Marcel the Shell with Shoes On."
Both Kroll and Slate and other young comedians with characteristic voices (the surreally pessimistic Anthony Jeselnik, the ironic W. Kaumau Belll focused on racial themes) are products of American comedy's decentralized world.
One of the festival's biggest visitor magnets is an interview: David Steinberg talks to Larry David.
Steinberg began as a stand-up comedian, but has developed into a respected television and film director as well as an unofficial comedy historian.
From 2005 to 2007, he hosted a televised broadcast “Sit Down Comedy with David Steinberg” on TV Land.
The meeting will take place at Town Hall in the middle of Manhattan.
“The city is definitely in the comedy DNA of Larry’s entire work,” Steinberg said.
He told me when he's here, he sometimes walks through the alley between two buildings and thinks: Hey, if I lose all my money, maybe I'll live here.
Iran satisfied with process of nuclear negotiations
Iran is satisfied with the negotiation process a week before the next nuclear meeting with the five UN veto powers and Germany.
"After years, we now have an agreement with the International Atomic Energy Agency to address the differences in recent years," Foreign Minister Mohammed Jawad Sarif wrote on his Facebook page.
Although there would still be a long way to go, the nuclear chief negotiator is satisfied with the negotiation process and also optimistic that in the end both sides will come to a solution.
Drama in Uruguay: Boys kill playing squadrons with machete
Two children have confessed to the murder of an 11-year-old in Uruguay.
The two 14 and 12-year-old boys had invited their victim to go bird hunting with them.
Armed with machetes and knives, they stabbed the 11-year-old on Tuesday and threw the corpse into a well, El País newspaper reported Thursday.
Afterwards, they would have played football in front of their apartments in a working-class district.
The corpse was found the following night in the village of Cerro Pelado, 15 kilometers north of the exclusive beach town of Punta del Este.
As an alibi, a five-year-old half-sister of the younger who had taken her should testify that she was sexually assaulted by the boy.
But when the girl described the crime in front of the police without mentioning an attack against her, the two boys admitted the premeditated killing of the 11-year-old.
Among other things, the elder cited a rivalry in the football game as a reason.
He was provisionally admitted to a psychiatric youth institution.
Pimp has to go to jail
Due to exploitative human trafficking, pimping, threats, insult and dangerous bodily harm, the District Court of Konstanz has sentenced a 33-year-old man from St. Georgen to three and a half years in prison.
The former Bundeswehr soldier, who worked as a bouncer after two missions in Afghanistan, made a confession as part of a trial collusion.
Accordingly, in five cases he met women via Internet or telephone contacts, which he sent after a few weeks against their will on the line.
He used to break resistance with violence and threats.
He kept the income of the women, some of whom bought for him for years.
By confiscating mobile phones and SIM cards, he subdued women's contacts to the outside world.
Together with a colleague who taught the women, he took them to various brothels in the southwest.
There he had them monitored in part in order to control their revenues.
In court, the defendant initially claimed that he suffers from severe trauma after colleagues in Afghanistan died in an explosion alongside him.
After a psychiatric expertent showed great doubts about the psychological restriction and thus of a limited culpability, the 33-year-old made a comprehensive confession.
The court no longer had to hear any of the aggrieved women
According to one detective, at least four other cases of exploitative human trafficking could be determined after an initial display by telephone surveillance measures.
The wiretapped conversations were marked by high aggression.
In subsequent interrogations, the victims would have confirmed their martyrdom.
Another witness, in which one of the women had discovered bruises all over the body, had made no statement to date.
It is "a completely fractured personality" who regards violence and exploitation in this profession as normal.
The defendant regretted his conduct yesterday.
"He broke away from these circles and wanted to live a normal life after detention," he said.
Oil price yields further down to USD 96 per barrel
The price of oil has also fallen further on Friday after worries about a high supply overshadowed a report that China's energy-hungry manufacturing sectory is growing.
December's reference raw oil grade for deliveries had falled 14 cents to USD 96.24 per barrel late in Europe in e-commerce on the New York Mercantile Exchange.
The contract fell 39 cents on Thursday, amounting to a 5.8 per cent price drop in October.
The large supply of crude has weighed on prices in recent weeks.
The U.S. Department of Energy on Wednesday said the U.S. supply had increased by 4.1 million barrels last week.
Over the course of five weeks, supply had risen by 25 million barrels.
Yet on Friday, there was evidence of stronger demand through two reports of an increase in activity in Chinese manufacturing.
This points to a continued recovery in China's economy, whose growth had risen to 7.8 per cent in the third quarter, having reached its lowest level in two decades in the previous quarter.
Crude oil from the Brent variety, a reference value for international crude, which is also used by U.S. refineries, fell 26 cents to 108.58 USD per barrel on the ICE Exchange in London.
Court Blocks NYPD's Stop-and-Frisk Policy
A U.S
U.S. appeals tribunatial 2 said Judge Shira Scheindlin's decisions would be suspended until an appeal by the city was decided.
The judge had ruled in August that the city violated the constitution with the way in which the program would be implemented for stopping and questioning people.
The city appealed against its assessment and the remediative measures ordered, including the decision to set up a monitoring body to assist the police in amending their policy and the relevant training programme.
The appeals court on Tuesday heard the arguments on the required suspension.
The appeal report stated that the judgment had to be relieved of the case because it violated the code of conduct for U.S. judges by determining that a judge should avoid the appearance of partisanship; this was partly grounded with a series of media interviews and public statements in which she had publicly responded to the court's criticism.
The judge had ruled that police officers violated the civil rights of tens of thousands of people because, inappropriately, they specifically targeted black and Latin American men with the stop-and-frisk program.
It appointed an external body to monitor the implementation of significant changes, including reforms in policies, training and service oversight, and ordered a pilot programme with body-carried cameras in neighborhoods where most checks have been carried out.
In August, the city of New York agreed to end the practice of storing the names and addresses of people who were released again after a check by the police.
An oral hearing to appeal the city is scheduled after March 14, 2014.
The stop-and-frisk tactic has been criticised by a number of civil rights advocates.
Controls of this nature have been around for decades in various forms, yet the number of recorded checks during the tenure of non-partisan mayor Michael Bloomberg rose to an all-time high of 684,330 in 2011, most of them black and Latin American men.
In 2004, four men who belonged to all minorities filed a lawsuit that developed into a class-action lawsuit.
Advocates of changes to the NYPD's stop-and-frisk program say the changes will end unfair practices and create a more trusted and effective police, as well as affect the way other police presidencies use the scheme.
Opponents are of the opinion that the changes would dampen the morale of the police, but not crime; also, money would be wasted and the broader problem of a police would not be solved, which is under pressure after the omission of thousands of jobs in the last decade.
The judge pointed out that she did not end the stop-and-frisk practice, which was constitutional, but only reformed the nature of implementation of the checks by the NYPD.
