When he realized how much money was lost on the road, he decided to help the Africans control a budget by the people.
Fuchtel also emphasized that a global economy also requires global love of humanity.
It should by no means be forgotten that Africa is closer than some people think.
From Gran Canaria it is not far to Africa.
Coulson used phone hacking for checking a tip
Former News of the Word editor Andy Coulson allegedly used “phone hacking, surveillance, and confrontation” while attempting to confirm a false tip about an affair with then-Interior Secretary Charles Clarke.
Prosecutor Andrew Edis QC told Old Bailey that News of the World had heard a false rumor in May 2005 that Clarke had a relationship with his “attractive special adviser” Hannah Pawlby.
The newspaper instructed private detective Glenn Mulcaire to chop Pawlby's voicemails and tracked them, but Coulson also called them, leaving behind messages, the court learned.
“According to the prosecution, Coulson, who is now the editor of NotW, is not someone who stands in front of people’s home and hopes to catch them red-handed, but one who likes to confront people with a story to see their reaction,” Edis said.
He said NotW used three procedures to investigatively research stories: telephone hacking, surveillance and confrontation.
The editor is personally involved in the third method.
Obviously he knows about the second, the surveillance, it can't be otherwise.
What about the first one?
Does he know about phone hackinging?
He denies that, but we say, “And if he knew about it.”
Rumours of a Clarkes affair were first picked up by NotW's feature editorial staff when a source sexually interested in Pawlby was told, "Don't waste your time, she's with Charles."
A bang with voicemails from their phone of at least three occasions was secured in Mulcaire's apartment in August 2006.
Investigators also found entries on the detective's computer on which Pawlby and her sister were stored as "projects."
At the time Pawlby grandparents received anonymous calls asking for information about them, Edis explained.
Meanwhile, former chief reporter Neville Thurlbeck and former reporter James Weatherup oversaw Pawlby's movements.
On June 18, 2005, Coulson left her a voicemail with: “I’ve got a storage that we’d like to bring out tomorrow that I’d like to talk to Charles about.”
Edis explained that Coulson's activities in this story followed the same pattern as with other important personalities, such as former Interior Minister David Blunkett.
The jury heard Thursday that Coulson confronted Blunkett about an affair with a married woman when he himself had been dating co-defendant Rebekah Brooks, who had been married at the time.
Coulson and Brooks are arguing about conspiring with others to hack phones between October 3, 2000 and August 9, 2006.
Mulcaire, Thurlbeck and Weatherup have admitted phone hacking.
In protesting against the planned wealth tax, French football will actually carry out the first strike since 1972.
A conciliation meeting comes to an end inconclusively.
In the conflict over the wealth tax, the government and professional football in France have maneuvered themselves into an impasse after a failed conciliation meeting.
President François Hollande received club and federation representatives on Thursday at the Élysée Palace in Paris and also listened to their complaints.
However, he refused to spare football from the planned 75 percent levy on income of more than one million euros per year, which all companies in the country are supposed to pay for two years from 2014.
The other side also remained tough.
The strike announced at the end of November will now also be carried out, said the head association of professional clubs (UCPF), Jean-Pierre Louvel.
The matchdays scheduled between 29 November and 2 December in Ligue 1 and Ligue 2 are thus cancelled.
Gechingen: No absolute protection possible
"We want to involve the inhabitants in the planning at an early stage," Mayor Jens Häussler opened the citizen information event on the flood protection concept in Gechingen.
Around 80 participants had come to the municipal hall to have the planned measures explained, ask questions, express concerns and contribute ideas.
Häussler clarified: "The final decisions are made by the members of the local council".
According to Häußler, the flooding that Gechingen hit unprepared on 15 May 2009 was a drastic event that raised the question of protective measures for the future.
A river basin investigation had been commissioned with the neighbouring municipality of Aidlingen, which is now the basis for the flood protection concept in Gechingen.
"The protection of as many built-up plots as possible is the goal," says Häußler.
It is based on the planning of the protection against a flood that is statistically to be expected every 100 years according to the German weather service.
The climate change is taken into account by a 15 percent surcharge.
In specialist circles, this is referred to by "HQ 100aKlima".
The water masses, which had led to considerable damage in Gechingen in 2009, corresponded to a 1000-year-old flood.
"There is no absolute protection, but we can achieve a relative improvement," Häußler said.
In order to achieve protection HQ 100aKlima, around four million euros must be invested in Gechingen, whereby the municipality can expect state grants in the amount of around 70 percent.
Häussler pointed out, however, that there would only be funding if an overall concept was implemented.
For this purpose, the municipality is dependent on the participation of the citizens.
An elaborate planning and approval procedure had to be completed.
In the best case, implementation could begin in 2016.
Among other things, the protection concept, which was already presented in the municipal council last week (we reported) local measures in the inflow of the river Irm from Stammheimer Tall.
Following the presentation of the flood protection concept, the participants took the opportunity to contribute their concerns.
Among other things, it was questioned why more dams and retention basins were not planned to protect the place.
Gregor Kühn, specialist planner of the commissioned engineering office Wald und Corbe from Hügelsheim, who presented the concept, emphasized that it needed the interaction of all presented measures in order to achieve the desired goal.
In addition, the most economical solution should be found, according to Joachim Wald von Wald and Corbe.
Displeasure prevailed among residents of the old town, which repeatedly suffer from an overload of the canal network, even in normal thunderstorms.
The problem is known to the administration and initial measures have already been taken.
Häussler pointed out, however, that the guidelines for sewer systems are different than for flood protection and this should not be mixed.
The municipality was obliged to interpret the sewer network in such a way that two- to three-year rain events could be tolerated.
Sizing the sewers larger would devoure millions.
Seriously injured after clash
Early Friday afternoon, two motorists were seriously injured in a head-on collision on Landesstraße 44 near Revensdorf.
The police immediately closed the road completely, but could initially say little about the course of the accident.
