It is clear that the 19-year-old handlebars of a VW Golf drove in the direction of Revensdorf and the 38-year-old man from Gettorf approached her with his Hyundai.
Both vehicles collided, with the woman being so severely trapped that it took firefighters just under half an hour to free her from the vehicle wreckage.
The real cause of the accident remained unexplained at first.
Both victims came to nearby hospitals.
The track remained fully closed for about two hours.
You could call him the Dostoevsky of the United States.
Philip K. Dick was a Gottsucher, a metaphysician, his novels and narratives also have something serial.
Amazingly the kinship in the exterior: the stinging eyes, the beard, the high forehead.
And just as Fyodor M. Dostoevsky, with his massive oeuvre, is to be understood as founder and apologist of the Russian soul, one reads Philip K. Dich as an American prophet who is quite true in his own country.
A number of titles have been published by Heyne Verlag, the Haffmans-Kassette with the 118 narratives expels two-thousand-eins.
Dick explains America - the realm of stunning technical progress paired with paranoia, security craze and the belief of being chosen.
Dick had experiences of God, his late books read like a mixture of John's revelation and a computer manual.
Maybe he was crazy, had drugs inflicted on his brain.
He understood early on that the computers are divinities.
He wrote a theology of the computer and asked what distinguishes a man from a machine, the creator from his creature.
That's the "Blade Runner" problem.
The killing.
It's a master of science fiction.
It's a fantastic writer.
His stories have inspired film directors like Ridley Scott, Paul Verhoeven and Steven Spielberg.
This story is particularly significant now.
The "Minority Report" dates back to 1956, exuding the smell of the Cold War and the McCarthy tribunal.
The principle of "pre-crime", "pre-crime", is being developed here, i.e
In Dick's world, which is terrifyingly like ours, mutants look to the future - and the police is grappling.
"We capture individuals who have not violated any law," the authority's chief said.
"We'll catch them even before they can commit a violent crime."
And: "There are no serious criminals in our society, but we have a penal camp full of pesudial criminals". They read time streams.
They assume that bad things are happening, and preventing that the criminal thought will be put into action.
Dick's narrative seems like the blueprint for anti-terrorism laws and the NSA's wiretapping frenzy.
Anticipation of the attack: This is how Obama's drone philosophy and practice works, too.
Dick's story isn't over yet.
The police chief complains that there is no more separation of powers, the military controls everyday life - and what is left of the state.
Then the superviser himself is monitored and convicted of a future crime.
The system's going crazy.
It's perfect, but it's lying.
Invent its own requirements.
Not only accepts errors and victims, but builds on them.
The system creates the danger it combats.
Reading Philip K. Dick means to get closer to the truth again and again.
Spying affair over Merkel testified in Russia
Former U.S. intelligence expert Edward Snowden could also make statements in his Russian asylum in the U.S. spying affair around Chancellor Angela Merkel.
Representatives of Germany's Federal Prosecutor's Office could either ask questions in writing or meet the 30-year-old in person in Russia, Interfax agency reported.
Earlier yesterday, the Greens member of the Bundestag Hans-Christian Ströbele was the first German politician Snowden to meet in Moscow to talk to him about the affair.
NSA revelations intensify corporate paranoia over government surveillance
On a mild day at the end of August, a German police helicopter flew deep over the U.S. consulate in Frankfurt am Main, Germany's financial capital.
On the instructions of the Federal Office for the Protection of the Constitution and Counter-Terrorism (BAT), the German domestic intelligence service, he was to photograph the roof of the U.S. outpost, which is located less than 5 km from the European Central Bank and the Bundesbank.
According to the German media, the BAT wanted to prove the presence of surveillance antennas and the action led to an exchange of words between the USA and the German Foreign Ministry in Berlin.
James Clapper, U.S. Director of National Intelligence, insisted again in September that the U.S. did not use opportunities for foreign espionage “to steal the trade secrets of foreign companies on behalf of U.S. companies so that they could expand their international competitiveness or profit.”
Yet since Edward Snowden, the whistleblower-made former collaborator, has begun publishing his cornucopia of U.S. surveillance secrecies, European governments and company bosses are no longer sure if they can take the director at his word.
Reports that the American National Security Agency is spying on the Brazilian oil company Petrobas and procuring data from American cloud providers, including Google and Yahoo, has pushed corporate paranoia to a new high because of government surveillance.
The barren to overflow brought the revelation that Chancellor Angela Merkel's phone had been bugged, possibly for ten years.
If the most powerful person in Europe can be targeted, then company managements are certainly also potential targets.
Snowden has made intensive cooperation between U.S. intelligence agencies and companies transparent.
I believe it is imaginable that this data will be used for mutual benefit.
“Germany needs to wake up,” explains Oliver Grün, president of BITMi, which represents small and medium-sized IT companies in Germany.
German companies believe the U.S
However, there has so far been no evidence in the documents leaked by Snowden that the US has passed on the trade secrets of foreign companies to its own companies.
Politicians have expressed their concern that the EU lacked certain IT and internet opportunities and that it should reduce its dependence on the US.
Companies are skeptical in this regard.
It was proposed in the Bundestag that we should build up a German Google.
I can only close my eyes and slowly reopen them ...
“It doesn’t work like that,” says Hasso Plattner, chairman of the German software maker SAP.
If you wanted a strong European IT industry, you shouldn’t have let it die out 20 years ago.
Everything in Germany is subsidized, from coal to cars to agriculture.
Everything, except for the IT industry.
Nevertheless, reach and technical sophistication from U.S. espionage agencies uncovered by the Snowden revelations have shocked firms that had previously seened China's greatest espionage risk.
Cloud computing is undergoing a major change, because European company managers are now aware that data stored in the U.S. is subject to jurisdiction there and is therefore potentially endangered.
According to a survey conducted by the Cloud Security Alliance, a trading institution, after the revelations about U.S. data mining activities with Prism, about ten percent of non-U.S.-based members rejected plans to use an American provider.
Jim Snabe, deputy managing director of SAP, says, “We’re seeing a new issue of customers that didn’t exist a year ago – it’s: where will my data be stored and can you guarantee that these will remain physically in this jurisdiction?”
Many German company managers take the view that the recent reports simply confirm what they already knew anyway, namely that powerful states want to steal their most valuable secrets and they therefore have to be guarded at all costs.
That economic espionage takes place is no surprise.
It's always been that way.
“It has been an issue for many years and has not fundamentally changed due to the current discussion,” says Kurt Bock, chairman of the chemical company BASF.
Americans are spying on a commercial and industrial level, just as we do, because it's in the national interest to defend our businesses.
Corporate leadership usually doesn't brag about the countermeasures you've taken, because that gives attackers advantages.
In large companies, it has long been reflected to employees that the taking of a free USB stick at a trade fair or the unguarded lying down of a laptop in the hotel room is not advisable, to say the least.
Ulrich Hackenberk, board member at car manufacturer Audi, says that for years it has belonged to the standard practice of collecting the mobile phones before a board meeting, so that they cannot be used as eavesdropping devices.
The German BAT advises executives to use simple pre-paided mobile phones if they travel abroad because smartphones may be compromised.
Prepaid phones are thrown away afterwards.
There is concern, however, that small and medium-sized enterprises remain vulnerable to hacking and surveillance.
In Germany, many of these companies are the world’s market leaders in the respective niche.
“Small and medium-sized enterprises often lack the experience, human resources and financial resources to effectively protect trade secrets against unauthorised access,” the BAT warns in a report.
The U.S
According to the Washington Post, France, Russia and Israel were ranked second behind China in the U.S
A board member of a German blue-chip company agreed that when it comes to economic espionage, “the French are the worst.”
Bernard Squarcini, former head of France’s DCRL, was quoted as saying this month in an interview: “Secretaries know very well that all countries, even if they work together in the fight against terror, are spying on their allies.”
Hechingen: Fair gives answer to wedding questions
"Marriage is the most important journey of discovery that man can undertake," said philosopher Sören Kierkegaard.
Tips on how at least the wedding celebration will succeed have now been given by a special fair in the domain in Hechingen.
