Parents of a Georgia teenager who died in tragic accident believe murder
Parents of the teenager, whose corpse was found in a curled-up wrestling mat in the gymnasium of his high school, are convinced that their son was murdered, the family's lawyer explained Thursday.
Kendrick Johnson from Valdosta in the U.S. state of Georgia was found on January 11 in an upright-mounted mat that stood behind the grandstand in the school gym.
Investigators from the Lowndes County Sheriff concluded Johnson died in a tragic accident, which the 17-year-old's family doubts.
“They are convinced that their son was murdered,” Benjamin Crump, Kenneth’s and Jacquelyn Johnson’s lawyer, told FoxNews.com.
They never believed he died the way the sheriff concluded.
“They are of the opinion that it lacks any logic, violates the laws of physics as well as against common sense,” Crump said.
Rather, they are convinced that the truth will be covered up in order to protect the person or persons responsible for the death of their son.
“Her son went to school to study and came back in the corpse sack,” he comments.
U.S. Attorney Michael Moore said on Thursday that there will be a formal investigation into Johnson's death, as a number of important questions remain unresolved.
What was the cause of death?
Is his death due to a crime?
Moore explained this at a press conference on Thursday afternoon.
I'm following the facts about whatever direction they're going.
My goal is to figure out the truth.
“I take the view that there is sufficient basis for a formal investigation,” he said.
Moore told reporters the initial autopsy indicated that Johnson had died from the consequences of "position-related suffocation."
However, a second autopsy resulted in another cause of death, according to Moore.
"There are a number of questions that need to be answered or confirmed," he said.
Moore added that if there is sufficient evidence of a criminal or civil investigation into Johnson's death, he will instruct the FBI to conduct the investigation.
At our request on Thursday, a representative of the county sheriff's authority was not immediately ready for comment.
A court in southern Georgia on Wednesday ordered that authorities must release any surveillance video that investigators have reviewed.
The teenager's parents said they hoped the video recordings contained clues about how he had died.
CDC releases allergy guidelines for schools
On Wednesday, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) released a set of guidelines for dealing with children's food allergies in schools.
These are the first such guidelines the U.S. government has issued as the number of school-age children with food allergies increases.
One in 20 children in the United States, meanwhile, has a food allergy.
The spread of food allergies among children increased by 18 percent between 1997 and 2007, according to the CDC.
The guidelines contain information for schools, which are intended to raise awareness of food allergies among teachers and staff, and how to deal with children showing allergic reactions.
It is also recommended that schools have epinephrine in stock – the EpiPen brand’s auto-injection is most commonly used – to face a potentially fatal hypersensitivity reaction.
Legislators in the states have recently changed regulations so schools can keep epinephrine in stock more easily.
The report also contains a list of typical symptoms mentioned by children who have an allergic reaction.
Children may say, “It feels like something presses on my tongue,” “My tongue feels like there’s a hair on it” or “My tongue tingling.”
Parents of intersex children can choose “gender indefinitely”
Germany is the first European nation in which a third gender is recognized for children born with indefinite genitals.
Newborns are no longer rigidly assigned masculine or female.
Under the new law, parents no longer have to declare gender for such children, but can state “indefinitely” or “non-determined” on the birth certificate.
The aim of the law is to relieve the pressure from parents so that they don't have to make hasty decisions regarding surgeries for gender assignment in newborns, and fight discrimination against intersex people.
According to the BBC, an intersex person said years later: “I am neither a man nor a woman.”
I'll remain the patchwork created by doctors, mutilated and full of scars.
It is estimated that one in 2,000 children are born each year neither as a boy nor as a girl.
They belong to intersengers, a group that falls under the diagnosis “sexual differentiation disorder” (DSD), a collective term for people with atypical chromosomes, germ glands (egropies or testicles) or uncommonly developed genitals.
Wallis Simpson may have been intersex.
The understanding of gender identification is still not very well developed, but most experts in the U.S
New York psychiatrist Dr Jack Drescher, who specializes in the issue of gender identification, said the new German law “sounds like a good cause.”
Intersex children are an ethical dilemma.
“Some people have life-threatening illnesses that necessitate surgery, but for most children this does not apply,” he said.
You can make a gender assignment without surgery and then wait to see how the identity develops.
The scientific data about how a child even develops his gender identity is not very accurate.
No one knows why it's happening.
It's like the secret, which is why people are homosexual.
A 2011 report to the European Commission states that intersex people must be distinguished from transgender or transgender persons, because their status is not gender-related, but is conditioned by their biological constitution, which is neither exclusively male nor female, but typically defines both simultaneously or not clearly as one of them.
These characteristics were found in subordinate sexual characteristics such as muscle mass, hair, breasts and stature, priority sexual characteristics such as reproductive organs and genitals or in chromosome structures and hormones.
The report also provides an overview of discrimination faced by intersex and transgender people in professional life, as well as the level of harassment, violence and prejudgment.
Boys not consistent with the gender picture now have a special camp.
It is now possible in Australia and Nepal for adults to provide male, female or “third gender” on official documents.
In June, Australian Norrie May-Welby (52) was recognized as the world’s first “genderless” person after a court granted the lifelong retention of “non-determined” gender status.
In addition to M and W, German ID cards will have a third assignment in the future, X for intersex, according to the Ministry of the Interior.
In neighbouring France, gender issues are still controversial, according to a news report on France 24.
In 2011, dozens of French MPs in the strictly Catholic country signed a petition that the “gender theory” should be removed from textbooks.
The U.S. website Catholic Online has also spoken out against German law, saying: “If the world is dragged into a new state, where gender is a decision, but not sexual activity, then we twist two more pillars of civilization.”
The mother of a newborn from Maryland explained to Baby Zone that she thinks it would be better if babies were assigned a gender at birth.
“Parental existence is already stressful enough, even without further restrictions, especially if you don’t know the gender of your own child,” she told the parenting website.
Children need stability and security.
Historically, children born with both male and female genitals were referred to as hermaphrodites, according to the attractive Greek god who had both sexes.
Ten years ago, medicine viewed gender as something you can wipe out and then rebuild.
But in the meantime, the ethical foundations of an operation are doubted, since it is now known how complex the gender identity is, and that doctors can also be wrong, because they do not know how the child feels when growing up with the gender assigned by them.
“In the middle of the 20th century, it was called a ‘psychiatric emergency’,” Drescher said.
If such a child was born, you didn't call the psychiatrist but the surgeon.
The prevailing theory about the treatment of children with indefinite genitals goes back to Dr. John Money from Johns Hopkins University, who took the view that gender is malleable.
He coined the term “sex identity” and argued that social and environmental impetus – as the parents raise a child – interacted with a child’s genes and hormones and thus shaped whether a person considered himself to be male or female.
However, in a 1966 fame known as “John/Joan”, his theories became controversial.
He advised the parents of a boy, whose penis had been severed during a botched circumcision, completely neutering the child and also removing his testicles and then raising him as a girl.
“Money presented this case as a successful example of a transformation, but it wasn’t,” Drescher said.
When the boy was about 15, he switched back to the male sex and married a woman.
At 38, however, he committed suicide.
According to Drescher, there are still doctors who would practice this model.
However, in the 1990s, with the entry of the Internet, survivors of these gender surgeries who are “not happy with the result” have come out.
Jim Bruce, a 36-year-old author from Montana who was born with XY-male chromosomes but ambiguous genitals.
Doctors weren’t clear about whether he had a large clitoris or a small penis and were convinced he could never lead a “satisfactory life” as a man.
So his external organ and testicles were surgically removed shortly after birth in 1976 and he was raised as a girl.
At the age of 12, he received female hormones.
"I knew I wasn't a girl," he told ABCNews.com
I've been unhappy, but it's been really hard to ask questions
At the age of 18, he was supposed to receive a vaginal plasty.
But since he was depressed and knew that something was wrong, he demanded his medical records.
What he found out was shocking.
I was sterilised at my birth – and no one ever told me that.
Bruce was born with a DSD through which his body could not produce enough testosterone to develop genitals.
After learning the truth, he switched back to male identity, received testosterone injections and had his breasts removed.
As a result of the operation, he has become infertile.
Today, he's campaigning for others in an organization called Interface Project that intersex people are perceived as normal.
But Anne Tamar-Mattis, managing director of the California-based legal group Advocates for Informed Choice, is worried that German law could “encourage and stigmatize”.
“Many activists are concerned that the German decision could encourage parents to make quick decisions and assign a ‘not fixed’ to the child,” she said.
We fear this favors interventions.
We think it would be better assign the male or female sex and then wait.
But we don’t yet know how the law will work, so we can only speculate.
Tamar-Mattis said her organization supports Australian law because "it allows adults to be recognized as a third gender."
“Adults should be able to make their own decisions about their legal gender,” she said.
The German law is concerned with the allocation at birth.
This isn't a struggle to force young children into at this time.
When they grow up, they can decide for themselves about their body.
But Dr. Arlene Baratz, a breast radiologist from Pittsburg who has a daughter with a sexual differentiation disorder and supports hundreds of others in a self-help group, says the German law “authorizes” both parents and children.
Katie, the daughter of Baratz, was born with male chromosomes, but has a DSD called complete androgen resistance.
Due to the receptor defect of the target cells for testosterone, Katie developed female characteristics.
She has a vagina but no uterus and no ovaries.
Today, Katie is 29 and married; she works at the University of Pennsylvania as a child psychiatry employee.
Although she is barren, she hopes to have a child through adoption or a surrogate mother.
“The law gives parents room, so they don’t have to make decisions rushed,” Baratz said.
It gives them the time to do different tests and clarifies things and thus gives them time before they write “male” or “female”.
In this way, everything is okay – you raise the child and love him.
You have a wonderful baby and enjoy parental happiness.
And you don't have to hurry to perform an operation that can't be reversed anymore.
“This includes the children in the decision and eliminates the fear that motivates parents, because they think they don’t do the right thing,” she explained.
Ultimately, the child decides on the gender that suits him better – and that’s wonderful.
As a result, children will be able to make the decision themselves.
Unknowns injure girl by shot from car
Unknown persons have injured a twelve-year-old in Saxony-Anhalt during gunfire from a car.
The perpetrators had probably fired at a group of children with an aerial pressure weapon on Thursday evening in Genthin.
This was announced by the police in Burg.
The girl was slightly injured on the left calf.
Rescue workers took the girl to the hospital.
The shot came from a small car that had driven past a group of six children and suddenly stopped.
Witnesses seen two people sitting in the car.
The perpetrators also shot at a bus stop.
Smashed a slice.
Afterwards, they drove off.
Criminal investigators are investigating, it said on Friday.
Up to this point in time, the Kempten police inspection can look back on a quiet Halloween night.
The officers were called only twice to egg-throwing children, who threw them against house facades.
Since the homeowners recognized this in time and removed the egg leftovers immediately, no property damage should have occurred.
Furthermore, in the urban area probably also some Sylvester bellers were ignited, but here too no property damage has occurred.
