However, until the arrival of the police, the man has found his cyclist blinds in the immediate vicinity, which is why the police did not have to take further action.
housing with a future perspective
Waltraud Ries actually lives fantastically.
Their house is located in a quiet residential area of Stuttgart with lots of greenery, an old tree stock, nice neighbors and not too much traffic.
In the city it is only a few minutes by public transport.
"You're hopefully not afraid of spiders?" Waltraud Ries asks, pointing to a fat spider on the door frame.
I've been looking for a new apartment for my husband and me for some time
But you know how difficult this is in Stuttgart.
The reason, however, is not spider phobia, but the concern of not being able to cope with the many stairs to the house and in the apartment in old age, she explains.
In addition: Since a meniscus operation, the mid-fifty woman has had to experience what it means to move from floor to floor with a handicap.
Recently, a book by her has been published on the topic 'Happy living in old age - Which type of housing is best for me?'.
"Our apartment is the best example of what doesn't work in old age," she begins.
When she moved into the duplex apartment with her husband more than 20 years ago, it was just a nice apartment in the middle of the countryside.
Today at the age of fifties Waltraud Ries thinks about it differently.
But she also knows that she is one of the few people who even deals with the topic of 'living in old age'.
Most displace it as long as possible.
Only the others are getting old," she says smirking.
Only when the pressure of suffering is really great, do you worry.
"But then it may already be too late," says Ries.
In her book, the interior designer presents 17 residential models for self-determined living in old age.
"But which type of housing you choose is always an individual decision," she explains.
There is no solution for age.
Even those who can afford retirement in Tuscany or Brittany should always consider that they can also get sick.
And: 'Without good knowledge of the national language, you can also unite in the Dolce Vita,' the specialist book author points out to consider those willing to leave.
Among their models are classic care models, alternative forms of housing or the oft-cited pensioner's shared flat.
In old age, everyone has their ticks and quirks
"For me, such a residential community would be nothing," she says with a wink.
Certainly not like in a student apartment with a shared bathroom and a kitchen.
"In old age, you just need your retreats," says Ries.
Nevertheless, the book author does not want to rule out that a senior living community can still function.
However, this would have to be different than in the classical student flatshare.
And sometimes you just want your rest," she believes.
With this opinion, at least in the state capital, she is not alone.
"Most old people in Stuttgart want to stay in their own apartment as long as possible", is also the experience of Theresa Rütten, the head of the citizens' service life in the age of the state capital.
Her department advises people around the topic of 'getting older'.
Therefore, especially older people would often accept inconveniences and restrictions, only to be able to stay in their usual environment, that is also the experience of the Stiftung Warentest.
Today there are many ways to make the house or the apartment barrier-free.
Ries recommends all those who deal with the age-appropriate conversion of her house or apartment, first of all to inform themselves thoroughly and not to commission the first craftsman.
Today there are specialists for the age-appropriate conversion.
Even in the rental apartment, one or the other can be realized, and if it is only the toilet seat increase, explains the interior designer.
But it is not always possible to redesign the existing apartment according to age and thus barrier-free, even if it is still beautiful.
Waltraud Ries had to have this experience.
Although you could install a stair lift within the apartment, which is also paved with stairs to the apartment door would be the much bigger obstacle.
She'll keep searching until she's found the ideal object for herself and her husband.
Before the relocation, she's daring more than before the spider at her apartment door.
U.S. to prosecute Snowden further
The U.S. hasn't changed its stance with former U.S. intelligence official Edward Snowden.
He is still accused of illegally leaking classified information.
Therefore, he would have to face criminal proceedings in the U.S
Snowden's recent remarks won't change that, either.
Earlier, after a meeting with Snowden in Moscow in Moscow, the German Green politician Hans-Christian Ströbele appealed to the USA not to threaten him further with punishment.
Psaki emphasized that Ströbele’s remarks were that of a parliamentarian and not that of a German member of the government.
Intelligence revealer Edward Snowden is in principle ready for a conversation with German authorities.
At first, however, he wants his situation to be resolved.
This has been made clear by the US-sought former NSA employee in an unaddressed letter published by the Green politician Hans-Christian Ströbele on Friday after a meeting with Snowden and forwarded to the federal government, the Bundestag and the federal prosecutor general.
According to a translated translation circulated by Ströbele, the letter reads as follows:
To the responsible persons
I've been asked to write to you about your mass surveillance investigation.
My name is Edward Joseph Snowden and I was formerly contractual or employed through direct employment as a technical expert at the National Security Agency (NSA), the Central Intelligence Agency (CIA) and the Defense Intelligence Agency (DIA) of the United States.
In the course of my preoccupation in these institutions, I witnessed systematic violations of the law of my government, which led me to act out of moral duty.
As a result of the publication of these concerns, I saw myself exposed to a serious and persistent incitement that forced me to leave my family and home.
I am currently living in exile and enjoying temporary asylum granted to me by the Russian Federation in accordance with international law.
I'm encouraged by the response to my political actions, both in the U.S. and elsewhere.
Citizens around the world, and also senior officials - including the United States - have judged the revelations on a system of all-encompassing surveillance that is not accountable to anyone as a service to the public.
These espionage revelations prompted many proposals on new laws and directives aimed at the previously covert abuse of public trust.
The benefit to society from these findings is becoming increasingly clear; at the same time, the risks accepted were visibly reduced.
Although the result of my efforts has been demonstrably positive, my government continues to treat dissence as a breach of allegiancy and seeks to criminalize and indict political expression.
Nevertheless, speaking the truth is not a crime.
I am confident that with the support of the international community, the United States government will abandon this detrimental stance.
I hope that, if the difficulties of this humanitarian situation are resolved, I will be able to participate appropriately and in accordance with the law in clarifying the facts relating to statements made in the media, in particular with regard to the truth and authenticity of the reports.
I look forward to a conversation with you in your country as soon as the situation is resolved and thank you for your efforts to uphold international legacy that protects all of us.
Edward Snowden testified by Hans-Christian Ströbele
Nursery has found a private buyer
"The building is in good hands," says Winterlingen's mayor Michael Maier.
The commune has sold the former kindergarten in Gartenstraße to a private person.
The local council agreed to the sale at its most recent meeting in the non-public section.
The building changed its owner at a "reasonable" price.
"The community is satisfied," Maier insists.
100 000 euros were allocated in the budget.
It is not yet clear how the new owner intends to use the previous kindergarten.
The mayor is right that Winterlingen handed over responsibility for the building to someone else before the winter and thus no longer has to pay for the maintenance of the house, how to heat, look for the garden and clear the sidewalk.
"This effort is gone now," Maier rejoices.
Square concert in high summer temperatures
The Musikverein Hammereisenbach gives a square concert on the terrace of Gasthaus Hammer.
As conductor Bianca Willmann lifts the baton at 7:30 p.m., the thermometer still shows 25 degrees.
About 50 listeners enjoy the sounds of Hammrich musicians.
Chairman Manuela Honeck explains the pieces of music.
In this context, Honeck thanked all those who had helped to successfully cope with the first musician hock.
Report: Obamar campaign pondered replacing Biden with Hillary Clinton
President Barack Obama's closest advisers have secretly contemplated replacing Vice President-elect Joe Biden with Hillary Clinton in the 2012 election campaign, according to the New York Times.
The revelation is the most noteworthy surprise of the long-awaited work of Mark Halperin and John Heilemann about the 2012 election campaign, “Double Down: Game Change 2012”.
The Times has a copy of the forthcoming box and it reported on Thursday night that the president’s top advisers conducted “extensive group meetings and polls in late 2011” to find out whether Obama’s dwindling hopes of re-election look better if he drops Biden.
According to Jonathan Martin, national political correspondent of the Times, the book thoroughly traces the history of the efforts of the campaign and the White House, namely the former White House Chief of Staff Bill Daley, to measure the effect it has on the poll ratings to swap former Secretary Clinton for the vice president.
The potential change was a well-kept secret within campaign infrastructure in Chicago and the Oval Office.
Only half a dozen of the president’s closest advisers—including Daley’s former campaign leader, Jim Messina, and former White House senior adviser David Axelrod and David Plouffe—knew about the planned change.
“Double Down” claims that Daley was at the forefront of Biden’s efforts, despite her “close, personal relationship,” before finally deciding against such a move, after the data showed that Clinton’s candidacy “wouldn’s chances don’t improve Obama significantly.”
In an interview with Martin, Daley confirmed that the administration had actually considered replacing Biden with Clinton.
“I’ve voiced quite a number of things to consider, and that was one of them,” Daley told the magazine.
You have to remember that the president had terrible poll numbers at the time, so we thought, “For God’s sake, what do we do?”
While Daley dubbed the investigations a “commanded care,” Martin told CNN’s Anderson Cooper that there had been considerable effort during the re-election campaign to find out if such a move would pay off in view of ballot boxes.
“In campaigns, there’sn’t as much money spent on surveys and focus groups if you’re not seriously thinking about something,” Martin explained on AC360.
However, it is not clear whether Obama knew that his team was thinking about an exchange.
Martin told CNN he asked Daley if his then-boss had known about the potential change.
Although Daley explained that he did not believe that the president was “aware” of the possible change, the former chief of staff admitted that it was “possible” for Obama to know about it.
Martin added that “double down” did not provide a definitive answer to the question of whether the political probes had reached Obama’s desk.
Cooper asked Martin if he seriously thought Obama didn't know about the investigation into whether to drop Biden in the election.
“Possibly,” Martin replied.
What's happening on the go with my email?
E-mail actually means electronic letter.
In the design of the Internet service e-mail similar to the conventional mail, was the letter secrecy technically implemented?
When designing the early Internet services, the focus was on making communication possible.
Therefore, the basic technical protocols did not pay attention to creating a stable foundation for listening-proof correspondence.
Therefore, a conventional e-mail corresponds to an open postcard rather than a sealed letter.
If user "Anna" sends an e-mail to "Benni", are the computers of the two directly connected?
Anna’s e-mail program or browser sends the mail to your service provider’s e-mail server in the first step.
For example, if she has a GMail account, that would be Google.
This mail server sends the content to the provider that Benni uses.
On the route, the mail can run via several other servers on the Internet.
Bennie can then call the content at his provider.
Are traditional e-mails being read on their way through the network?
Most of the mails are read several times by software robots on the go.
At the provider of the sender, it is usually checked whether the mail carries a malicious program as an attachment.
A virus check is also carried out on the receiver systems.
In addition, the providers check whether it is an annoying and unwanted spam mail, which is immediately deleted or at least sorted away into a spam folder.
Do the robots have another task?
In systems such as GMail, the robot also determines the information that Google needs to place context advertising.
If Anna and Benni communicate via GMail about their upcoming holiday trip, Google can display corresponding links to holiday offers.
Do unfamiliar people also read the emails?
The probability that unauthorized persons will read an e-mail is extremely low.
Theoretically, however, this is possible.
In companies, administrators often have the opportunity to read electronic mail.
Law enforcement and intelligence agencies also have legal ways to intercept or take note of emails.
In addition, there are possible illegal spying actions.
Is it possible to protect e-mails against reading?
With encryption according to the OpenPGP standard, an e-mail can be effectively encrypted against reading.
With high technical effort, it is also possible to obscure the metadata of an e-mail communication, so that it cannot even be readily recognized who communicated with whom.
How important are Yahoo and Google in the email market in Germany?
Yahoo and Google play only a minor role in the mainly privately used mailboxes, according to an investigation by Convios Consulting from August 2013.
While Yahoo-Mail's shares had fallen most recently, GMail was able to gain significantly from Google.
Mother buried in woods and retirement conceded
When his elderly mother died, a man from Wolfsburg forged a plan.
He buried the dead in a forest - and continued to concede their pension and care allowance.
In order to be able to further collect pensions and care money, a 67-year-old Wolfsburger buried his dead mother in the forest, according to police investigations.
At first, he covered up the death of the 89-year-old, then he slashed the money for more than a year and a half.
Now the 67-year-old has to answer for fraud, as a spokesman said on Friday.
Accordingly, the man hid the woman in a forest near Helmstedt.
The man was suspected as early as May: At that time, it was noticed that no more medication was needed for the mother in need of care.
Investigators said the pensioner said that his mother had travelled to Spain.
Because this seemed strange to the officers, they continued to investigate.
It turned out that the two had lived in a shared apartment for more than 15 years and that he had last cared for them.
The 67-year-old finally admitted the death of his mother.
An autopsy, according to the police, revealed no evidence of a capital crime.
Fancy ice chame that glows in the dark?
A British entrepreneur has developed the first ice glowing in the dark – using jellyfish.
Charlie Francis took advantage of the fluorescent properties of the marine animals and created a luminous treat from them.
He came up with the idea after reading research on jellyfish and convincing Chinese scientists to artificially produce the luminous protein.
The ice cream reacts with the tongue during consumption, which raises the pH value in the protein and triggers the glow.
Chris explains that the ice cream reacts to the heat in your mouth and glows – so the more you lick, the brighter it gets.
Charlie, founder of the ice cream company “Lick Me I’m Delicious”, says: “The product is unbelievable, but still in a very early production phase, and so you get two grams of it for 200 pounds.”
The protein used in the ice cream reacts with the tongue at a neutral pH value.
When the mouth heats the protein, the pH rises and the ice glows.
We've tested it in the last few months and it seemed ideal to bring it to market on Halloween because it has this wonderful luminous effect.
It's probably the most expensive ice cream I've ever made because the jellyfish phosphor is four times more expensive than gold.
Each bullet costs me about 140 pounds.
But it tastes delicious for that.
Charlie's experimental Bristol-based company is famous for its unusual flavours, which include beer, cheese, beef and gold leaf.
But his latest creation should be even more ambitious.
He said, "I'd like to develop an invisible ice cream."
This in and of itself is impossible due to the refraction of light caused by the ice crystalls that make up ice cream; but I suspect that we find a way to make it.
The ice cream uses the fluorescent properties of a jellyfish artificially produced by Chinese scientists
The TSV Morsum is after six matchdays still without any point in the handball association league.
Right now it comes for the team of Ingo Ehlers tomorrow to the derby against the TSV Daverden, who celebrated his first victory last weekend.
With this game, the season begins anew for us.
"I hope that we finally have our head clear," Ehler's behavious is optimistic.
With the exception of Hendrik Blohme, he finally has his entire squad at his disposal.
Daverden, like us, also relies on tempo play.
Therefore, we must keep our error rate low.
First and foremost, however, it is important to increase in the coverage, which has recently left anything but a certain impression.
Daverden's coach Thomas Panitz is much more relaxed after the first win of the season against Nordhorn.
The game, of course, was a house number.
But that's why we're far from the favorite, because anything can always happen in the Derby.
Nevertheless, Panitz now wants to follow-up in Morsum to start a series.
Jan-Malte Jodat will be missing.
The A-juvenile Joost Windßuß, who is active with double-playing rights at the A-Jugend-Bundesligist HC Bremen, celebrates his season debut.
If you’ren’t hot in a game like this, you should stay at home.
Halloween 2013: In numbers
When I was a kid, Halloween had something magical.
My sister and I were allowed to eat sweets, stay up late and dress up for the neighborhood.
Today I've become more a Halloween muffle.
In the last two years, I haven't agreed to distribute sweets from my apartment, and probably won't do it this year either.
But according to statistics, I'm a black sheep when it comes to Halloween.
The majority of Americans – 158 million, to be precise – celebrate Halloween this year and spend a total of US$6.9 billion for candy, costumes and decorations, according to the U.S. Association of Retailers (NRF).
One thing I look forward to every Halloween are the trend values.
Costumes will make up about 1.2 billion of the US$6.9 billion, according to the NRF.
This year, inciting inanimated objects are the last cry.
Women no longer have to wrap themselves in sexy clothes, they can now also embody seductive food such as pizza, hamburgers or carrots.
As for men, I'm expecting plenty of zombies thanks to The Walking Dead, and I bet the daft punk spacemen will make it into the Instagram feeds this year.
According to Google, the most wanted costumes are zombies, Batman, pirates and witches.
I'm guessing there's nothing wrong with tradition.
We dressed up our dogs last year and weren't alone to my astonishment.
In fact, Americans are spending 330 million dollars this year on costumes of their pets, according to the NRF.
That’s a lot of “hot dogs.”
As for sweets, we'ren't squeamish.
Americans will spend 1.9 billion dollars on it this year, according to The Nielsen Company.
That's about 270 million kilograms of candy bars, lollipops, candies and gummy bears.
So good news for the 41 million people, according to the U.S. Department of Commerce, who move through the neighborhoods with the saying “sweet or sours”.
In fact, we’re going to buy over 40 million kilograms of chocolate for Halloween and – let’s not kid – eat.
The only thing we don't want to consume is sugar-sweet Candy Corn; nevertheless, 15 million kilograms of it will be sold around Halloween, according to the National Confectionery Manufacturers Association.
That's about nine billion corn grains.
It's a mystery I haven't been able to solve yet.
Nothing belongs more to Halloween than houses where it haunts.
They have great names such as “Terror Behind the Walls”, “Howl-O-Scream” and “The House of Shock”.
There are actually 1,200 officially recognized haunted houses in the United States, which generate 500 million dollars in proceeds, according to America Haunts, and this includes the wonderful photos, in which you pee in your pants and then put the friends on Facebook, where you don’t get rid of them again, and then the guy you’re into leaves a comment like “nice facial expression”.
But let's finally get to the pumpkips.
Charlie Brown familiarized us with the big pumpkin when we were kids, and carving a pumpkin lantern is like decorating the Christmas tree – something we’ve done from an early age.
Luckily, the “baby in the pumpkin” trend only started last year thanks to Pinterest, so most of us grew up carving the pumpkin and not sitting in it.
This year, Americans will spend about 106 million dollars on pumpkins, according to the U.S. Census Bureau.
The pumpkin lantern, which slowly rots in the front yard, is believed to come from Illinois, where 245 million kilograms of pumpkins were harvested this year.
If you want to score correctly, then call Tim and Susan Mathisdon in Napa, California, carving something pretty out of their 921-kilo pumpkin.
Apartment fire in Helmbrechts: Rescue workers recover charred body
The fire brigade had to disengage in Helmbrechts late Thursday night.
A three-family house in the district of Hof catches fire at night to Friday.
The rescue workers retrieve a charred corpse from the house.
Rescue workers have found a charred corpse in a three-family house during a fire in Helmbrechts.
Whether it was a resident is still unclear, as a spokesman for the police in Bayreuth said.
By late Thursday night, it had burned on the ground floor of the house.
By the time firefighters arrived, the flames were already pounding out of a window.
Firefighters went to the apartment and found the body in a room.
This and another room burned out completely.
The entire ground floor was damaged.
All other residents weren't at home during the blaze, the police spokesman further explained.
The building is no longer inhabitable for the time being.
The exact cause of the fire was not clear at first.
Germany's largest state church has launched an offensive to promote the profession of pastor.
The background is a possible shortage of pastors: The Evangelical Lutheran Church in Hanover expects that the number of its currently around 1,800 pastors will halve according to the current situation by 2030.
For young people today, the profession is very attractive and offers good prospects for the future, said pastor Mathis Burfien (43) in an interview with the evangelical press service.
It is attractive to be able to determine the work and daily routine itself.
For the first time, Burfien has commissioned a pastor on a full position to inspire young people to study theology.
At present, fewer and fewer young people decide to study theology after graduation.
Burfien attributes this to the process of dechoralization: "God's voice is quiet, the world is noisy".
The profession is characterized by great freedom and diversity.
I am master of my schedule and can set my priorities where it is important to me.
Pastors, as chaplains, could be very close to people.
They earned as much as teachers and could make a good living out of it.
Surely in the free economy more is paid, but theologians have a safe employer for it.
This suits the young people of today, who are not only interested in careers, but a meaningful job.
According to the state church, which includes three-quarters of Lower Saxony, around 60 pastors are currently retiring each year.
At the same time, about 40 graduates of a theology studies begin their vicariate.
In sparsely populated areas such as the Harz, Emsland or Wendland it could be difficult to fill positions in the future.
Burfien intends, among other things, to organize study days for young people and also invite celebrities who have studied theology.
The theology studies provide a broad education.
You can even become federal president with it.
Land Rover Rally Series Announced
In the interior there are racing seats and six-point belts as well as a hands-free system.
Options range from increased brakes, a service package with support of bowler-works mechanics and logistical support to vehicle storage between events.
Drew Bowler, managing director of Bowler Motorsport, explains: “The rally customers who come to bowlers have changed.”
They're not all experienced racers, they're people looking for suspense and adventure, as well as an achievable path to world-class events.
We are delighted to open this path in partnership with Land Rover and the MSA and believe that the format opens up new opportunities to experience the various rally disciplines in the UK and abroad and prepare newcomers to Rally Raid’s hardships and reality.
We’ve really had fun developing the Defender Challenge car – it’s going to be an entertaining championship.
In addition, at the Defender Challenge there is a training and testing day in February as well as the option to participate in desert races in North Africa and the Middle East.
Firefighting called to the rescue of a pooch stuck 15 meters above the ground on a dangerous rock outcrop in a quarry
Cockerspaniel bitch Ruby had run away after a minor road accident
Three days later, she was spotted by a walker in the quarry in her predicament
Firefighters roped off at rock face and rescued the dog from certain death
The fire brigade managed to bring a pooch to safety, which was stuck on a 15-meter-high protrusion in a rock wall.
The nine-month-old Cockerspaniel bitch Ruby had run away after a traffic accident on Sunday afternoon and survived alone for three days before she was rescued from a quarry on Wednesday.
Scott Alderson, 25, and his girlfriend Becky Hall, 20, who own the dog, arrived at Flappit quarry in Denholme, West Yorkshire, to welcome Ruby back and say thanks to West Yorkshire Fire and Rescue Service firefighters.
They had desperately searched for their missing bitch and posted calls for help on social networks when she escaped into the quarry after the minor traffic accident.
Around 2:15 p.m. on Wednesday, a walker carrying out his dog saw the stranded Ruby on the 15-meter-high heel in the quarry.
The Cleckheaton Fire Station technical rescue team rushed to the spot and roped himself off to Ruby's rescue, which was then transported upwards in a dog box.
Andy Clayton, specialist in the technical rescue team, said: “She was in a dangerous situation.
It was located in the middle of the rock wall – 15 meters from above and the ground.
She didn’t move a muscle during her rescue – she was frozen stiff.
But she's okay now.
She then ate a few dog cakes afterwards.
That was quite an unusual effort.
The fact that the dog has been discovered is unbelievable.
Peter Lau, a specialist in the technical rescue team, said: “Ruby was very lucky.”
There was the possibility that she was very badly injured or worse.
Ruby was taken to veterinarian for examination, but he found nothing but exhaustion and dehydration.
Becky Hall from Halifax, West Yorkshire, said, “Watching the rescue was scary.”
I couldn't believe she was really up there.
It was wonderful to hold them in their arms again.
The veterinarian explained that if the exhaustion had become too great and it had collapsed, it would presumably have crashed.
Firefighters did great things.
It was really courageous what they did.
We're just so grateful and each of them has been absolutely incredible.
Scott Alderson of Keighley added, “We were afraid she would fall down, but she stayed up.”
Firefighters were awesome.
I can't believe where she was.
Mick Jagger explains that he never turned on Katy Perry when she was 18.
During an interview with an Australian radio station this week, the pop star said she had collaborated as a background singer on Jagger’s song “Old Habits Die Hard” from 2004.
Perry explained that she ate dinner with the alt rocker, and that “he dredged me when I was 18.”
She added, “That’s a long time ago and he’s been very nice to me.”
In a statement, a representative of Jaggers, 70, said on Thursday that he “categorically denies that he had ever made attempts at rapprochement with Katy Perry.”
“Maybe she confused him with someone else.”
Perry was one of the singers who made a guest appearance on this year's Rolling Stones tour.
Her new album “Prism” immediately landed at #1 this week.
George Kerevan: European unbundling gives Scots a choice
A new day, a new horror story about independence.
This time, we are warned that an independent Scotland is needed to join the Schengen area as a condition for EU membership.
This provides the keyword for stories about passport checks in Berwick and a barbed-wire-secured boundary along Hadrian's wall.
In fact, the Strathclyde template pointed to the possible economic benefits that more freedom of travel in the restant of Europe would bring, but – as expected – this did not play a role in the headlines.
Nor has anyone mentioned that EU member states often bend their strict rules if it is cheaper for them.
Since Scotland is not currently part of the Schengen area, continued non-compliance for Brussels would only be a small concession in return for what you really want from the Scots.
So actually not a story.
And one that has come in such a year that it already resembles a fossil: For the first time I heard the “independence means passport controls” duck at least 40 years ago.
An interesting point in the constant repetition of this ancient history is lost.
What's what's to expect an independent Scotland to dance after the European whistle?
What's the London yoke to exchange for that of Brussels, especially now?
This is the real European news: the great plan for unifying Europe made after the war has stalled.
With the euro crisis, the Europe project officially died.
Everywhere in the EU, parties are gaining ground who are hostile to the EU or who want to throw overboard the euro as a single currency.
Even in Germany, the Eurosceptian party Alternative for Germany – founded only this year – received just under five million votes in the federal election in September and thus effectively expel the Free Democrats (the equivalent to the British Liberal Democrats) from the Bundestag.
There has always been domestic opposition to the plan to create a federalist Europe.
However, the current economic crisis turns out to be a turning point.
Due to the strict austerity policy imposed by Berlin and the European Central Bank in connection with the straijacket, into which the respective national economy is coerced by adhering to the common currency, many people are of the opinion that the Europe project has gone too far.
The crisis of the euro has little to do with the fact that national governments are causing excessive budget deficits – this was only the case in Greece.
Instead, the Eurosystem has nailed its members on exchange rates that are beneficial for German exporters – and that’s what German politicians would like to leave.
Without the possibility of devaluation of the local currency, southern Europe with a built-in productivity disadvantage against Germany can be found.
The only option is to cut wages and public spending – spurred by Berlin.
Behind the current budget and currency problems lies a deeper European productivity problem.
As a result of the “green” energy directives prescribed by Brussels – a code for the subsidisation of French and German energy companies at the expense of consumers – European industry pays twice for electricity and four-fold for gas compared with the United States.
That's a crippling cost-disadvance, as we've already seen in Grangemouth.
All of the world's wage fixed-states do not protect the European petrochemical industry from being cornered by cheap American shale gas.
As a result, a revolt draws up, especially in France, the former cheerleader of the EU.
After the war, France's political elite saw the EU as a vehicle with which one could keep Germany at chess and lift Paris on an equal footing with Washington.
But Berlin no longer needs Paris as a gateway to political legitimacy and has impressed Europe's own economic policy, with which the ailing French economy struggles.
The result: Marine Le Pen's right-winning, anti-EU Front National has just won a decisive by-election, pointing the ruling Socialists to third place.
The Front National is now France's most popular party with 24 per cent of the electoral vote - a timely warning for Britain's Labour party that it can't assume that a split on the right spectrum would automatically mean benefits for the left.
What does Le Pen do with their newfound popularity among the working-class white French?
She wants to use the EU elections next year to forge an anti-EU, anti-communal currency block in the European Parliament.
If the anti-EU parties do well in these elections – and this is very possible – then for the first time such a bloc could dominate the European Parliament.
This is the point I would like: in the not-too-distant future, growing resentment against the EU and the common currency will come together to abolish the euro.
The EU will not disappear, but it will rather become something like the “Europe of (sovereign) nations”, which General de Gaulle favored.
Germany and a few of its satellite economies may be sticking to the euro, but France and southern Europe will return to their own currencies.
I expect Britain to distance itself from this project in the hopes of flattering with the U.S.
However, Washington's growing interest in the Pacific space suggests that Britain will stand alone in the Atlantic rain.
Where's Scotland with all of this?
We can decide to be a region of (in the last consequence) Little England.
Or we can defend our own economic interests – which includes telling Berlin and Brussels what they can put where.
I suspect Scotland could drive quite well in a looser European network, provided we keep our own currency.
Cooperation with other similar countries is easier in an unfederated Europe of nations.
Otherwise, we should consider doing the same to Norway and retaining our economic independence.
The SNP government in Scotland is – remarkably – the most successful political movement against austerity in Europe and gained a spectacular majority in 2011 due to the rejection of proposed (and undertaken) cuts that Labour Finance Minister Alistair Darling and the subsequent Tory-Lib Dem coalition were responsible for.
It would be ridiculous for Scotland to opt for independence now, only to then be given an austerity by Berlin and Brussels.
Early pubertity: Growing up faster
Afro-American and Hispanic girls tend to achieve puberty rather than their white old-age comrades, research shows.
Bodily changes don’t immediately mean puberty
There's no evidence that hormones or other chemicals are responsible
Experts believe epidemic obesity is a trigger of early puberty
The trend towards early puberty is less pronounced among boys
Former CNN correspondent Pat Etheridge is a journalist who specializes in children's health and family issues.
Should a mother be worried if her daughter grow breasts and pubic hairs at the age of seven or eight?
At this week's annual American Academy of Pediatric conference in Orlando, Florida, pediatric endocrinologist Dr Paul Kaplowitz explained that these early bodily changes were relatively normal among American girls and represented a new norm.
“I spend a lot of time reassuring parents – usually there’s no sympond that puberty is occurring quickly,” Kaplowitz explained.
Obvious indications of the development, such as growing breasts, shame and armpit hair as well as body odor, are apparent in girls earlier.
Nevertheless, there has been only a slight shift at the age of menarche (occurrence of the first menstrual bleeding) in the last 40 years.
In the United States, the average age is 12.5 years, slightly less than 12.75 in 1970.
“When the development of the breasts begins, it still takes at least two to three years right up to the menarche,” Kaplowitz, also author of the book “Early Puberty in Girls: the essential guide to dealing with this common problem.”
Time is the most accurate test on how puberty progresses.
It’s controversial, which actually represents the onset of puberty, but it’s “precocious” when breast augmentation is accompanied by a growth spurt before the age of eight.
In most cases, the process slows down or stagnates – something a pediatrician can monitor closely.
In the event of rapid progress, tests could be indicated by an endicronologist so that serious problems such as tumors or cysts can be ruled out.
There are treatment methods for delaying early menstruation and resisting another consequence: the premature aging of bones, which can ultimately lead to growth inhibition and thus small body growth in adults.
Recommendations for drug or hormonal therapy are based on the age of the child, the rate of development, the rate of growth and emotional maturity.
Psychosocial aspects are also important.
Kaplowitz is reluctant when it comes to medication, but he admits: “Supressive puberty can alleviate behavioral problems and the feelings of girls, unlike being their peers.”
The other big problem is understandable: Parents just don't want their very young daughters to have the period.
“They’re worried because of the risk of pregnancy or how they handle the hygienic side,” Kaplowitz said.
"It was a shock," recalls a woman whose daughter got her days at ten.
Even if there were initial signs and we had talked about menstruation, she wasn't emotionally prepared.
She came frightened and excitedly home from school because she was the first in her circle of friends.
There are many extensively published theories about the causes of early puberty.
However, there is no conclusive evidence that hormones in milk or other foods, chemicals in the environment or sexual messages in the media would be cautious.
Boys, much like girls used to come into puberty.
Kaplowitz believes that the most convincing premise is the epidemic of obesity.
He assisted in conducting a study in 2001 on girls aged six to nine, which establishes a link between body fat and entry into puberty.
Other research supports this conclusion, but there are many other factors that played a role.
In this country, African-American and Hispanic girls tend to achieve puberty rather than their white comrades.
There are different explanations for this.
Globally, early puberty seems influenced by all sorts of things, from economic conditions to the climate to the genes.
Another mystery: Although boys have grown the facial and pubic hair earlier, the trend towards a complete earlier puberty is not as pronounced as in girls.
Other physicians at the AAP conference underscored the complexity of the issue.
The appearance of acne and pubic hair is widespread even in babies and toddlers.
"We need to be careful how we identify the true beginning of puberty," said Dr. Lawrence Silverman, a pediatric endocrinologist at Goryeb Children's Hospital in Morristown, New Jersey.
Parents shouldn’t hesitate to seek advice from their pediatrician on how to talk to their child.
"This can mean that you have to have a conversation sooner than expected," Kaplowitz advised.
If you stay calm, the child usually reacts well.
Girls blooming early need the assurance that the process is a normal part of life, even if it occurs sooner than thought.
Jumbo manufacturers argue over seat width in the face of large orders
There has been an altercation between leading aircraft manufacturers about the breadth of seats in the tourist class on long-haul flights, indicating the tone for a bitter clash at the Dubai Airshow this month.
The dispute revolves around the seat width offered to passengers on longthawing flights in the tourist class – not always the ones that airlines strive for the most, but this designated space represents the key to the efficiency claims on the latest Airbus SAS and Boeing Co aircraft.
Airbus this week called for an industry standard that dictates a seat width of at least 46 cm (18 inches) in the tourist class, but U.S. arcrival Boeing says airlines should decide that.
The dispute comes at a time when aircraft builders are vying to sell even larger versions of their twin-engine long-haule aircraft, and are expecting record orders at the November 17-21 event.
How the rear part of the aircraft is designed – in particular whether there are rows with nine or ten seats – is crucial for the stated economic results that the new “mini-jumbo” design is supposed to deliver.
Boeing claims its modernized “777X” holds 406 people in seats in the tourist class, which are over 17 inches wide, allowing ten per row.
Airbus declares that the competing version of the A350 carries 350 people in 18-inch wide seats in the tourist class, with nine per row.
Aircraft giants often hand out slashes about technical details by means of advertising in the trade press.
Now Airbus is appealing directly to the view of the Dubai Airshow, where the 777X with over 100 orders is expected to make the race.
Recently, the company presented an ad that could be the start of a new ad war and showed donors a picture in which three people squeezed together in a restaurant, the title: “Would you accept that?”
“Boeing is proposing long-haul flights in seats that are tighter than regional turbo-props,” says Airbus sales chief John Leahy.
In light of changing dietary habits, people are getting thicker, but airplane seats haven’t changed radically.
Between the early 1970s, when the Boeing 747 defined modern long-haul travel, and at the end of the century, the weight of the male average American has increased by 10 percent between the ages of 40 and 49, according to U.S. Department of Health.
According to U.S. health statistics, the waist circumference of the male average American in the 21st century is 10.8 cm.
Airbus says its rival adheres to a seating concept from the 1950s, when the average body circumference of the freshly baptized “jet set” was narrower.
According to Airbus, investigations have been carried out to prove that an additional inch (2.54 cm) per seat increases the quality of sleep by 53 percent.
Boeing denies Airbus's figures on the seat dimensions and says it is not at manufacturers' discretion to decide how airlines shape the balance between air fares and facilities.
The company also explains, according to investigations, the experience on the plane depends on more than the seat width.
“It really boils down to giving airlines flexibility so they can do what they think they’re trying to do in order to be successful,” said Kent Craver.
They don't want us to dictate to them what makes them profitable.
They know their business better than anyone else.
For passengers, it's about more elbow clearance, but it's becominging an increasing issue for manufacturers that can impact sales.
Behind the dispute is a race for aircraft orders with an estimated volume of at least 700 billion US dollars by list prices in the coming decades, which could have a lasting impact on the export balance sheet of the USA or Europe.
As Reuters first reported in July, the seat layout is the driving force behind the dispute over the new jets.
Both Airbus and Boieg claim 20 percent more efficiency per seat with their latest twin-engine long-haul designs than the market leader in this segment, the 365-seater Boeing 77-300ER.
Boeing's statements about the results depend in part on the 777X, with its ten seats per row, compared with the original design of nine seats in the 777.
The increased cost per unit is still lower than with currently used machines with ten seats per row.
“That’s why Boeing is squeezing more seats into his aircraft to make it more competitive into our products,” says Kevin Keniston, head of passenger comfort at Airbus.
On the other hand, analysts, full cabins with ten seats per row at existing 777s indicated that many passengers opted for the tighter arrangement, which could come with cheaper fares.
“Eighteen inch seat width would be great for passengers, but the reality is: from an economic point of way point of view, the Airbus offer is controlled by the threat posed by the 777,” says Mary Kirby, an expert in cabin facility, founder and editor of Runway Girl Network.
Airbus and Boeing don't offer seats, but a catalog of suppliers that airlines can choose from.
The world-riding jet sellers even carry tape size with them to measure the set-up at the competition.
Even if they promote comfort, all manufacturers also offer jets with a high-density design for low-cost airlines and regional flights.
Airbus offers an A350 with ten-seater rows, but hasn’t sold one yet.
Until recently, Airbus has stressed the importance of more customizability of the cabins and offered wide seats at the aisles for some jets.
Excluding the support of the only other manufacturer of major modern jets, experts say the call for a new industry standard will presumably fizzle out, but could be distracted by the wave of 777X sales.
New anti-nicotine vaccine could eliminate the pleasure of smoking
Scientists have developed an anti-nicotine vaccine that could spoil the fun of smoking.
A single dose of the vaccine could protect mice from nicotine addiction for a lifetime.
Further experiments are needed before human tests, which can take a few more years, but Professor Ronald Crystal of Weill Cornell Medical College in New York said the initial results were good.
“We are very hopeful that this type of vaccination strategy can once help millions of smokers who have tried to quit and have tried all the methods available on the market, but had to experience that their nicotine addiction is stronger than these approaches,” Cornell said.
The new vaccine contains a harmless virus that has been altered in such a way that it contains the genetic information to produce nicotine antibodies.
The virus selectively infects the liver cells, which then produce a constant stream of antibodies.
The antibodies hunt the nicotine molecules in the blood and neutralize them before they reach the brain, so that a smoker does not experience a nicotine high.
In trials, vaccinated mice who were given nicotine maintained their normal activity levels.
Mice without vaccination, on the other hand, would have taken a “time-out,” the researchers explained, a sign that the nicotine had reached her brain.
The experiments are described in the journal Science Translational Medicine.
Former tobacco vaccines failed because they contained antibodies.
Vaccinations had to be given so often in order to maintain antibody levels that they were expensive and impractical.
But the cost of the new vaccine is likely to be significantly lower because it turns liver cells into antibody factories.
As Professor Crystal said, a future vaccine for people, if it's quite surely, could be given to children before they were tempted to try a cigarette, thus preventing nicotine addiction.
It is more likely that smokers would use it to stop.
“They then know that when they start smoking again, they no longer feel pleasure because of the nicotine vaccine, and this helps them to put off the habit,” he explains.
British scientists said the results were interesting, but at the same time warned that further research was still needed.
Tripodi denies having been influenced by Obeid
An investigation will be launched against the former minister of the laboratory party Joe Tripodi from New South Wales by the state corruption oversight.
The former NSW minister denies having changed Maritim lease regulations at the request of his political mentor Eddie Obeid, who owned covert holdings of three properties on state-controlled land.
The Independent Commission Against Corruption (ICAC­ Independent Commission against Corruption) on Friday extended its investigations into whether Obeid has influenced various ministers of state to extend leases on Circular Quay, where the Obeids operated two restaurants and a café, without a tender after their expiry in August 2005.
Now it is being investigated because of the accusation that Tripodi had known of Obeid's covert interest in the properties after Tripodi's former deputy chief of staff Lynne Ashpole testified accordingly on Thursday.
In years of talks that began in 2005, the government had urged public tenders for the leases.
The tenants were against it and also wanted longer terms.
In 2009, the leases were extended without a public tenderince to the companies on Circular Quay, which brought in the Obeids 2.5 million dollars annually.
Tripodi, who was port minister from February 2006 to November 2009, had originally spoken out in favour of public tenders.
However, he denied having made the changes at Obeid's request, which, as Tripodi confirmed, had urged a reorganization of the government's lease settlements.
A telephone transcript submitted to the ICAC showed conversations dating from August and September 2007 between Obeid, Tripodi and Steve Dunn, a senior official who had moved to the port ministry after working under Obeid at the Fisheries Authority.
“Was this issue discussed in these telephone conversations the elaboration of commercial leasing arrangements?” asked Deputy Commissioner Anthony Whealy Tripodi.
“No,” Tripodi replied.
I can't remember what was being discussed, but it definitely wasn't.
Definitely not between me and Mr Obeid.
Israeli warplanes attack target in Syria, according to official sources
Israeli fighter jetties had attacked a target in the Syrian port of Latakia on Thursday night, a senior government official told Fox News.
The officer did not elaborate on what the target had been, but said it had been at least one.
According to Associated Press, the target was Russian SA-125 missiles.
At least twice this year, Israel has already carried out air strikes on missile shipments in Syria.
Foreign workers with 457 visas could be subjected to ‘aptitude test’
As part of extended measures, the government is considering an “appropriatement test” for foreign workers with 457 visas.
If the test were to be introduced, it would be used via a criterion designed to prevail that the 457 will be used to fill unqualified workplaces or as a back door for inflicting family and friends into Australia.
Today, a discussion proposal from the government was published, after the former member of the Labor Party, Maxine McKew, condemned the government's comments regarding foreign workers, because these could be consummed as an insult by Australia's neighbors.
“Loud declarations such as ‘foreigners are supposed to line up at the rise’ and ‘Australians hire first’ are a very unpleasant relapse in times when unions called for a protected labour market,” she told the Australia India Institute today.
Historically, this meant that white workers should be protected – and if some in the region seized an echo of this historical artifact, I wouldn’t be surprised.
The discussion proposal outlines twelve measures once envisioned by former Immigration Minister Chris Bowen.
Immigration Minister Brendan O'Connor, who stayed yesterday for talks on people smuggling in Sri Lanka, has implemented five of the recommended changes, the others are still being considered.
If the “Criterion of Authenticity” is taken over, then someone applying for a visa could be checked whether “the application is genuine in cases where the applicant is related to or personally related to the holder or a relevant person of the supporting entity.”
Firms could also be required to account for the number of 457 visa holders, having finally hired hundreds in the past companies that initially wanted to support only a small number of workers.
Meanwhile, a 35-year-old sri-Lankian asylum seeker presumably died of a heart attack after arriving on Christmas Island this week with a refugee boat.
The troubled nine-year-old son of the man, who had come to Australia with him, has been cared for since his father's death on Wednesday by an adult cousin who was also on the boat.
Australian authorities took the man to Christmas Island Hospital where he died.
A study has been announced by the British government to increase the benefits of the HS2 railway project for Scotland.
The work of HS2 Ltd proposes that high-speed links between Scotland and the north of England should be realized at the beginning of phase 1 in 2026.
Transport Minister Baroness Kramer said the project "will bring Britain closer together."
Scottish Traffic Minister Keith Brown said he was very pleased to be working with the UK government on this plan.
Phase 1 consists of a new high-speed rail connection between London and the West Midlands.
After completion of Phase 2, there will be connections to Manchester and Leedes.
In June, the government corrected the estimated cost of constructing the high-speed road between London and northern England from 32.7bn pounds to 42.6bn pounds.
The British government, which is in talks with Transport Scotland, has instructed HS2 Ltd to look for further trail capacity and travel time improvements for Northern England and Scotland.
This includes the possibility of a travel time of ultimately three hours or less from Glasgow and Edinburgh to London.
Baroness Kramer said: “Our destination for HS2 is a truly national routing network that brings Britain and its cities closer together.”
We're going ahead with HS2 because it brings enormous benefits.
Without it, we are facing a capacity crisis in our railway network.
Yet it's also about the connection between 18 British cities, including Glasgow and Edinburgh, which will be improved thanks to HS2.
Scottish State Secretary Alistair Carmichael commented: "Today's announcement is good news for Scotland."
On behalf of the Scottish Government, Ketek Brown urged Carmichael to "unequivocally" advocate Scottish involvement in the HS2 network.
Brown said: “The high-speed rail line holds the potential of enormous economic benefits for Scotland, but at the same time Scotland’s economic power also provides an argument for fast rail connections across the UK.”
That's why we're pleased to work as partners of the UK government and examine the options for a high-speed rail connection in Scotland, which creates benefits for everyone and complement the Glasgow-Edinburgh joiner that the Scottish Government is already planning.
I look forward to examining the investigation report with the British ministers next year and jointly adopting the next steps.
Regulations on electronic devices in aircraft remain provisionally in force in Australia
Australian air passengers must continue to shut down tablets and smartphones during take-off and landing, despite efforts in the U.S. to loosen regulations for such devices.
The U.S. Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) allows U.S. airlines to change their requirements, allowing passengers to read e-books, watch or play e-books on their devices during critical flight phases if they are in “flight mode.”
During the majority of the flight, passengers are already allowed to do so, but many people find it annoying that they are not allowed to read their e-books at take-off and landing.
However, Australian companies are observing the decision that U.S. airlines have to do a lot of work on, but are not pursuing any immediate plans to change their regulations.
CASA also stated that it would look at the announcement, but stressed that the restrictions on the use of electronic devices in critical flight phases in Australia still apply.
“CASA does not currently have any specific regulations regarding the use of electronic equipment in the aircraft,” it said in a statement.
The issue is subject to provisions requiring airlines to maintain safety at all times and passengers to comply with the safety instructions provided by cabin staff.
Virgin has already held talks with CASA about an expansion of use of its Wi-Fi entertainment system on flights and stated that it is open to changes, but these must be initiated by the legislature.
“We would welcome a review of authorization of electronic devices by CASA because we are convinced that this would improve the customer experience, now that we are offering (wireless entertainment) during the flight,” a spokesperson said.
Qantas said it would stick with the current regulations until further notice.
“Our current guidelines envisage that electronic devices must not be used at take-off and landing, and we don’t currently have plans to change that,” the company said.
FAA regulations apply to U.S. airlines.
However, we are always interested in developments in regulations from which passengers can benefit, and will certainly take a close look at the FAA decision and the reasons behind it.
The decision has a different effect on U.S. companies depending on the age of the fleet.
You need to prove that your aircraft can tolerate the radio interference by mobile devices, and change manuals, training materials, carry-ond baggage programs, and customer briefing.
“Having an airline has confirmed the tolerance of its fleet, it can allow passengers to use portable, lightweight electronic devices such as tablets, e-book readers and smartphones at any level,” the FAA said in a statement.
In rare cases in poor visibility, the crew will instruct the passengers to turn off the equipment during landing.
The group also recommended storing heavier devices safely under the seat or in the compartments above the seats upon take-off and landing.
Before the precinct derby against Schalke, BVB supporters with luminous rockets had caused ugly scenes in the Veltins Arena.
Borussia Dortmund subsequently announced harsh consequences.
Ahead of Friday's Bundesliga match against VfB Stuttgart, the ultras reacted with a silence - at first.
It was an unfamiliar, almost ghostly acoustic, which presented itself to the viewer until shortly before the kick-off of the Bundesliga match between the German runner-up Borussia Dortmund and the VfB Stuttgart at Signal Iduna Park.
Most of the guest fans could be heard with their chants.
The southern grandstand, on the other hand, where the most loyal - and loudest - BVB fans stand, initially presented itself exceptionally quietly: no chants, no battle calls.
The pedestal of Capo, which otherwise coordinates the joint cheering, remained unoccupied.
And at the very front in the grandstand, a large flag waving "Section Stadium Prohibition".
Only when goalkeeper Roman Weidenfeller, as always, became the first BVB player to enter the pitch, briefly blazed cheers.
As his team-mates followed, there was loud singing and waving scarves - but far from everywhere.
In the central blocks 12 and 13 of the south tribune, nothing stirred.
The fact that stadium spokesman Norbert Dickel listed once again the positive experiences of the last few days - derby victory, victory over Arsenal, contract extension of coach Jürgen Klopp - did not create a festive mood.
Only five minutes before kick-off, the Capo climbed its podium - and until then the tension, which until then had almost been graspable with hands, unloaded in a loud scream when Dickel, as usual, queried the mood on the ranks in the stadium.
As always, it was the turn of the southern grandstand - and finally the loudest place in the stadium was here again as usual.
And at the latest when with kick-off from the southern grandstand came the vociferous invitation "Derby winner stands up", everything was again as always.
The silence before was probably a reaction to the events of the past few days.
Immediately before Revierderby at FC Schalke 04, some of the BVB supporters had fired luminous rockets into the spectator ranks and onto the pitch - and almost met goalkeeper Roman Weidenfeller.
The game was lately kicked off because of the chaotic scenes.
BVB boss Hans-Joachim Watzke had then received the tops of the Dortmund ultras in his office - and it is believed Watzke's words, this was a rather uncomfortable date for the fan representatives.
"There will be massive cuts for the entire ultra-movement," the BVB boss proclaimed at a panel discussion in Frankfurt on Thursday evening.
As an immediate measure, Watzke prohibited the ultras from a choreography for the home game against VfB Stuttgart.
"That's what I've forbidden," he explained.
We can't give up the zero-tolerancing policy on pyrotechnics.
In addition, different possibilities of punishment have been shown to the Ultras - until Tuesday, they now have time to decide on one.
Otherwise, according to Watzke, the BVB will determine a sanction by the end of next week.
"This is going to be drastic," Watzke assured.
One can be curious how the reactions in the south grandstand will turn out.
Pawnshops are booming in Singapore as the middle-class crisis has arrived
In a pawn shop in Singapore's Bendemer shopping centre, Janani Amirthalinga exchanges a golden bangle, a ring and a pair of earrings to be able to pay for their daughters' school fees.
“My husband and I just bought a house, there’s all my money in it,” says Amirthalinga.
Even if she earns 3,000 Singapore dollars (about 1,730 euros) as an administrative employee every month and her husband also works, the monthly family income is not enough, she explains.
In factually, demand in parts of Southeast Asia – where household debt is increasing – is so great that ValueMax, where it made its exchange, this week became the third pawnhouse listed on the Singaporian stock exchange.
Pledging jewellery is not only a quick way to get money – 1,300 Singapore dollars in the case of Janani Amirthalinga – but also almost as favorable as an unsecured bank loan.
Typically, pawnshops in Singapore are demanding an annual annual interest rate of 17 percent, just above the 15.4 per cent demanded by United Overseas Bank, a local lender with a branch in the same mall.
Pfandhäuser, however, offer the advantage that there is no need to verify creditworthiness or proof of income, which is why loans are possible faster than with banks.
That's why millions of people in the region turn to pawnhouses after families feel the pressure from rising cost of living as well as increasing household and consumer debt.
After five years of robust growth since the global financial crisis and cheap loans due to a loose financial policy in the developed economic areas, lower- and middle-income families are picking up pawnshops in order to make up for the difference in stuttering the economy.
This week, rating agency Standard & Poor's described growing household debt, mainly due to rising mortgages, as a risk factor for the creditworthiness of Asian banks.
According to the agency, Malaysia, Thailand and Singapore have the highest budgetary debt in Asia in relation to gross domestic product.
Malaysia is at the top of the list with 80 percent of GDP, an increase of 60 percent in 2008.
Economists are also worried about Thailand's high level of consumer debt this week's narrowly come out of a technical recession.
On Thursday, the data showed a continuing weakness in exports and a slash of consumer demand.
“In the end, this means that in the face of rising costs, middle-to-long (the lower-income scale) people will try to supplement their income wherever possible,” says Song Seng Wun, an economist at CIMB, a Malaysian bank.
Historic highs in gold prices over the past two years have intensified the trend towards pledging personal belongings, as people take the opportunity to turn the family's jewelry into cash.
In Singapore, about 70 percent of the pledged items in the city-state's 200 pawnshops.
“The price of gold looks good, let’s plead Grandmother’s gold chain and get it back next month.”
The largest Thai pawn shop operator, EasyMoney, has experienced a 20 percent increase in customers in recent months in its stores.
The growth in the deposit business is so strong that ValueMax, operator of the Branch in Bendemeer and 15 others in Singapore, not only wants to expand into neighboring Malaysia – where the company operates four houses – but also outside Asia, according to Yeah Lee Ching, Managing Director of ValueMax.
The company finances this with 60 percent of the 66 million Singapore dollars, which it took this week with the IPO in Singapore.
While some lenders were caught in crossfire because of high interest rates, Yeah explains that pawnshops not only offer cheaper interest rates than other lenders, but also did not directly increase the debt.
“Customers don’t lend items that they already own, and liquefying personal valuables doesn’t enlarge household education,” she explains.
The pawnbroking business is increasingly accepted socially as short-term, secured financing.
Nor are all the people who use pawnbroken in financial trouble.
Rich people from Singapore also come to ValueMax's stores to plend gold bars and Rolex watches, for which they receive up to 60 per cent of the purchase price in cash.
Customers come to us from every social shift.
“These include the wealthy who borrow something for a business or investment in the short term, or small businesses that need to bridge bottlenecks in cash flow,” Yeah says.
Sometimes they just need money very quickly.
NSA extracts data from Google and Yahoo - Snowden wants to help
The intelligence revealer Edward Snowden has a fundamental interest in helping Germany to investigate the increasingly explosive NSA spying affair.
According to Hans-Christian Ströbele, a member of the Greens’ Bundestag, during his surprising meeting with Snowden in Russia, the conditions under which the ex-intelligence official would testify at a German public prosecutor’s office or before an investigative committee.
Snowden had referred to his complicated legal situation, Ströbele told the ARD magazine "Panorama".
Bailout over 325-million-dollar Australian dollars for Tasmanic healthcare
The Australian government insists on the tough conditions linked to the bailout over 325-million Australian dollars for Tasmanic healthcare to ensure that the state's government can't waste the money.
Federal Health Minister Tanya Plibersek has announced that the Commonwealth will take "immediately" measures to avert a crisis caused by an aging population of the island, a higher rate of chronic diseases and system restrictions.
Financing for a four-year period was decided after government deliberations with independent Tasmanian MP Andrew Wilkie.
“The government has put together an emergency bailout package that we believe is addressing the unique challenges facing the state,” Plibersek said.
The 325-million-dollar package includes 31 million dollars of emergency aid for electoral benefits.
A further 2600 surgeries, including orthopedic interventions and treatments due to grey cataract, are intended to eliminate residuences.
In addition, there is money for day clinics in Hobart and Launceston, better follow-up care after inpatient stays, specialist training, mental health services and the introduction of a system for the electronic recording of health data in local hospitals.
“These investments are in line with the ideas that affected clinicians told me were the best way to improve the Tasmanian health system,” said Tanya Plibersek.
The minister insisted on a strict reporting and accountability of the Tasmanian government.
The state would have to maintain the current level of funding in order to receive money from the Commonwealth and report monthly on where the additional funds were spent.
A three-member commission will be set up to ensure that the state provides health services as effectively and efficiently as possible.
As Wilkie explained today, the 325-million-dollar dollars would be of little use if “they aren’t accompanied by real reforms that put the public Tasmanic health system on a more sustainable course.”
Notwithstanding this, he praised the government for responding to his request for urgent help, which he first expressed to the prime minister in early May.
“I’m confident that federal aid will make a big contribution to taking the state’s public health system off the worry list,” Wilkie said.
According to the state's government, these electoral performances will largely make up for recent cuts.
But the health policy spokesman of the opposition at the federal level, Peter Dutton, is of the opinion that today's announcement is only a "pavement solution".
“We’re in today’s situation because the Labor state government withdrew 430 million dollars from the health care system,” he told ABC TV.
It cannot be that a state government pulls almost half a billion dollars out of the system and the Commonwealth puts 300 million into it and then pretend to be good news.
Dutton urged Plibersek to guarantee that not a single dollar of the bailout will be spent on additional bureaucracy.
Guillaume Nicloux's adaptation of Denis Diderot's novel impresses with exceptional production equipment and contemporary details, but is also heavier than necessary.
The plot, which takes place in France of the 1760s, tells the poignant story of Suzanne, a young nobile who is sent to the monastery by her family.
As she rebels, she is confronted with the extreme cruelty of an extremely sadistic superior and becomes the object of erotic fascination of another.
The film never slips into lustfulness or sensation – and that’s where his problem lies.
The solemn seriousness with which the film is told also makes it a tormentious penitentiary shirt for the audience.
Syria has destroyed its ability to produce chemical weapons, according to a surveillance group
Syria has destroyed important facilities for the production of chemical weapons and poison gas ammunition, the global monitoring group on chemical weapons said on Thursday, while heavy clashes occurred in the north of the country near one of the sites where alleged toxic substances are stored.
Also on Thursday, a Syrian activist group announced that more than 120,000 people had been killed almost three years ago.
The announcement by the Organization for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons (OPCW) arrived one day
1 November that the Hague-based Damascus had set to destroy or “inoperable” all facilities for the production of chemical weapons, including all machines for the mixing of chemicals into poison gas and the filling of ammunition.
Completing this operation, which basically represents the first stage of destruction, is an important milestone in an ambitious timetable aimed at destroying all Syria's chemical weapons by mid-2014.
The destruction of the facilities means that Syria can no longer produce new chemical weapons.
Damascus, however, still has to start destroying the existing weapons and inventories.
According to assumptions, there are about 1,000 tons of chemicals and weapons in the country, including mustard gas and the nerve gas sarin.
The announcement came at a time when fighting took place in the city of Safira on Thursday that experts say there is a production facility for chemical weapons as well as deposits here, according to Britain-based Syrian Observatory for Human Rights.
The activist grouple, persecuting the number of deaths via a network of activists in Syria, declared on Thursday 120,296 people had died.
Of these, 61,067 were civilians, including 6,365 children.
On the part of the government, 29,954 of them belonged to President Bashar Assad's army, 18,678 were pro-government fighters and 187 Lebanese Hezbollah militias.
Also among the dead are 2,202 deserted army soldiers and 5,375 fighters of the opposition, many of them foreigners.
On July 25, the U.N. estimated the number of deaths in that conflict since March 2011 at 100,000.
This figure hasn't been updated yet.
The conflict has forced about two million people to flee the country.
Assad's troops have been battling the rebels for weeks in Safira, many of whom have ties to al-Qaida groups.
The Observatory said there had been casualties on both sides on Thursday but did not give details.
The fighting underscores the danger faced by chemical weapons inspectors, because their mission to liberate Syria from its poisonous arsenal is a race against time in the midst of a raging civil war.
According to the OPCW, which works closely with the United Nations, the team is “now convinced that it has seen and witnessed all the important production and mixing/filling facilities mentioned by Syria and its destruction.”
Therefore “no further inspection activities are currently planned.”
Earlier in the week, inspectors had stated they had completed the first round of checks and visited 21 out of 23 of the sites specified by Damascus.
Due to safety concerns, it was not possible to visit two facilities, the inspectors said.
On Thursday, OPWC said the two sites “were abandoned according to the Syrian statement ... and the chemical weapons program objects there had been relocated to other mentioned facilities that were inspected.”
It wasn't immediately clear whether the site in Safira was one of the two facilities the OPCW inspectors couldn't visit.
Syria has put forward a plan for the complete destruction of its chemical weapons, which must be approved next month by OPWC's executive committee.
“I’m pulling my hat off to the bravery and courage you’ve all shown in fulfilling the most challenging mission ever carried out by this organization,” Ahmed Uzumcu, director-general of the monitoring organization, said in a commentation published by OPCW.
For three years now, the mainly Sunni Muslim rebels have been battling against Assad's government and its security forces, to which many members of the Alawites belong, a secession of Shiite Islam.
In view of further developments, the head of the observation group, Rami Abdurrahman, said there had been a strong explosion at an air defense facility in the Syrian coastal province of Latakia on Wednesday.
The cause of the explosion is not known, he said.
Wrath over verdict for Bali bombers
Survivorors and relatives of the 202 people who died in the Bali bombing in 2002, reacted angrily to the verdict for the last instigator who was brought to justice, since Umar Patek, in her view, was to be shoot by state law.
Patek, who spent just under ten years as one of Southeast Asia's most wanted persons, was sentenced to 20 years in prison yesterday for his role in the construction of explosive devices for the bombing.
With penalty suspension, he could be released after 15 years.
The 45-year-old was found guilty of the mass murder because of the attack on two nightclubs in Kuta's popular tourist district, in which 202 people died, including 88 Australians, and many more were injured.
There was also a guilty verdict in a number of terrorism-related charges, which included a wave of explosives attacks on churches in Indonesia on Christmas Eve in 2000.
Prosecutors had demanded a life sentence, even if she could have applied for the death penalty for the man who, because of his reputation as a master-employed bomb builder, also referred to as a “demolition man.”
The decision has aroused painful memories at June Corteen, a mother from Perth who lost her 39-year-old twish-daughter Jane and Jenny in devastation wrought by Patek and his co-conspirators nearly ten years ago.
Struggling with tears, she said Patek should have been sentenced to death.
I'm really saying he was supposed to follow in the footsteps of the other guys.
"You should put him in front of a firing squad," Corteen told AAP.
I have to live every day with the fact that I won't get any more grandchildren and I'll never see my daughters
The Sari Club was razed to the ground when, on October 12, 2002, shortly after 2 p.m., a huge bomb exploded in a small truck parked in front of the nightclub.
Peter Hughes was at Paddy's Bar, where just 20 seconds earlier a suicidal bomber exploded a backpack filled with explosives.
He fell into coma for a month after the attack and “died” three times as he hung on a life support system.
Hughes said Patek deserved the same fate as the three other members of Jemaah Islamiah – Amrozi, Mukhlas and Imam Samudra – who were responsible for the carnage and executed four years ago.
Really, this guy was supposed to get the death penalty in front of everyone else.
Leaving him alive—no, there’s no reason to keep him alive.
Getting 20 years after killing 202 people and injuring many hundreds isn't much.
Patek is the last of the Bali bombers to stand trial.
He evaded almost a decade of arrest, but was finally picked up in the Pakistani city of Abbottabad in January 2011, where U.S. forces killed former al-Qaeda chief Osama bin Laden nearly four months later.
During the trial, an FBI agent testified that intelligence reports had yielded the indication that Patek was located in Pakistan to meet bin Laden with the aim of rebuilding the connection between Southeast Asian terrorist groups and al-Qaeda.
"He didn't turn himself in," Corteen said.
Until recently, he has shown no remorse for how much suffering he has inflicted on other people.
The verdict was issued before the ten-year anniversary of this year's attack, which is being commemorated with commemorations in Bali and Australia.
"There will be a lot of tears this year," Corteen said.
Patek can still appeal against his verdict.
FAA: Passengers can now use electronic devices on aircraft (but don't conduct cell phone calls)
Air travelers can now use their electronic devices for reading, working, playing, listening to music and watching movies from departure to landing, but can't make phone calls, according to the new guidelines issued Thursday by the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) in the U.S.
However, passengers should not expect these changes to be implemented immediately.
How quickly they would be implemented depended on the respective airline, FAA employee Michael Huerta explained at a press conference.
Airlines must prove to the FAA that aircraft comply with the new guidelines and that they have updated flight staff training manuals, as well as the regulations for stowing the equipment in accordance with the new rules.
The FAA said it had already received plans from some airlines that would like to expand the use of wearable electronic devices on airplanes.
Among these airlines were Delta and JetBlue.
“Depending on the condition of the plan, we could approve the expanded use of electronic devices very soon,” the FAA said in a statement.
Currently, passengers will have to switch off smartphones, tablets and other devices when aircraft doors are closed.
They are not allowed to take them off again until the plane has reached an altitude of three kilometers and the captain gives the green light.
Upon entry into the landing approach, passengers have to switch them off again and are not allowed to take off again before the plane has landed.
According to the new guidelines, airlines whose planes are protected against electronic interference could allow their passengers to use the devices even on take-off, landing and on the tarmac, the FAA explained.
Most of the new commercial airplanes and other aviators that have been upgraded to use Wi-Fi at higher altitudes should meet the criteria.
Laura Glading, president of the professional association of professional flight attendants, welcomed the changes.
“If the new scheduling has been safely implemented – and we are working closely with the airline in this regard – both sides will benefit from this,” Glading commented.
We're honestly tired of having to feel like guard on this issue.
Nevertheless, Internet connections for surfing, exchanging e-mails, text messages or downloading data below three kilometers remained prohibited, the authority explained.
Passengers are encouraged to put smartphones, tablets and other devices into flight mode.
So still no “Words With Friends,” the Scrabble-liking online game that actor Alec Baldwin played on his smartphone in 2011 when he had to publicly leave an American Airlines jet because he refused to turn off the device while the plane was parked at the gate.
And heavier devices such as laptops must continue to be stowed away, as there is a risk of injury here if they fly through the cabin.
Mobile phone calls during the flight will also remain prohibited.
Regulatory literacy for telephone conversations lies with the Federal Communications Commission (FCC), not the FAA.
FAA may lift ban on certain electronic devices on take-off and landing
Last month, Mark Rosenker of the National Transportation Transportation Safety Board, safety expert on national traffic issues on CBS News said cell phones were still seen as a risk.
“Mobile phones are really a problem, not only because they could potentially cause interference with navigation devices, but because we from the FCC know that transmission from the air can also disrupt transmission masts,” Rosenker said.
An advisory committee of the industry, launched by the FAA to investigate this issue, last month recommended that the government allow a broader use of personal electronic devices.
In recent years, the pressure on the FAA to ease restrictions on use has increased.
Critics such as Democratic Senator Claire McCaskill of Missouri argue that there are no valid security reasons for the ban.
Restrictions are also increasingly difficult to enforce, because the use of the devices has become ubiquitous.
In some studies, there is talk that up to one-third of passengers forget or ignore the shutdown of the device.
The FAA began to restrict the use of electronic devices by air travelers in 1966, after reports of disruptions to navigation and communication devices had become known from passenger-carried FM radios, which at that time represented the latest technology trend.
New aircraft are far more dependent on electrical systems than previous generations of aerial vehicles, but they are also designed and approved by the FAA that they don’t mind electronic interference.
Airlines have been providing Wi-Fi to passengers for several years when altitude is reached.
The flies adapted to Wi-Fi systems are also insensitive to interference.
The vast majority of passenger planes should be suitable for the stronger use of electronic devices according to the new guidelines, Huerta said.
Today's electronic devices generally provide much less radio radiation than previous generations.
For example, e-readers emit only minimal radiation when turning.
But when downloading or sending data, the transmission values are stronger.
Advocates of an easing of restrictions on device use by passengers include Amazon.com.
In 2011, company employees loaded a passenger plane full of Kindle e-readers and made a test flight to determine problems, but couldn't detect any.
Members of the FAA advisory committee voiced mixed feelings about the risk of using equipment.
Douglas Kidd of the National Association of Airline Passengers said he felt the disruption caused by the devices was real, even if the risk was minimal.
Other committee members said there were only occasional reports of pilots speaking out in favor of a disruption of aircraft systems by the devices, and most of them were very old.
However, the FAA recommended allowing pilots to arrange the shut-off of the devices by the passengers under poor viewing conditions in the case of instrument landing.
A grouping from the travel industry welcomed the changes and described them as a sensible adjustment for travelers who would be extensively equipped with technology today.
“We are pleased that the FAA recognizes that a pleasant passenger experience is not at odds with safety,” said Roger Dow, CEO of the U.S. Travel Association.
Bird rescued by aerial rescue service from North Sea drilling insiland released again
A bird flown to the coast by air rescue service, which had been found exhausted on a North Sea drilling island, was discharged back into nature.
The water ralle was flown by helicopter to Aberdeen last month, where it was pampered by the Scottish animal welfare organisation SPCA at its rescue centre in Alloa.
Colin Seddon, head of the centre, said: “This water ralley was probably a wintry migratory bird from northern Europe that was stopped by the strong winds over the North Sea.”
Apparently, the exhausted bird sought refuge on the drilling rig.
He added, “He couldn’t keep flying, which is why we were contacted asking for help.”
When she was released, the water ralley was fit and healthy again.
Is Europe's elite ready to do business with Britain?
Business for Britain was launched in April with the intention of bringing the economy together and defining what those who ensure prosperity and work in the UK would like to change our relations with the EU.
To this end, we have commissioned the largest and most comprehensive survey of British business leaders and asked them what they think about Britain, the economy and the EU.
YouGov surveyed over 1,000 executives from the economy, which roughly map UK corporate sizes, sectors and regions.
The results of the survey will surprise many.
We've found that the vast majority of companies now export outside Europe and focus on countries that modernise and grow while states are stagnant in the EU.
They hope that new trade links with countries such as China, India and Brazil will be favoured by the government side, rather than dwelling on the long and exhausting process of reforming the obscure EU institutions.
Asked by their view on certain issues regulated by law – from antitrust law to product legislation – the majority of business leaders believe that control of these key competences should be brought back to Westminster.
There was general dissatisfaction about the internal market, as according to the companies, the costs of the Brussels regulations, meanwhile, would outweigh the benefits of being part of Europe’s trade zone – even 40 per cent of large corporations agreed to this, which are traditionally the most Europe-friendly.
After all, and that's presumably the most, our survey of business leaders revealed that a clear majority of Britain wants a course towards changing the agreement and a relationship with the EU based on trading isn't on politics.
This realization, found in companies of all sizes and leading corporations, shows that the economy is pushing for a “sensible change” that brings the booklet of action back to Britain.
It's about a lot - an amendment to the deal and better conditions for Britain would lead to a change of opinion in favour of remaining in the EU in a referendum at 16 per cent.
The Prime Minister should have no doubts: this poll shows that the British economy is behind its plan for renegotiating Britain's conditions for EU membership.
It also shows that the companies expect a renegotiation a significant shift in the balance of power back to the UK.
A better deal for the British economy is possible and increasingly necessary after the eurozone is now moving towards closer economic and fiscal union.
The priority must be jobs and growth in the UK, as the result of our survey shows, which means increased attention for companies on trade and a fundamental change in the Brussels approach to regulations.
Gazprom’s Alexei Miller describes pipeline in Bulgaria as the beginning of a new gas era
The start of construction of the South Stream pipeline in Bulgaria represents the launch of one of Europe’s largest energy projects, the Gazprom board said.
“Today’s event is a milestone: construction work has begun on the Bulgarian section of the South Stream gas pipeline, Europe’s largest and most important project,” Gazprom CEO Alexei Miller said in a statement on Thursday.
The project is a key element for the energy security of the entire European continent.
South Stream is expected to ensure greater diversity in Russia's export routes through Europe.
A treaty dispute between Gazprom and its counterparts in Ukraine, where the majority of Russia's gas is stored for Europe, provides an increased risk in conventional routes, as can be heard from the official side.
Miller said the direct connection to Bulgaria, a member of the European Union, meant that the geopolitical risks associated with transit countries were “forever” eliminated.
Bulgarian consumers will receive gas from South Stream at discounted tariffs if the entire project goes into operation in 2015.
According to Gazprom, construction work is scheduled to begin in other subordinated countries on the planned route at year-end.
The pipeline is designed to have an annual capacity of 62.3 billion cubic meters of natural gas.
For national and international investors, there is no way away from the real estate location Germany.
The economic framework data is right, and the financing options are excellent.
This has been tenor of the Börsen-Zeitung's 9th real estate day.
The German real estate markets benefit from the economic strength and the good cyclical conditions in the country in Europe.
Christian Ulbrich, CEO Europe, Middle East and Africa of Jones Lang LaSalle, pointed this out at the Börsen-Zeitung’s 9th real estate day.
According to Ulbrich, looking at the transaction volume shows how they are looking for German commercial properties.
In the first three quarters of this year, it increased by 31% compared to the previous year.
In Britain, the increase was only 6%, in France 19%.
"Real estate investments offer an attractive interest rate," Ulbrich said.
The yield gap between real estate and federal bonds is at a historically high level.
