Hearing before the Royal Commission: Two YMCA employees accused of sexual offenses against Jonathan Lord
Two YMCA employees from New South Wales (NSW) were charged with sexual offences in connection with children, before allegations were made in 2011 against the child care worker Jonathan Lord from Caringbah, as learned by the Royal Commission for child sexual abuse.
But at the opening statement before the commission, the YMCA had declared that “there has never been an incident of child sexual abuse in the organization.”
Managing director Phillip Hare was questioned about a case in which a YMCA employee had been indicted for misdemeanors with child pornography, and an other in which one physical education teacher from YMCA Caringbah Hall had been convicted in 1991 of the abuse of children entrusted to him.
Hare told Gail Furness, a commission-assistant lawyer that he had known about the first case but did not know the second.
He acknowledged that YMCA’s opening statement to the Commission was also inaccurately claimed that “there have been external checks carried out by the YMCA, in which the YMCA has been recognised at the forefront of child safety.”
Evidence before the Commission shows that the YMCA has been informed that it has received the second-lowest assessment of four possible ones in a Department of Education and Communicences quality audit in August of that year.
Hare, who joined the YMCA at 21, acknowledged that the management had failed “from my way down” when hiring Lord and failed to ensure that employees were aware of their obligations to report violations of child safety guidelines.
Lord had been convicted earlier this year of sex offenses against twelve boys during his two-year work for the YMCA.
He was sentenced to at least six years in prison.
Hare denied, however, that the YMCA had a cultural problem that had prevented employees from reporting Lord's violations regarding the safety of children.
Employees testified that they had witnessed violations, among other things that Lord was alone with children, cared for them privately as babysitters, let them sit on his lap, said to one that he loved it, and let children play with his mobile phone.
Danielle Ockwell, whose superior was Lord and who had asked for training on child protection because she was concerned about his conduct, testified that children’s service manager Jacqui Barnat, who was Lord’s superior at YMCA Caringbah, “was very intimidating and very unapproachable most of the time.”
The managing director stated that he would not accept statements from employees that they did not dare to report to their superiors.
Instead, he said, their friendship with Lord had impaired their judgment, which is why they did not report him.
Hare stated that he had communicated his view to the YMCA NSW’s board of directors that the lesson the organization had to draw from the “Jonathan-Lord incident” “doesn’t affect reporting” by employees, and the Board agreed to it.
Hare said the decision to urge employees to sign secrecy commitments shortly after the allegations occurred had been taken by YMCA's managing director of child services Liam Whitley.
He said that this was intended to prevent contamination of the evidence, but which had been “overzealous” and poorly implemented.
The YMCA NSW had not been a child-safe organization in the period when Jonathan Lord was employed there between 2009 and 2011, Professor Stephen Smallbone of Griffith University, an expert on child sexual abuse, told the commission.
He explained that there had been “serious problems” in hiring, reviewing, training, training and supervising employees.
The hearing was adjourned until December 20.
Tony Blair declared that he would seize the chance to return as British prime minister - but concedes that a comeback was unlikely.
In an interview tonight on the occasion of the fifth anniversary of his departure from office, the 59-year-old announced his views on various domestic political issues.
Since resignating in June 2007 after a decade as head of state, Blair has largely avoided commenting on British politics, and limiting his comments mostly to foreign policy and his role as envoy of the Quartet of Peacemakers in the Middle East.
Asked if he would like to return to prime minister's office, Blair was quoted as saying: "Yeah, for sure, but it's unlikely that to happen, so ..."
When the equine show experts gathered in droves in Cardiff to crown the horse of the year, they were aware that a fierce competition was imminent.
But no one was prepared for three-year-old Fenton Kirkland.
The Knirps, who isn’t even in kindergarten just a few months ago, and his Shetland pony toffee trotted loosely through the three laps, picking up the first prize – leaving 30 adult opponents behind.
The inseparable couple, both equal in size, were praised for their appearance, behaviour and stylism at the annual competition hosted by the Sunnybank Equestrian Centre in Rudry near Cardiff.
During his appearance in competition against men and women with elegant melons, he had put on his pork cap at a cheeky angle and led the two-year-old toffee through the ring.
Fenton was praised by the jurors for his natural skills in dealing with the pony, which was well above his age.
And Toffee received top marks for his demeanor and personality.
Fenton had received Toffee last March as a gift for his third birthday and has been practicing with the Shetland pony every day since.
His mother, Donna, 30, said: “Fenton and Toffee are a great team.”
They had to compete against the entire field of participants and have walked off the pitch with Gold Cup and Rosette.
It wasn't until the second time he'd competed with Toffee, and we've all been thrilled when he won.
Complete strangers in the arena considered him so phenomenal that they wanted to be photographed with him.
The little boy from Nantyglo village near Ebbw Vale, South Wales, follows in the footsteps of his aunt Sharon Howells, who has been performing horses for over ten years.
Howells said, “The whole audience was electrified and everyone cheered him on and clapped.”
He ran the entire length of the arena on sand and even though he looked so tiny, he did fantastic work.
Fenton is crazy about animals – he loves horses, tractors and farms and has two chickens he takes care of.
The way he’s started won’t be long before he’s on the “Horse of the Year” show – and I’m sure he’ll do well.
A spokesperson for the annual horse show said, “Fenton is only three, but he knows how to deal with his pony.”
Together, they're a great team.
The jurors rated Fenton and Toffee afterwards how well they would have beaten themselves and presented themselves in the screening ring.
They pay attention to good cooperation between pony and guide – and that’s where Fenton and Toffee were the best in the ring.
I'm sure Fenton's cute clothes played their part, that really fit the occasion perfectly.
Chinese newspaper to be 'reformed' after call
A Chinese newspaper that had demanded the release of a reporter who had been charged with defamation on the front page should be reformed, a press regulator explained.
Guangzhou-based Neue Express had printed a rare public call for the release of journalist Chen Yongzhou.
But Chen later admitted on television that he had accepted bribes to falsify reports about a part-state company.
Now the New Express will be subjected to “comprehensive rectification,” the regulator said.
The “rectification order” was issued by the authority for press and publications, radio, film and television in Guangdong.
Preliminary investigations revealed that the New Express, which belongs to the Yangcheng Evening News Group, had released several inaccurate reports on the publicly traded company Zoomlion between September 2012 and August 2013.
“The editorial management of the New Express was disorderly,” the regulator said in a statement.
“It was decided to impose an administrative penalty for Chen Yongzhou and revoke his reporter’s license.”
“The Yangcheng Evening News Group was also instructed to carry out a complete rectification of the New Express and recommended identification of the relevant responsible persons at the New Express and to immediately review the management team of the New Express.”
Chen had written several articles for the New Express in which he reported on alleged financial irregularities at a manufacturer of construction equipment called Zoomlion.
After being arrested, the newspaper published two appeals for his release on the front page and stood behind his journalistic work.
But then Chen appeared on state television and admitted that he had published false reports against payment.
"In this case, I have harmed Zoomlion as well as the entire news industry and its ability to gain the trust of the public," he told state-run news channel CCTV.
I've done it primarily because I've been out for money and fame.
I realized that I was doing the wrong thing.
Following Chen's apology, the New Express released an apology on the front page and said it had failed to review the reports correctly.
Various well-known suspects have recently made confessions on television.
Experts say confessions are still routinely forced, despite a change in the law earlier this year, during which authorities were banned from forcing someone to accuse themselves.
Drug tunnel with its own rail between the U.S. and Mexico discovered
One of the most sophisticated tunnel for drug smuggling between the U.S. and Mexico, fully equipped with lighting, ventilation and electric rail operations, has now been discovered.
The U.S. authorities described the 120-by-90-centimetre tunnel as one of the most sophisticated passages they'd ever found.
The tunnel, zigzagged over a length of just under six football pitches, connects warehouses near Tijuana, Mexico, and San Diego, USA.
In the area, there are countless inconspicuous warehouses, which makes it easier to hide trucks loaded with drugs.
The tunnel was closed before drugs could be transported unrecognized, authorities said.
Eight-and-a-half tons of marijuana and 148 kilograms of cocaine were recovered during the discovery of the tunnel, according to the court's records.
Three men who, according to the authorities, worked as drivers, were charged with possession and the intended sale of marijuana and cocaine.
They face prison sentences of between ten years and for life.
In Nogales, Arizona, smugglers rely on an extensive underground network of dismantling channels.
The tunnel is the eighth major passage that has been discovered in San Diego since 2006.
Some of the largest tunnels were discovered after the marijuana harvest in central Mexico in October, which poses the challenge for drug cartels on how to get their product to consumers quickly.
In 2010, authorities found an almost 640-meter-long passage with rails that ran from a kitchen in an apartment building in Tijuana to two warehouses in San Diego.
Orlando Blomo and Miranda Kerr still love each other
Actor Orlando Bloom and model Miranda Kerr want to go their separate ways in the future.
In an interview, however, Bloom said that he and Kerr still love each other.
Miranda Kerr and Orlando Bloom are parents to two-year-old Flynn.
Actor Orlando Bloom has commented on separation from his wife, top model Miranda Kerr.
In an interview with U.S. journalist Katie Couric, which was scheduled to air on Friday (local time), Bloom said "life sometimes doesn't go exactly the way we're planning or hoping."
Kerr and himself still loved each other, the 36-year-old emphasized.
“We’re going to support each other and love each other as parents of Flynn
Kerr and Bloom have been married since 2010, in 2011 their son Flynn was born.
Veganism, according to lexika, is subordinate to vegetarianism, but it differs from it in quite considerable ways.
Because while vegetarians allow themselves the consumption of cream cake, ice cream or a strong edamer, these feasts are not on the menu of a vegan.
Milk, cheese, egg, in addition to refraining from meat and fish, the veganer lets away from all the consumption of animal products - honey is also denied.
What remains, one might wonder, but on closer inspection, the vegan diet is characterized by a considerable ingenuity.
"Bread spreads are very popular," explains Ute Henkelmann, the owner of the local reform house, which has a large number of special products available for vegans.
Reuben Proctor also likes to visit the shop in Domgasse.
For 13 years the native New Zealander has been vegan, in Lampertheim he finds (almost) everything he needs to live. "In 1997 I changed my diet and became a vegetarian".
At the turn of the century, the next step took place and I became vegan.
"The conviction came not only because of the diet, but because of the choice of shoes," says Proctor, who prefers imitation leather to normal leather.
He realized that it is also possible in other everyday matters without animal products.
In the first place, being vegan is an ethic.
"I don't want animals to have to die for me," Proctor expresses the vegans' maxim, which extends to overall consumer behaviour.
Instead of mourning burgers, scrambled eggs or gummy bears, the vegan quickly discovered completely new products, the enthusiasm increased.
"It's a double bonus," says Proctor today, "actually, being vegan doesn't represent abandonment, it's no ascent, but it's an enrichment." Assumpting you'll reduce the choice of dining options with the change in diet, Proctor is more likely to attribute to the power of habit.
And it was precisely these conditionings that caused some well-intentioned attempts to fail.
"The addiction factor is a very crucial factor that allows many to give up quickly, similar to smoking," Proctor makes a comparison.
And object: "Certain tastes are not attached to the animal".
Often it is the spices or the type of preparation that make up the taste.
"But you can also create the same effect in other ways," explains the vegan.
Proctor, for example, brings eggplants, mushrooms and also a few vegan sausages.
He pays attention to a balanced, varied diet, in which it should also taste hearty.
Of course, you want to have something between your teeth, that’s legitimate.
"It's supposed to taste, but that's not just to be reduced to animal food," Proctor said.
He had not suffered any hympsic damage up to eating disorders, another widespread assumption.
For me, the transition had a positive aspect.
I don't absorb foreign cholesterol and have an increased vitamin content.
There are high-performance athletes who eat exclusively vegan.
"My blood counts are good, I often get checked," Proctor reveals.
In particular, the lack of vitamin B12 warns many veganismic critics.
"Since animals are nourished with feed instead of grazing in the pasture, it is questionable whether in the meat you buy in the freezer, the vitamin B12 content is high," Proctor finds.
For vegans, there is also the possibility to consume the coenzyme in the form of tablets.
"My B12 scores are fine," he explains.
Proctor does not have to go a long way for food procurement.
Staples are everywhere, after all, and every supermarket now has soy milk and other products.
Everything I need, I find on the spot.
Only from time to time I buy something special.
"Many people overlook that only the finished products are expensive, but that's the case of "ordinary" finished products."
In any case, the preparation of vegan dishes is far cheaper and less time-consuming than commonly assumed.
"There's a lot to discover in the house kitchen, with a few small tricks you can achieve good, if not partly better results," says Proctor.
Margarine instead of butter, a little more baking soda and carbonic acid instead of egg, with its vegan cheesecakes Proctor was also able to amaze his in-laws.
The Vegan lends a new spice to family celebrations.
This has dynamism, if you cook together, it has a new significance.
"The situations that can arise are as different as the people themselves," says Proctor.
Of course, he has also been skeptical.
Don't you like my food anymore?
"Should I feel bad now, because I drink milk?" - with such questions, you have to act as a veganer with sensitivity.
"I don't reject people, I'm part of this society," Proctor doesn't want an enclavation.
And more specifically, the everyday world does not seem as foreign to vegan as presumed - and the veganer is not as restricted in everyday life as commonly assumed.
Fast fooding is also allowed.
Fries are everywhere.
"Many kebab shops offer falafel, or a vegetarian yufka," says Proctor.
In a pizzeria he swerves on pasta dishes, or just orders a pizza without cheese, all a matter of habit.
The restaurants are slowly getting into it.
"When I started eating vegan 13 years ago, everything was even more difficult," the New Zealander can find a development: "You have to talk to the people, make them aware of it."
"If there's enough inquiries, they may be inclined to supplement the menu," he hopes.
Vegan restaurants are developing, such as the galley in Mannheim or Café Vogelfrei.
Proctor has the wish that other restaurants also have at least one vegan dish on the menu.
It’s basically vegan dishes for everyone.
Anyone can eat them, so they're exactly the opposite of exclusive.
"Meat dishes, on the other hand, are exclusive in the truest sense of the word, because they exclude others," Proctor notes.
"If you want to avoid food scandals, then veganism is a safe way to make most of it impossible from the outset," Proctor says.
However, the purchase of vegan products can be a bit difficult at the beginning.
In particular, if one learns to understand that animal additives can also contain in peanuts as dyestuffs, one seems to quickly leave the courage with the observance of ethics with so many hurdles and traps.
Reuben Proctor and Lars Thomsen have written a book: "Veganissimo - animal ingredients and their alternatives" explains the many ingredients that are added to food and cleaning products.
"Lititually you develop a scanner view and know which characters and logos you have to pay attention to "It's sometimes better to take small steps than to stop or stumble," Proctor knows.
Veganism, it is an important moral finger-pointing that strikes consciousness.
Maybe he doesn’t quite manage to abandon his habits, but at least he can teach the consumer to critically question his habits.
The exchange operator Nasdaq OMX continues to hold its customers.
Trading at the Nasdaq Options Market was disrupted on Friday afternoon German time.
In a statement, the operator blamed technical problems.
The other eleven U.S. option markets, including two of Nasdaq OMX, continued their business unhindered.
The latest incident continues a whole series of smaller and bigger breakdowns at the exchange operator.
Only on Tuesday, the indices of the Nasdaq had not been calculated for an hour because of errors in data transport.
There had been two breakdowns in August this year alone in one week.
At first, U.S. investment bank Goldman Sachs had sented massively faulty purchase orders to options markets because of technical problems.
As a result, it took the exchange operators almost a whole day to look through the orders and delete them.
Two days later, half of the total U.S. stock trading came to a halt for several hours because of a computer breakdown on the Nasdaq exchange.
Facebook's IPO had also caused a stir in the past year.
Nasdaq systems had failed to cope with the tide of buying and selling orders, the SEC later noted, thundering the company to a record fine of 10 million dollars.
Constance: Cyclist drives 63-year-old
According to the police, the accident occurred when a 26-year-old man was on Thursday evening, around 10 p.m., with a ladies' bike on the left sidewalk from the station square in the direction of Marktstätte.
When suddenly a 63-year-old man stepped onto the sidewalk from a restaurant, the cyclist could not brake in time, according to police.
In the following collision, the pedestrian was pushed against the house wall, went to the ground in the further course and contracted an approximately 15 centimeter-long laceration on the head.
An ambulancy team took the injured person to the hospital for medical treatment.
British police issue Assange with exprolation notice
British police today handed over an extradition notice to WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange, who has sought refuge in the Ecuadorian embassy in London and applied for asylum.
Scotland Yard stated that the 40-year-old Australian had been offered a “call to turn himself in”, which asked him to appear in a police station, and added that if he did not comply with this, the arrest threatened him.
Assange threatens extradition to Sweden on charges in a sex offense after his options have been exhausted under British law since the Supreme Court rejected his appeal against extradition earlier this month.
Since he fears that Stockholm will extradite him to the U.S., he had sought refuge at the Ecuadorian embassy in London on June 19 and asked the South American country for political asylum.
Scotland Yard "admitted to a 40-year-old man an invitation to stand at a police station at a time chosen by us," a spokesperson said in a statement.
He continues to violate the terms and conditions of his release on bail.
The embassy gave no comment on the delivery of the police request.
Assange fears he will be extradited from Sweden to the United States and charged with possible espionage charges there after posting over 250,000 diplomatic messages on WikiLeaks.
Rider who had raced between his legs at 210 km/h and hot drink will receive £1,000 penalty
One motorist was imposed a £1,000 penalty because he had been driving between his legs at up to 210 km/h and a hot drink.
Andrew Howie, 35 from Tiptree, Essex, was halted on May 27 with his Mercedes Benz on the A120 at Braintree.
After the police stopped him, they discovered the drink cup between his legs.
In front of Colchester District Court, Howie pleaded guilty without taking the necessary care and attention.
He received seven points and a six-month driving ban.
Howie has also been convicted to pay £90 costs as well as a £100 casualty surcharge.
Tax for foreign property owners against London's speculative bubble
The Ministry of Finance has provisionally calculated the CGT measure, but is still awaitinging the final decision of Chancellor George Osborne, who in the 2012 budget had imposed a stamp duty of 7% on apartments costing over two million pounds as well as annual fees for buyers who hold apartments as a company rather than individuals.
Earnings already exclude stamp duty for residential property in the districts of Westminster and Kensington & Chelsea, which stood at 708 million pounds in 2012/13, the proceeds taken together for Northern Ireland, Wales, Scotland, the north-east, north-west, Yorkshire and the Humber region.
Cook said: “Following the increases in stamp duty on high-priced housing and the introduction of related legislation against circumvention, it’s hardly possible to claim that high-quality properties are taxed too low, regardless of the impact of the obsolete municipal tax system.”
“However, this move could make some foreign investors more reluctant when it comes to buying real estate in London, or prevent owners from selling,” he added.
World-class properties – the top five to ten per cent of the housing market by price – in the wealthy south-west belt in London, which stretches from Fulham to Wimbledon, have been priced by a record 11.8% last year.
Prices in Central London showed uniform annual growth of 5.6%, but were overshadowed by an emerging “domestic market” where the south-western part of the city, the north (7.4%) and the east (6.5%) experienced an upward trend, according to a study by Savills.
Scientists have been able to shed more light on the context of a dog’s tail swizzling with its mood.
Earlier research had shown that happy dogs wagged more to the right (from the dog's point of view), while it was the left side in nervous dogs.
But now scientists say that other dogs can perceive these subtle differences and react to them.
Professor Georgio Vallortigara, a neuroscientist at the University of Trento, said: “In humans, it is well known that the left and right hemispheres of the brain react differently to stimuli that evoke positive or negative emotions.”
Here we tried to look at this with other species.
He also noted that in dogs, just like in humans, the right hemisphere of the brain was responsible for the movement of the left side and vice versa, and that the two hemispheres played different roles in the feelings.
To learn more about how dogs are responding to their conspecific's lateral tail wagging, the researchers supervised the animals as these films of other dogs looked at each other.
They measured the heart rate of the four-legged friends and analyzed the behavior.
We will probably soon understand why the tail moves in one, sometimes in the other direction
Professor Vallortigara said, “We showed the dogs movies with dogs – either a true-to-life version or a silhouette to eliminate distracting other aspects, and we were able to steer the movement of the tail more to the left or the right.”
When the animals saw an otherwise expressionless dog moving the tail to the right (seen from the perspective of the wagging dog), they remained completely relaxed.
But if the tail tendency was mainly to the left (again from the perspective of the dog wagging), their heart rate rose and they looked restless.
According to Professor Vallortigara, the dogs do not intentionally communicate with each other through these movements.
Rather, he is convinced that the dogs have learned from experience in which movements they should worry and when not.
He said: “If you have different encounters with other dogs and wagging with the tail in one direction is rather linked to friendly behavior and the right side produces rather less friendly behavior, then one reacts based on these experiences.”
The researchers say the findings could help dog owners, veterinarians and trainers gain a better understanding of their animal's emotions.
John Bradshaw, an expert on dog behaviour and a guest fellow at the Faculty of Veterinary Medicine at the University of Bristol, explained that this was not the first study to examine whether left and right was important for dogs.
Last year, a team from the University of Lincoln found that dogs turn their head to the left when they look at an aggressive dog, and to the right when it comes to a satisfied dog.
“Dogs are more likely to approach a robotic dog when his ‘tail’ wags on the left and not on the right, rather than becoming restless – just the other way around than in the Italian study.”
According to his statement, the differences could stem from the fact that the dogs in the various studies did not interpret the animals in the films or the robot dogs as dogs.
A study of how dogs react to real dogs could help, he explained.
“While there’s considerable evidence for many different mammals that the two hemispheres are used for different purposes, many of the details have yet to be determined—and dogs are no exception,” Bradshaw said.
However, since their behavior can simply be recorded, it probably won’t be long before we understand, which is why the tail sometimes moves into one direction, sometimes in the other.
Arctical Monkeys postpone appearance in Glasgow due to Alex Turner's condition
Rock band Arctic Monkeys has postponed an appearance in Glasgow after their lead singer contracted laryngeal inflammation.
The band from Sheffield was scheduled to play at Hydro on Friday.
Due to the illness of the lead singer Alex Turner, however, the concert must now be postponed.
The band's announcement came after she had to postpone an appearance at the LG Arena in Birmingham on Thursday.
In a statement on its official website, the Arctic Monkeys said: “Following the decision to cancel the show at LG Arena in Birmingham tonight, as well as on medical advice, the Arctic Monkeys will also have to cancel the show at Hydro in Glasgow on Friday, November 1.”
“Alex Turner has been diagnosed with throat inflammation, which is why he unfortunately can’t perform.”
The show at the LG Arena in Birmingham will now take place on November 20, which has been moved to November 21 at Hydro in Glasgow.
All tickets remain valid for these shows.
We apologise to all ticket holders for the inconveniences caused thereby.
Please contact customer service at the outlets where you have purchased your ticket in case you need further assistance.
Pope Francis appoints first cardinals in February
Pope Francis will appoint new cardinals of the Catholic Church for the first time on February 22, the Vatican announced on Thursday.
Cardinals are the highest-ranking clericists in the Catholic Church after the pope and they are also the ones who choose a pope, so that Francis will thus appoint the first group of men who will ultimately participate in the election of his successor.
There are 201 cardinals.
However, a cardinal who has reached an age of 80 can no longer participate in the papal election – a group of 120 “eligible cardinals”.
In a statement in which the news was announced, Father Federico Lombardi, a spokesman for the Vatican, said that a meeting of all cardinals would take place before the ceremony on the appointment of the new cardinals, which was also known as consistorium.
“Pope Francis has decided to announce his decision to convene the Consistorium in February in advance so that other meetings requiring cardinals from all over the world can be planned,” Lombardi said.
Jack Valero of Catholic Voices said the number of electoral cardinals was likely to have declined by February.
He explained that a pope usually appoints as many cardinals as necessary so that the number of cardinals entitled to vote would be 120 again, and as many cardinals over 80 as he would like.
Next year's consistory is significant because it is the first since the election of Francis in March this year, Valero said.
There is currently a certain overhang on the sides of Europe, and Italy in particular.
“It will be interesting to see if the new pope will appoint cardinals from the rest of the world to restore balance,” he said.
Forty percent of Catholics live in South America, but they have only a tiny number of cardinals.
The cardinals will also be the first to be appointed since the formation of the Council of Cardinals founded by Francis, a group of eight cardinals from all over the world who are tasked with finding opportunities to reform the Church.
In the past, the pope decided everything alone.
“Now Francis has chosen these eight cardinals to help him,” Valero said.
He declared that it was “well possible” that Francis was asking the cardinals for advice.
But so far we haven’t been in this situation – that’s all brand new.
Valero said that typically the bishops would be called to cardinals from big places, but Francis was “full of surprises—so we don’t know who he’s appointing.”
GM recalls new pick-up trucks in U.S
General Motors is recalling just under 19,000 of its all-new 2014 Chevrolet Silverado and GMC Sierra Pickup trucks to fix a problem with the manually adjustable seat, according to a notice from the U.S
For some of the trolleys, there may be a defect in the mechanism for restoring the front seat backrest.
For this reason, they do not comply with the federal guidelines for vehicle safety at headrests.
“When hitting the vehicle from behind, it’s possible that the headrester won’t protect the occupants properly, so there’s an increased risk of injury,” the message from the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA).
The recalled models were made between August 1 and September 10.
Supply of the GM trucks began in June and represents the key model launch of the leading U.S. automaker since the 2009 insolvency-related restructuring.
GM briefed vehicle owners about the defect in the first half of October.
The NHTSA was unable to review the notification letter because of the 16-day government shutdown, which had a negative impact on car sales in October.
Sales of the Silverado and Sierra trucks, redesigned for the 2014 model year, increased by 20 percent in the first ten months of the year, GM said on Friday.
In October, GM sold 42,660 Silverado and 16,503 Sierra pickup trucks.
GM shares rose 1.4 percent to US$37.47 on the New York Stock Exchange on Friday afternoon.
Desperation criek of an Obamas voter
I've elected President Obama twice in hopes that change is possible
He says Obama has made commendable efforts that have been thwarted by Republican blockade tactics
Blockade policy doesn't apologise Obamacare's website problems, the drone strikes
Obama's memoirs about the 2008 election campaign are a sad reminder of what would have been possible
Nathaniel P. Morris is a second-year student at Harvard Medical School.
I'm currently reading a terribly sad book.
It's a book that I believed would cheer me up during the gloomy second year of my medical studies and give me hope again.
“The Audacity to Win” and describes the memoirs of Barack Obama in the 2008 presidential election.
When I'm done with my patient transcripts in the evening and going to bed, that's when the book takes me back to a time when politics inspired millions and could take the breath off a speech.
The election became a landslide victory and news anchors paused to show the historical significance of this hour.
My classmates were crying with joy and my parents collected all the newspapers they could find.
A young team of visionaries was on their way to the White House and the nation was ready for change.
When Obama moved to office in 2008, he had approval ratings of 82%.
And then I'll close the book.
Switching to the present again is a rude awakening, as if being torn out of a dream.
It’s hard to remember these optimistic times – they seem to be a vague memory, a sad reminder of missed opportunities.
In the years since I cast my vote for the first time, something has actually changed.
But it's just nothing I could have imagined.
I recognize the great and multifaceted things Obama has achieved, from the passing of the Affordable Care Act for health insurance to military withdrawal from Iraq, the end of forced secrecy of homosexuality in the army and the killing of Osama bin Laden.
I, too, am of the opinion that partisan political blockade tactics have tipped too many aspirations with which our nation should be brought forward: immigration reform, a public option for health insurance and the closure of Guantanamo Bay, to name a few.
However, having defended the Obama administration against colleagues and acquaintances countless times, I have come to an end with my explanatory attempts.
I've reached a point of political desperation.
The republican blockade policy cannot explain why eavesdropping of foreign heads of state or the killing of innocent children by drones overseas.
It cannot explain why the National Security Agency collects data about the private lives of Americans and why whistleblowers are being punished who disclose government misconduct.
It cannot explain why Anwar al-Awlaki, a U.S. citizen, was assassinated without trial, nor why public funding and spending limits are circumvented during the presidential election.
It does not justify the findings of a report saying the White House's efforts to immobilize the media are the "most aggressive ... since the Nixon administration."
And recentuarily, it can't apologise the inability of designing a simple website in the more than three years since the adoption of the Affordable Care Act.
I don't know if that's what I should've expected.
Whether I should have understood at 18 that the government's work could contradict the political campaigns preceding it.
Obviouslely, the Deputies office is not a foreseeable course as an opposition party and random events such as the Newtown Massacre shape public discussion.
But if I look at the examples mentioned above, then they seem largely elected by the government itself.
And that bothers me the most.
I re-elected Obama in 2012, not because I was excited about his candidacy.
Mitt Romney represented a confusing and unfermented alternative, he didn't seem to be able to set his direction and positions.
I had the impression that a second termine for Obama, free from the pressure of future elections, would fulfill the hope we'd heared of for so long.
However, by lowering approval ratings for Obama to less than 45% this week, the return to 2008 through this book has become all the tougher.
It awakens in me the longing for the many promises that have dissipated.
This week, I read a section in the book that describes how Obama had to pocket a heavy loss against Clinton in Pennsylvania primaries.
In a meeting with election workers after the decision, he told his employees they would have to get back on track and follow the purpose of their cause.
“I want us to find our magic again,” he said.
We need to remember who we are.
It's five years later, Mr President, and I couldn't agree with you more.
The views expressed in this commentary are exclusively that of Nathaniel Morris.
Clive Palmer claims Prime Minister Tony Abbott has a conflict of interest on parental leave
Billionaire and MP Clive Palmer says Prime Minister Tony Abbott is in a conflict of interest in his plans for parental leave because his daughters could become pregnant and therefore benefit.
The mining magnate, who finds himself in a judicial dispute over the payment of 6 million dollars in carbon tax, made the salution as he wanted to swipe questions off the table about whether he was in conflict.
The Palmer United Party could control up to four votes in the Senate, which is important for deciding whether the carbon and mining taxes will be cancelled or slashed.
Yet Palmer claimed that only ministers could have conflicts of interest and Abbott's daughters would personally benefit from the laws.
“He finds himself in a major conflict of interest when it comes to paid parental leave, because when one of his daughters becomes pregnant, he has a direct interest in whether she receives leave or not,” Palmer said.
Two months after the electoral election, the Electoral Commission officially declared Palmer the winner of Fairfax's Sunshine Coast headquarters by 53 votes after the recount.
Palmer called for a revision of the ballot reconciliation process, so that it takes place more quickly.
Tony Abbott's daughters Frances and Bridget.
Should these elections be decided two months after the end of voting?
"We need a better system," he said.
Why shouldn’t we have a system where you march in, enter the data into a computer, choose immediately, and then have the result at 6:30 in the evening?
Palmer also criticized the use of pencils when marking the ballot papers.
Do you use them because you can erase the results if you don’t like them?
Coordinating with pencils these days is unbelievable.
The Electoral Commission has considered the options for an electronic election and recently published a joint legislative proposal with New Zealand.
Palmer, 59, explained that his policy includes an international airport for the Sunshine Coast and that he will take his new office “very seriously.”
A public office is about public service.
“We strive for no reward other than that of history, namely that we can serve this community at a critical time,” he said.
RBS suspends two currency traders
Royal Bank of Scotland has suspended two traders from its foreign exchange division, according to two people familiar with the situation; this is another indication that the worldwide official investigation into the alleged manipulations in the currency market is picking up speed.
Some of the world's largest banks, including UBS, Barclays, Deutsche Bank and RBS, confirmed that they are working with the authorities on investigations into the world's largest financial market, where US$5.3 quadrillion changes hands every day.
The two traders would be the first RBS employees to be suspended in the widening inquiry involving the scandal over Libor's interbank business.
The bank, which made no comment on the suspensions, confirmed this month that it had received requests for information from authorities.
“Our investigation into this issue continues and we are cooperating fully with the FCA and other regulators,” the bank said two weeks ago.
Last montharday, people related to the situation indicated that RBS had forwarded the records of emails and instant messages sent by and to a previous trader to the British regulator, the Financial Conduct Authority.
This trader, Richard Usher, had left RBS in 2010 and is said to have been furloughed by his current position as European head of forex spot trading at JPMorgan.
Rohan Ramchandani, head of European spot trading at Citi, was furlough this week and Matt Gardiner, formerly a senior currency trader at Barclays and UBS, was suspended this week at Standard Chartered.
None of these traders has been accused of any wrongdoing.
Usher's instant message group included bankers at Barclays and Citigroup, people close to the situation said.
UBS announced this week that it had taken steps against some of its employees after the Swiss regulatory agency Finma stated that it was investigating suspected manipulation of the foreign exchange market at a number of Swiss banks.
At least six authorities worldwide – the European Commission, Finma, the Swiss competition authority Weko, the FCA, the U.S. Department of Justice and the Hong Kong Monetary Authority – are currently dealing with accusations that bankers are said to have agreed on transactions on the currency market.
HSBC, Citigroup, JPMorgan and Credit Suisse have also launched internal investigations or received requests for information from authorities, people familiar with the situation said.
Banks scour instant messages and e-mails from several years in search of cases of misconduct.
The news about these investigations has thrown traders into turmoil in an area that has belonged to one of the most profitable trading units of investment banks in recent years, but was under pressure this year because low volatility in currencies restricts opportunities for speculators.
Some bankers have tried to downplay the affair and said it was nearly impossible to manipulate the extensive and highly liquid foreign exchange market, but leading traders believe that this is not necessarily true.
A leading trader noted that, despite the enormous daily volume in currency trading, the fragmentation of liquidity between the various trading platforms and the increasing use of own internal platforms by banks means that “you can influence the market even with very low ticket prices.”
The news came out the same day when Credit Suisse announced that it had terminated a trader of its London Exchange Traded Funds office this week after causing a loss of just under six-million-dollars at the end of last year.
The bank promptly informed the competent authorities and worked with them.
"We are sure that the trader acted alone and that the matter is contained," Credit Suisse said.
Painted the construction fence on the Bechtle property
A group of the "Swiss Meadow" initiative has embellished the construction fence on the Bechtle property on the spa promenade in Bad Herrenalb.
In addition, she cleaned and widened the footpath, according to a press release.
The planned covering of the disintegrating house in the back of the property had not been allowed for security reasons, because the site threatens to slip away.
Alfred Abel, who currently manages the property, had discussed the beautification action with his colleague Reinhard Domke from the BI.
Prior to BI's action, a banner with the message "We're in favour" had been attached.
This, however, did little bothered the approximately 20 members of the citizens' initiative in their action.
Their motto on the big poster was "We are against building ruins".
In doing so, she wanted to point out the danger of even larger ruins on the Swiss meadow - should one day the giant project fail.
In conversation with many passers-by in front of the "construction site", it became clear that some citizens believe that the realization of the project of the city will be better for many, although not a few have the same questions about the dimension of the presented project as the BI.
But they're starting to resign because the city is delaying those responses any further, it says on the part of the BI.
As can be heard, the mayor's public statements also have an effect.
May had said at the municipal council meeting that he feared the end for the thermal bath should the population vote in the citizens' decision against the plans for a bathing and wellness complex on the Swiss meadow.
Some therefore appear almost scared, others perceive it as a "plump extortion attempt" on the population, according to the press release of the BI.
The BI thanked the donors for the color and all the helpers.
Rangnick scolding for Schiris: Mane no swallow king
As satisfied as Ralf Rangnick is satisfied with the current highest form of Bundesliga table leader Red Bull Salzburg, the sporting director of the "Bulls" was disappointed by the referees.
"There is currently a dangerous tendency that Mane and Alan will be pushed into the swallow kings category," he took the two offensive swirlers into protection on Thursday at a press conference in Salzburg.
The most recent reason for Rangnick's criticism was yellow for Mane at the 3:0 against Grödig last Sunday, in which the Senegalese an alleged acting became a doom - which, of course, was none, as the TV pictures showed.
"It's a blatant wrong decision," said Rangnick, describing referee Harkam as "completely overwhelmed."
Harkam would also have contributed "his share" to the escalation between Salzburg coach Roger Schmidt and Grödig coach Adi Hütter after the game.
He defended his German compatrion decidedly: "I would have behaved the same in every single situation as Roger Schmidt".
Schmidt himself doesn't want any contact with his colleague after the skirmish with Hütter.
I wouldn't know why.
"Because I'm the one who has been insulted," insisted the 46-year-old.
In view of the run of success, Rangnick voiced a hymn of praise.
"There are only two words for that: really good," he said.
There's not much to suspend.
Above all, the way the squad plays is impressive.
It's supposed to continue like this.
There will hardly be any squad changes in the winter transfer period.
There aren't great reasons to make a difference.
"Excluding a player approaches with the desire that he wants to leave the club," Rangnick clarified.
Supreme Court confirms Obama's health care bill
It's a huge victory for the Obama administration: The U.S. Supreme Court ruled today that the health care law closely linked to Barack Obama is constitutional.
By a vote of five to four, the judges ruled that the individual mandate of the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act – forcing U.S. citizens to take out health insurance or pay a penalty by 2014 – is constitutional under the state’s tax authority.
Chief Justice John Roberts sided with the chamber's four more liberal members, while judges Scalia, Thomas, Alito and Kennedy ruled against it.
The court also upheld the remaining sections of the 2700-page law and also took the view that the presupposition of the health law that states would either have to expand the entry requirements for Medicaid or otherwise remove all state Medicaid funding is not unconstitutionally restrictive.
The lawsuit against the law had been initiated by 26 states and the National Federation of Independent Business.
The bill had been firmly opposed by all leading Republican presidential candidates, including eventual challenger Mitt Romney, in the 2012 pre-election campaign.
Norway: Norwegian place takes light with giant mirrors
With huge mirrors, the inhabitants of a Norwegian village have brought light to their gloomy valley.
Due to the low degree of incidence of sunlight, the Rjukan, located in the Vestfjord valley, usually sinks from autumn to spring in the shade of the surrounding mountains.
With three gigantic reflectors at a height of 45 meters, a century dream came true on Wednesday.
"Finally!" enthused mayor Steinar Bergsland at the starting ceremony on broadcaster TV2.
Some valley inhabitants adjusted sun chairs, others put on sunglasses for safety reasons.
Until now, sunseekers had to ride a cable car to a nearby summit in winter.
Ten years ago, the local artist Martin Andersen had presented the proposal to steer the rays into the valley with mirrors.
However, this basic idea has existed since 1913.
After several years of debate, the city council had finally adopted the 5 million crown (around 615 000 euros) expensive project.
A similar construction has been providing winter sunshine to the Italian alpine resort of Viganella for several years.
Mail barely empties on Sundays
"Many citizens won't have noticed it yet, the red dots for Sunday emptying are still at various mailboxes of the core city and the neighborhoods, but if you read the small print on the yellow containers of Post AG, you quickly find out that the emptying on Sundays and public holidays no longer takes place," the FDP announces.
A request from the FDP has confirmed this.
On Sundays and public holidays, only in front of the post office building at Ernst-Ludwig-Straße 36 and Jakob-Müller-Straße 1 in Hüttenfeld will be emptied.
Whether in Hofheim and Rosengarten is still emptied on Sunday, will not be answered in the answer to the request of the FDP.
The social and youth policy spokesman of the FDP, Fritz Röhrenbeck, wants to catch up here.
Sunday emptying is an important postal service, especially when it comes to appointment mail.
"Despite the price increase for letters on January 2013, this company is thinning it radically and unreasonably."
Thomas Bittner, chairman of the parliamentary group and city association of the Liberals, supports his group colleagues: "What annoys me is the fact that the emptying times here are secretly changed or deleted".
The post office is miles away from proximity to the citizens.
"At least the mailboxes in the districts of Neuschloß and Rosengarten have to be emptied again on Sundays," Bittner and Röhrenbeck said in unison.
Also for the core citizen applies, especially non-mobile fellow citizens would have to be able to reach a mailbox with Sunday emptying on foot.
Röhrenbeck asked in the city parliament whether the city administration is planning talks with the mail about this matter.
Mayor Erich Maier replied that the post office had not communicated with Lampertheim.
To his knowledge, however, there is also a Sunday emulation in Hofheim.
Your party has advocated privatisations.
"Then decisions are made elsewhere," Maier said.
Frankfurt parking fees are expected to rise vigorously
Parking in Frankfurt could soon become sensibly more expensive.
The city magistrate is discussing this morning about a submission by traffic director Stefan Majer (Greens).
Accordingly, the parking fees should be increased by 50 percent.
However, it is not the prices that should be raised, but the time interval of parking meters and parking ticket machines should be shortened from 30 to 20 minutes.
In December, the city council has yet to decide.
The retail committee of the IHK Frankfurt thinks this "is not a good idea".
The parking facility immediately in front of the shop was "an advantage that should not be underestimated for smaller specialist dealers".
The Verkehrsclub Deutschland is of the opinion that an increase after two decades is "entirely appropriate and actually long overdue".
Public transport is also becoming more expensive.
Freudenstadt: Fast actions surprise hosts
With a narrow squad, Messrs I of the TSV Freudenstadt had to compete against the TG Gönningen of the Swabian Alb in the Schiller school gym in Reutlingen.
The Älblers stood with an experienced team on the other side.
However, it soon became apparent that, due to ages, they could not quite keep up with the rapid actions of the Freudenstädter.
With the hard and placed serves, the Gönningers were extremely difficult.
They repeatedly missed the passes and attacks leaked neatly by Simon Schenk and Eduard Schulz over the middle of the net and were not on hand in block defenses.
Two high first set wins clearly expressed the superiority of Freudenstädter in all respects.
In view of this crushing and game-defining performance, the third set was then won a bit lax and listless to the 3:0 final score (25:13, 25:14, 25:22).
On November 2, a much more powerful opponent will be on the field with TV Baiersbronn, who was able to clearly prevail in Nagold.
TSV Freudenstadt I: Dominik Bäuerle, Yannik Büchle, Sebastian Dölker, Felix Pälchen, Stefan Räller, Simon Schenk and Eduard Schulz.
In the B-Class, the second teams from Baiersbronn and Freudenstadt faced each other in the Murgtalhalle in Baiersbronn.
They delivered a hard-fought two-hour match.
Nervousness on both sides caused errors and point gifts, especially when accepting the ball; even with the passes, the precision was sometimes scarce and in some critical game scenes, overzealousness prevented the eye for possible point gains.
In this performance-wise very balanced encounter in the TSV team of Marcus Blasutto, however, the great physical commitment and a pleasing commitment of both teams was consistently right.
Nursing day at the smalle en
For the fifth day of nursing in the aftermath of the Natura 2000 project Upper Hotzenwald, a group of committed participants met at the small English hut in Ibach.
Due to suggestions from the population on this day in the area of the hut, the view into the valley should be cut free, said Mayor Helmut Kaiser.
According to Kaiser, many hikers described this area as the highlight of the gorge trail, on which the landscape offers the ideal setting for magnificent views.
He thanked those present for their willingness to cooperate and emphasized that the nursing day was a suitable instrument to strengthen the connection between people and nature.
It offers every citizen the opportunity to set accents in nature itself.
At the same time as the tourist aspect, the ecological consideration could also be taken into account.
Friederike Tribukait from the nature conservation department of the Freiburg government office explained this ecological aspect following the greeting by Landrat Tilman Bollacher, who had taken over the patronage and wished the action a good success.
According to Tribukait, the tourist goal of vision coincides with the goal of preserving the now strongly overgrowing old Allmendweid field with its specific protected habitat types by opening and connecting to the remaining pastures.
Certain species, such as rare butterflies and grasshoppers, cannot overcome barriers in the form of forest areas.
You will need these free connections for species conservation.
Tribukait said she didn't want to fail at this point to break a lance for the planned biosphere area.
The current measure does not only fit seamlessly into this planning.
A biosphere area also offers the opportunity for sustainable economic and ecological development on the basis of internationally recognized Unesco status and thus a continuous addition to what has been achieved with the life project as well as the previous nursing days for the future.
After these various explanations, precinct leader Christoph Wehle and life project manager Cornelia Bischoff divided the helpers into groups.
The larger trees had already been knocked in advance, the forestry tower may be ready.
A group of helpers should take care of the release of juniper bushes and the care of a dry stone bar.
The goal was the clear view at the viewpoint.
Löffingen: Köpfler still the only candidate
Information on the Internet: Although the Baar town of Löffingen with its 7514 inhabitants, an intact infrastructure, a pioneering school, children's, youth and senior citizen policy, as well as its own municipal utilities can be described as an attractive city, no second mayoral candidate has yet come forward.
As the only candidate for the succession of the mayor Norbert Brugger, who is no longer a candidate due to age reasons, the 46-year-old banker and business economist Dieter Köpfler from Löffingen has so far been confirmed.
The application period expires on Monday, November 11, at 6 p.m.
Google, Samsung, Huawei sued over Nortel patent
The group, which owns thousands of former Nortel patents, stricked an avalanche of lawsuits for patent infringement against mobile phone makers on Thursday, including Google, the company that was outbid by him at Nortel's bankruptcy auction.
Rockstar, the consortium the Nortel patent had bought for U.S.$4.5 billion, sued Samsung Electronics Co Ltd, HTC Corp, Huawei and four other companies for patent violations before the U.S. District Court in Texas.
Rockstar is jointly owned by Apple, Microsoft, BlackBerry, Ericsson and Sony.
Google is accused of patent infringement in seven cases.
The patents concern technology that aligns search terms on the Internet with relevant advertising, according to the lawsuit, which hits the core of Google’s search engine business.
Representatives from Samsung, Huawei, HTC and Rockstar weren't immediately reachable.
Samsung, Huawei and HTC are making phones that work with Google's Android operating system, which is in fierce competition with Apple's and Microsoft's mobile products.
In 2011, Google placed an initial bid over 900 million dollars for the Nortel patents.
Google increased its offer several times and recently offered 4.4 billion US dollars.
After being outbid with Rockstar's Nortel patentants, Google acquired Motorola Mobility for US$12.5 billion, a business that was also motivated in part by Motorola's library of patents.
“Although Google has failed in its attempt to acquire the lawsuit patents at the auction, Google infringed and continues to do so,” Google said.
Rockstar is demanding high damages from Google, as it claims Google's patent infringement is intentional, according to the allegation.
Egypt sworn in first freely elected president
Mohammed Morsi takes the oath of office, but his day of triumph should not mean the end of political disputes in Egypt.
Islamist Mohammed Morsi promised a “new Egypt” when he was sworn in as the country’s first freely elected president, succeeding Husni Mubarak, who had been expelled from office 16 months earlier.
With his inauguration before the Supreme Constitutional Court, Morsi also became the first freely elected Islamist president of the Arab world and Egypt's fifth head of state since overthrowing the monarchy about 60 years ago.
He took the oath in front of the 18 judges dressed in black robes of judgment, which has its seat on the Nile and is modeled on an ancient Egyptian temple.
“We strive for a better future, a new Egypt and a second republic,” Morsi said at the solemn ceremonial ceremony broadcast live on state television.
“Today, the Egyptian people have laid the foundation for a new life – absolute freedom, a true democracy and stability,” said the 60-year-old Morsi, a U.S. trained engineer and member of the Muslim Brotherhood, a fundamentalist group that had been sharply targeted by the respective government since its incorporation 84 years ago.
Hundreds of soldiers and police guarded the building when Morsi arrived in a small car convoy shortly after eleven o'clock local time.
Only a few hundred supporters had gathered outside the court to cheer for the new president, and in contrast to the presidential pomp from the Mubarak period, traffic was only briefly stopped so that the car convoy could cross the usually busy road that connects the city centre with the southern suburbs.
Humbled as an uncharismatic Brotherhood “replacement wheel,” his personal prestige has increased since his victory and a Friday speech in which he presented himself not only as a candidate of the Islamists, but of all those who wanted to bring the aspirations of the 2011 uprising against the authoritarian Mubarak.
“Egypt is now a civilian, national, constitutional and modern state,” Morsi, dressed in a blue suit with a red tie, explained to the judges in the wood-paneled chamber where he took the oath of office.
Later, Morsi drove to the University of Cairo, where he was supposed to give his inaugural address.
He was officially greeted by an army band that played the national anthem he listened to in custody.
The military leader Field Marshal Hussein Tantawi was present.
Upon his arrival he was greeted by hundreds of attendees in the university’s largest lecture hall with chants from “The army and the people are one hand”.
Built in 1908 as a bastion of secular education, the University of Cairo developed into a stronghold of Islamist student groups in the 1970s.
Morsi took a symbolic oath in Tahir Square on Friday, the birthplace of the uprising that led last year at the end of Mubarak's authoritarian rule, and he vowed to reclaim presidential powers that the military council had taken over from out-officiation head of state.
But with taking the official oath before the court, and not as usually before parliament, he bends over the will of the military, indicating that the power struggle will continue.
Morsi's speech in Tahir Square was full of dramatic populist gestures.
Falkenberg discotheque makes for two great evenings
The new season in the Falkenberger discotheque "Blue Velvet" has begun.
This Friday evening it's "Pump this party", tomorrow Saturday evening it continues with an Ü 25 party.
Fat beats and cool sounds are of course promised by the organizers on both days.
Worker fell from ladder: seriously injured
When replacing a faulty external sensor, a 51-year-old worker in Eggelsberg crashed off a ladder on Thursday and seriously injured himself.
The man from Lamprechtshausen wanted to replace a defective heating sensor on the outer façade of an inn.
As he climbed onto the ladder leaned on the gutter, it slipped away and the man crashed onto the concrete floor.
He suffered serious injuries and was taken to the Salzburg State Hospital by rescue helicopter "Christophorus 6."
Rapist in South Africa: Twice life imprisonment
Nine months after the brutal rape and mutilation of a 17-year-old girl in South Africa, the perpetrator was sentenced to twice for life on Friday.
This was reported by SABC.
The victim later died as a result of the serious injuries.
Johannes Kana had already been found guilty at the beginning of the week of the crime.
The act had sparked horror nationwide.
The then 21-year-old had raped Anene Booysen in February at an industrial site in Bredasdorp near Cape Town.
For 14 hours, doctors struggled with the survival of the victim, but in vain.
The sight of the young people was so appalling that the caregivers and sisters needed psychological assistance.
Cana had admitted during the trial to having been in a bar on the night of action with Booysen.
He also confessed to beating and raping her, but he denied being responsible for the girl's death.
Police had initially assumed several perpetrators but released two suspects from custody because the evidence against them was insufficient.
Prosecutors also emphasized during the proceedings that she was convinced that Kana was alone at the time of the attack.
Rapes are almost part of everyday life in South Africa: every year there are about 64 000 complaints of sexual violence.
This is almost ten times as much as in Germany, but with 82 million people has significantly more inhabitants than South Africa (50 million).
Police and women's associations in South Africa, however, estimate the dark figure for sexual violence at 10 to 25 times.
Jumbo visits the Black Forest ham
Jumbo in search of the "best ham in the world": For the TV show "Galileo" visited the Pro7 star Bonndorf.
The speaker clarifies right at the beginning: The most important thing for the ham is the pork legs, turbomast is not a good prerequisite.
Can ham that you get in every supermarket, so be a quality product?
Uli, as head of the large ham factory in the Black Forest, Jumbo explains the working methods of the company "Adler" in Bonndorf.
"Germany's favourite ham," as Jumbo announces the Black Forest ham.
Only with hair protection and smock he comes inside the factory.
Jumbo, too, has to disinfect his hands before he is allowed to reach for the microphone.
Jumbo spotted for refreshment at fast food restaurant in Waldshut
"I've been able to work huge quantities here," marvel the television makers, Black Forest ham as a mass product.
16,000 clubs a week speak for themselves.
Weight, fat content, color: Uli explains what is important in terms of quality.
Most pigs are slaughtered at six months.
TV with level?
Details such as the ph value of the meat also play a role.
The darker the meat, the higher the ph value.
"I'm curious if you measure Roberto Blanco, what happens then," Jumbo cynically said.
Here, the level of private television is presented in pure culture.
But it's still worth watching.
The viewer sees that disassembling the clubs continues to be handmade.
Because: Black Forest ham is traditionally to be found without bones on the shelf.
The secret of ham is the spice mixture with the salt.
Smoke tower and smoke firing visually reminiscent Jumbo of a "submarine."
The trick: fir branches provide the special taste of Black Forest ham here in the smoke.
For 20 days in the smoke finally make the ham almost black.
"Now the disc disappears in the jumbo," he announces the test eating.
"It's really delicious," the TV star concludes.
This will allow everyone involved to live.
Do you think travel agencies are a thing of the past thanks to the Internet?
Flight Centre appears to be resisting the trend.
The company has ramped up its full-year profit outlook, expecting record revenues from holidaymakers in Australia and the UK.
The travel company now expects an adjusted annual profit before taxes of between 325 and 340 million dollars, compared to 305 to 315 million dollars in its earlier forecast.
If the current targets are reached, it means growth of 12 to 17 percent compared to the record profit of 290.4 million dollars in the period 2011/12.
As managing director Graham Turner announced, Flight Centre made eight percent profit in the first half of the year and posted a strong start into the second half of the year, especially in the Australian and British private business.
“Since the beginning of the year, our ten countries have been profitable and some of them are well on their way towards towards record annual earnings before interest and taxes,” he said
These include Australia and Britain, which are typically among our strongest takers.
In Australia, the leisure industry has recovered in the second half of the year, balancing a slightly weaker market for business travel.
Things went similarly well for Flight Centre's leisure business in the UK, while business customers also spent less here.
U.S. business recovered compared to the seasonally weakener first half of the year, so a full-year profit is expected for the third year in a row.
Shares of Flight Centre rose 3 cent to 38.20 dollars yesterday.
Schools are encouraged to have a greater focus on math, spelling and grammar
In courses on English literature, pupils will now have to cover at least one play by Shakespeare, a 19th-century novel, romantic lyricism and contemporary British novels from 1914 onwards.
“Unseen texts” are also included in the examination to stimulate broader reading.
The combined course from English literature and language will be abolished.
From 2015, pupils will have to take an independent GCSE exam for language, with strong incentives to choose English literature as a separate qualification.
The Ministry of Education will publish the new curriculums for English and mathematics tomorrow – the first subjects that have been radically redesigned.
Other core subjects will be changed next year.
In a separate initiative, Ofqual, the regulator for examinations, will announce a reorganization of GCSE structures, including a new grading system and less coursework.
In a speech over the summer, Education Minister Michael Gove said that there is a “broad consensus that we need to reform our examination system in order to restore public confidence,” stressing that GCSEs would become “more challenging, ambitious, and stricter.”
Studies show that in English schools there is less time spent on math – 116 hours per year or three hours per school year – than in most other countries.
On the other hand, Australian schools teach an average of 143 hours a year and pupils in Singapore receive 138 hours.
Although there will be no formal requirement to provide more time for mathematics in the timetable, coalition circles say that the comprehensive math examination – combined with a stronger weighting of the subject in rankings – will presumably encourage schools to spend more hours.
In the curriculum, more attention is paid to “problems from real life”, including financial mathematics.
Lovely animals and delicious cakes
The most beautiful rabbits of different breeds and colours will be presented this weekend in the Bürgerhaus.
Ratings have already been carried out on Thursday
Once again, the W514 local association is hosting this large exhibition.
Breeders of the clubs existing in Soest County show their best animals as well as young Wickeder hobby owners their rabbits here.
On Thursday, six judges carried out the evaluation of the numerous rabbits and were able to honor outstanding animals.
Visitors to the show will be well informed, as the assessment of all animals is attached to the cages.
Mayor Hermann Arndt performs the official opening of the exhibition Saturday at 2:30 p.m.
The rabbit show is open to all visitors on Saturday from 10 a.m. and on Sunday from 10 a.m. to 3:30 p.m., followed by the award ceremony.
The guests are also invited to a raffle equipped with stately prizes and to a deliciously stocked cafeteria.
Pamela Anderson separates from her legendary blonde curls and performs for the first time with a new bubi head.
Pamela's blonde curls have been famous since her role on the television series Baywatch.
Pamela Anderson is the latest star to shock his fans with a drastic new haircut.
Former Baywatch beauty has separated from its long blonde curls in favour of a platinum-blonde short-haircut.
The 46-year-old actress presented her latest look in Los Angeles on Wednesday and also put a snapshot on her Twitter page.
It's the first time in 20 years that the blonde sex symbol has short hair, and we're loving the moral change.
What do you think of Pammy's hair?
Tell us your opinion below in the comments.
Salem: Johanna Rahner at the Ecumenical Conversation Forum
At the next Ecumenical Conversation Forum on Monday, October 21, in the New Museum of the Castle, it is about the topic "To hell with hell - theological reflections on today's handling of the last things".
Speaker is Professor Johanna Rahner.
The Ecumenical Discussion Forum is an initiative of the Catholic and Protestant parishes of Salem, Salem Castle and Bodenseekreis Cultural Office.
Johanna Rahner wants to investigate in the forum the question of how today the "last things" after death.
In the past centuries, people would have known too much of it, relying on those images of heaven and hell, "which still characterize our ideas to this day", the contemporary prophetic doctrine of the hopes for completion is characterized by a certain embarrassment or even speechlessness.
"The greater hope has been driven out to Christians by religious criticism," says the speaker.
“It provided us with so many question marks that we seem to have lost the exclamation points.”
Johanna Rahner continues: "But the decisive challenge remains the annoyance of death".
Associated with this is the question of that longing, "which dreams that in the end everything may be good".
Johanna Rahner, born 1962 in Baden-Baden, studied Catholic theology and biology at the Albert-Ludwigs-University Freiburg from 1982 to 1989.
She received her doctorate in 1997 as Dr. theol., also in Freiburg.
Habilitation for Fundamental Theology and Ecumenical Theology followed in 2003 at the Westphalian Wilhelms-University.
Since 2010 Johanna Rahner has held a Chair of Systematic Theology at the Institute of Catholic Theology at the University of Kassel.
Geislingen: More than 100 young Catholics from Kleiner Heuberg reaffirm their creed in confirmation
In two services 101 15- and 16-year-old young people have been confirmed to the pastoral care unit Am Kleinen Heuberg.
In the morning, the Laudato-Si group accompanied the church service, in the afternoon the youth choir The Spirit.
With the reception of their firmament, the youths said yes to their baptism and asked for the power of the Holy Spirit.
In the past few months, under the direction of deacon Reiner Dehner and the help of numerous parishioners, the firmlings had prepared for the big feast day.
In different projects, they got to know different tasks of Christian community life and experienced the Youth Day together in Untermachtal.
Illmensee: Second mountain bike race inspires participants
In wonderful weather, 214 cyclists came to Illmensee to tackle the circuit over the hill chains around the lake.
Ulrich Knobel from the organising sports club Illmensee announces this.
Among the participants, especially on the short distance over 15 kilometers and 300 vertical meters, were also numerous cyclists who wanted to practice their hobby once in a competitive atmosphere.
Two top-of-the-range junior riders were also at the start in this competition: Felix Bader from Bad Waldsee and Pascal Treubel from Aach-Linz achieved good rankings at the German Championships with the recommendation.
As expected, these two were able to stand out from the field.
As in the previous year, Felix Bader was able to save a small time advantage to the finish and defended his title as the overall winner of the short distance.
Pascal Treubel was able to place second again.
Among the ladies, it was Theresa Duelli from Team Nabtraum who made it to the top of the podium.
Anne Adel from Illmensee and Leonie Treiber from Owingen came in second and third place.
A pleasing development is the increasing number of drivers who live or work in the community.
Not only in terms of performance, the whole range of athletes was represented.
The age structure also ranged from ten years for the youngest up to over 70 years among the oldest starters.
In the main race this year there were even more absolute top athletes at the start.
Four drivers were able to break away from the top regional drivers.
Including Philipp Pangerl, a mountain bike semi-professioner from the Black Tusk racing team.
Pangerl, together with a team-mate, has already been world champion in twelve-hour driving twice.
This year he became European champion in this special discipline.
Roland Ballerstedt, who already owns two German championship titles in duathlon.
After 45 kilometers and 900 vertical meters, there was an eyelaze final in the finish line in front of the three-lake hall.
Pangerl was the first to race across the finish line.
Just one second behind, the experienced Ballerstedt was able to secure second place in the overall standings.
At the same time Hermann and Warthmann finished third in the overall standings.
In the women's standings Christiane Cohsmann was the quickest in the main race.
Areane Blersch from Binzwangen secured second place ahead of Natascha Werner from Stuttgart.
Athletes and volunteers were very satisfied with the smooth and well-organized course of the event.
Travelling through mud, rivers and jungles for free medical care
Dr Georges Bwelle brings free medical assistance to remote villages in Cameroon
Bwelle and his team spend almost every weekend treating hundreds of patients
There aren't many doctors in the West African country, just one for 5,000 people
Vote here or with your mobile device
Dr. Georges Bwelle is one of the top 10 of the “CNN Heroes of 2013”.
You can vote for him or any of the other heroes in the top 10 to vote for the “CNN Hero of the Year.”
The winner receives 250,000 dollars to continue his extraordinary work.
For 21 years, Georges Bwelle looked at how his sick father repeatedly lost consciousness and traveled to hospitals that didn't have the means to help him.
Jamef Bwelle was injured in a car accident near Yaoundé, the capital of Cameroon in 1981.
At first he had only a broken arm, but then an inflammation formed, which spread to his brain and caused a hematoma there, which was supposed to affect him for the rest of his life.
"There were no neurosurgeons in Cameroon," says Georges Bwelle.
We'd've taken him out of Cameroon if we'd had the money.
Instead, Bwelle spent years accompanying his father to overcrowded clinics and hospitals to get the treatment they had to offer.
"It's not easy," Bwelle commented.
You can leave the house at five in the morning and run to the hospital to be the first, and yet you're not first.
There are a lot of patients.
Some people even die because they have to wait.
The situation has barely changed since Bwelle's father died in 2002.
In Cameroon, there is only one doctor for 5,000 people each, according to the World Health Organization.
In contrast, the relationship in the United States lies with a doctor for 413 people.
But even if there were a doctor for them, most Cameroonians could not afford the visit.
Two out of five people in the country live below the poverty line and nearly three-quarters of health spending in the country is private.
“The only problem they have is poverty,” Bwelle said.
Poverty makes it impossible to enjoy life.
His father and so many of his compatriots were suffering to see the decision to do something about it in Bwelle.
Dr. Georges Bwelle and his volunteer team conducted 700 free surgeries last year.
He became a doctor himself and works as a vascular surgeon at the Central Hospital in Yaoundé.
In addition, he founded a non-profit organization called ASCOVIME, which travels to rural areas on weekends and offers free medical care there.
Since 2008, he and his volunteer group have helped just under 32,000 people
Almost every Friday he and up to 30 people squeeze into minibuses and drive with the laced medical equipment on the roof over unpaved terrain to the villages that need help.
Everything doesn't always go smoothly.
Even more than once they had to push the vehicles through rivers and mud.
But when they arrive, they are received like heroes: a feast, singing and dancing and the best accommodation that the place has to offer.
In these villages, free medical care is really a reason to celebrate and wave – with his broad smile and unlimited energy – is happy to be part of the cheerful celebration.
The next morning, the team then begins examining hundreds of patients.
“We treat 500 people per trip,” says Bwelle.
They come from a radius of 60 kilometers around the village – on foot.
Each of these weekend clinics provides medical care in a range of areas.
Many people are treated for malaria, tuberculosis, malnutrition, diabetes, parasites and sexually transmitted diseases.
Others receive crutches, donated glasses or free birth certificates – a document required for the school, but cannot afford many poor families.
In the evening, the team carries out simple surgeries with local anesthesia.
The surgeries usually take place in a school, town hall, or apartment; after the procedure, patients get up and go into the recreation area to make room for the neighbor.
The lighting for the surgery and the disinfection equipment is fed by the group's generator and that's how Bwelle and his volunteers work until the early morning hours on Sunday.
It's an exhausting workload, but the village musicians usually help keep the team motivated.
“They’re drumming all night so that we can stay awake and continue our work,” Bwelle says.
On Sunday, the team then drives back to the city, tired but proud of the work done.
The group – a mixture of Cameroonian physicians and foreign medical students – performed 700 free surgeries last year and knows that with its work, it makes a huge difference to the people it helps.
One man explained that the free operation of a herriate, which he had received, now allows him to return to work.
“It’s changing my family’s future,” the man said.
In addition to the weekend clinics and his work as a surgeon in the hospital, Bwelle also works at night in private clinics in Yaoundé.
With this second job, he finances 60% of his charitable activity, according to Bwelle; the rest comes from private donations.
“I really don’t know when he’s asleep,” says Katie O’Malley, who is studying medicine at Drexel University in Philadelphia for the second year and volunteers in Bwelle’s group.
He's always either in the hospital or trying to raise money for the organization so he can go on these campaigns.
For medical and nursing students like O'Malley, who come from the U.S. and Europe and support Bwelle, that's an opportunity to gain hands-on experience they'd never get at home.
“We can be involved in surgeries, where we’re helping to dallow off blood or keep the instruments for Dr. Bwelle,” explains O’Malley.
That would never be possible in America as a second-year medical student.
The students usually pay for their trip to Cameroon themselves and frequently bring donated medical supplies.
But once they are in Yaoundé, Bwelle takes care of accommodation, transport and instructions.
“He’s undoubtedly a hero,” O’Malley says.
He dedicates his life to this organization and his desire to help the Cameroonian people knows no limits.
The almost constant work is not a burden for Bwelle.
Helping others to a happier life and thus fulfilling a promise he has given to his father gives him great pleasure.
“I’m so happy when I do this work,” says Bwelle.
And I'm thinking of my father.
I'm hoping he'll see what I'm doing.
I'm doing it to make people laugh again and to alleviate the pain.
Visit ASCOVIME’s website and learn how you can help.
Excitingly mastered the fight
The role of Santiago, the old man in Hemingway's novel "The Old Man and the Sea," is cut into Horst Janson.
Janson himself is an old rabbit in his profession, acting.
No one can fool the old man Santiago while fishing.
That's just how the two complement each other magnificently.
One almost thinks to be able to see the wrinkles in the neck of the old man, of which the fisher boy Manolo speaks.
If Santiago is tired of life bent on the chair, that's real with Janson.
In addition, the role of the old fisherman is through and through a character role that Janson masters excellently.
Hemingway told the story of a fighter, a courageous man who does not give up and ultimately wins the fight against the fish through his knowledge and also his willpower.
Janson is on stage in long distances as a sole actor.
He manages to focus the attention of the audience on himself, so his monologues aren't bored for a moment.
His efforts to finally pull the fish to the surface of the water are so real that viewers forget that the sea only consists of blue-lit water bottles.
Director Jens Hasselmann lets the old man act in the boat in front of the fishing village.
In the course of the performance, he puts the active scene in the light: the hut of Santiago, the bar of the singer Marie-Luise Gunst, who sings with her songs and her magnificent voice.
The four musicians play Cuba's rhythm and are also guests of the bar.
Tourism: Descention to the Romans
At a length of around 120 metres, a sewerage sewer from the Roman period is preserved below Cologne.
If you visit Cologne, you won't get past the cathedral.
The two towers protrude into the sky and make it the second tallest church building in Europe.
But only a few visitors who marvel at the two filigree steeples suspect that there is also a lot to discover under the cathedral.
Rainer Schulze is an expert into Cologne's underworld.
Meeting point for his guided tours is the service center of Köln-Tourismus directly at the cathedral.
The tour begins quite unspectacularly: "We're starting in the underground car park," says Schulze.
In the seventies, the city fathers wanted a car-friendly city, and therefore of the remnants of the old city wall, only what was not too big.
Most users of the car park are therefore rushing past the Roman remains today and only park their car.
But if you take a closer look, you will discover an archaeological site that has been separated by metal griding rods in the parking deck D2.
You can see about five-meter-high ruins made of palm-sized stones.
"It's remnants of the Roman city wall that was built in the second half of the first century AD," Schulze recounts.
Before going back to daylight, Schulze lets the participants on the upper park level take a look at a sinister, 15-meter-deep shaft.
This is the fountain of the old cathedral, which is unfortunately hardly noticed.
The next stop of the tour is the praetorium, and here the participants descend back into the depths.
We are witnessing the official residence of the once most powerful man north of the Alps, the praetor of the CCAA.
What CCAA means, a visitor wants to know.
"The abbreviation stands for "Colonia Claudia Ara Agrippinensium", which in good German means "city of Roman law and city of the Agrippinensians, founded under Emperor Claudius at the site of the altar for the imperial cult".
And the praetorium is the former governor's palace, the power center of the Roman empire on the Rhine.
From the antechamber of the exhibition there will be a tunnel access to the Roman sewer, which is preserved here at a length of around 120 metres.
"You can quietly walk along the so-called Cloaca Maxima if you want," Schulze cheers the guests on.
Schulze still has a surprise.
After getting the key at the checkout, he leads the group to a 16-meter-deep, walk-in shaft.
"We're now in the mikvah, a ritual Jewish bathroom," he explains.
The Jewish community built its bath deep into the soil to get to the groundwater, which was indispensable for the ritual washings.
To this day, in the lower end of the mikvah, whose origins date back to the eighth-century, different water levels can be seen due to changing levels of the Rhine.
The mikveh will become part of an archaeological zone on the town hall square, for which the construction work is to begin shortly.
However, a few more years will pass until completion.
Leg almost chopped off: New edition of rocker trial
A bloody attack in the rocker milieu will be reopened after more than four years.
A trial for attempted manslaughter, dangerous bodily harm and property damage against two 30-year-olds has begun before the District Court Frankfurt (Oder).
It is accompanied by strict safety precautions.
The defendants remained silent at the start.
They are said to have been active in the rocker scene and belonged to the Bandidos at the time.
Prosecutors accuses them of stabbing and smashing three men into a car together with unknown accomplices in Finowfurt in June 2009.
All the victims were seriously injured, a man almost lost his leg.
The victims are said to have belonged to the competing Group Hells Angels.
One is said to have been a high-ranking member from Berlin.
This was preceded by a chase with several cars.
According to the indictment, it was about precinct fights and a demonstration of power.
The two defendants were acquitted in an initial trial in early 2012.
A court spokesman said at the time that it had not been possible to prove that the men had actually been involved in the bloody feud.
The perpetrators had been wearing masks and had not been recognised.
The Federal Supreme Court overturned the verdict after revision of the public prosecutor's office and referred it back to the regional court for retrial.
One of the defendants is currently in open execution because of another offence.
At the beginning it was mainly about the reconstruction of the processes on the crime night.
As a witness, the then investigating commissioner was invited.
The focus was on mobile phone conversations of the defendants and other persons that the police had wiretapped.
It's difficult that rockers in court almost always stick to a law that's baring for them - and don't testify.
This also applies to victims.
In the first trial, only one of the victims had unpacked.
Prosecutor Stefan Golfier of the public prosecutor's office in Frankfurt (Oder) said on request that new traces had surfaced since the last trial.
For the trial, eleven more days of negotiations are scheduled.
The disposable society doesn't think
The night was long, the music loud and the mood good, but at some point it goes home.
Only in the bellies of the people in the passenger and back seat, the faminity is noticeable.
Sure, one or the other cocktail or sparkling wine may be responsible for this!
What could be more obvious than going to the next drive-in chilled to treat yourself to a little something?
I admit, I like to be part of it late at night or early in the morning.
A few fries, a cola, a burger - and then nothing like going home and into bed!
But on the way home, the obvious differences between me and other hungry disco walkers are noticeable.
Because you have to miss that you throw your fast food bags out of the car window with constant regularity?!
Especially on the weekends, discarded paper bags lie on roadsides and in parking lots.
"Why?" I wonder.
Are the people too stupid to take the bags home or to the next bin?
It's not just that the paper toy is ruining the landscape.
Bags have already obscured transport facilities such as guardrails and thus triggered accidents.
About such thought "disposable society" but does not think, presumably she does not even know what this word actually means.
If you travel more often in the evening, especially with several people in the car, you know what a back seat can look like after-celebrated nights: clothes, bottles and other rubbish pile up there.
A few bags more or less wouldn't make anything - in contrast to the street, where no one wants to trample on the food leftovers of others.
I already see the day coming when the towns and communities are too stupid - and the fast food chains have to demand deposits for their bags.
Obama's Withdrawal on Health Care Policy
After intense criticism yesterday, President Obama backtracked on his frequently repeated, unequivocal promise: “If you like your health insurance, then you can keep it.”
After hundreds of thousands received a termination letter from their insurer, Republicans accused the president in recent days of deceiving the American public.
Yesterday, Obama relativized his original promise.
“The vast majority of citizens who have functioning health insurance can keep it,” he said during a speech in Boston.
In response to the numerous media reports on the terminations, Obama urged those Americans who have received such communications to look for new insurance on the open market.
Most citizens could thus receive better, more comprehensive health insurance for the same price or cheaper than forecast.
“They’re coming for a better deal,” he said.
The government described it as unsurprising that the five percent of the population, who have taken out insurance themselves, would have to look for a different tariff, as their contract did not meet the new standards under the Affordable Care Act.
“I want to tell these Americans directly, ‘You’ve earned betters,” Sebelius said at a hearing before the Houst House Energy and Commerce Committee.
Sebelius, which is responsible for implementing the Affordable Care Act, stated that the launch of the online platform had been “miserable” since October.
“I’m as frustrated and angry as everyone else,” she said.
I'm determined to regain your trust.
This utterance of an angry Sebelius to an adviser sitting behind her at yesterday's House-in-Department's hearsement was recorded after an argument with Republican Rep. Billy Long, referring to whether she should be required to participate in Obamacare.
After more than three hours of hearing, Long Sebelius repeatedly afflicted the question of why the “architect” of the Affordable Care Act had not voluntarily waived state-sponsored insurance in order to take out a tariff at HealthCare.gov itself, which she now wants to sell to millions of Americans.
Jerusalem government fails to confirm attack on Syrian airbase
An American government official who wished to remain anonymously claimed on Thursday that Israeli warplanes had attacked an airbase in Syria's Latakia a day earlier.
The target was "missiles and related equipment," which the Israeli government feared was intended for Lebanon's Hezbollah, according to CNN.
Syrian and Lebanese media, as well as Al-Arabiya, had earlier reported that the base in the port city of Latakia had been shelled during the night from Wednesday to Thursday, blaming Israelis.
Israel's Channel 2 television station showed satellite images of the base in Latakia, on which Russian S-125 Newa defense missiles as well as a battery of SA-3 missiles could be seen, according to Channel 2 have a range of 35 kilometers and can carry warheads up to 70 kilograms.
Uncertain The government in Jerusalem has not officially confirmed the attack.
However, the Reuters news agency quotes an also anonymous Israeli government official as saying that he believes that Israel carried out the attack, but is not certain.
In any case, a spokesperson for the Israeli Defense Ministry said: "We do not comment on these reports".
Journalist Ron Ben-Yishai emphasized in the daily Yedioth Ahronoth that the Syrian regime had already tried, partly successfully, to deliver surface-to-air missiles to Hezbollah.
The Israeli government had repeatedly warned that any attempt by Syria to supply Hezbollah with chemical or other dangerous weapons means crossing a "red line" that a military response would follow.
John Kerry declares in an unprecedented admission that U.S. espionage has "gone inappropriatelyness
John Kerry hinted with an unexpected admission a softening of U.S. defensive stance regarding its surveillance programs by saying the espionage had "gone inadequately too far" in certain cases.
The secretary-officiary also admitted that, together with Barack Obama, he was responsible for switching to “autopilot” when the explosive revelations of whistleblower Edward Snowden about the NSA’s espionage activities became public.
Due to the leaked information, the U.S. government has fallen into a diplomatic storm of indignance on the part of its allies.
During a video-linked speech at the Open Government conference in London, Kerry said: “Quite without question, the president and I and others in the government have learned of some things that have almost expired by autopilot, simply because there was the ability to do so, dating back to the Second World War and the Difficult Years of the Cold War, as well as, of course, 9/11.”
He then confessed as the first high-ranking member of the U.S. administration that U.S. espionage had crossed a border, but also emphasized that no rights had been abused.
He said, “In some cases this has gone unreasonablely too far.”
The President is determined to provide clarity and therefore carries out a thorough investigation so that no one has to feel that abuse takes place here.
I assure you that the rights of innocent persons will not be compromised in this process.
Kerry insisted, however, that the NSA was a force of the good and many lives had been saved by its surveillance operations.
“We’re dealing with a new world where people are willing to blow themselves up.”
There's radical extremism in the world that's obsessed with killing people, blowing up and attacking governments.
So what if you were able to intercept and stop this before it happens?
We've actually prevented plane crashes, attacks on buildings and the assassination of people because we've learned of such plans in advance.
Meanwhile, U.S. legislators will travel to Europe to dispell concerns about alleged American espionage and convince Europeans of the need to continue working with the U.S. on counterterrorism efforts, the chairman of the Senate subcommittee on European affairs stated Thursday.
Senator Chris Murphy, from Connecticut, said he had spoken to representatives of the European Parliament and others this week and was concerned about their threats to no longer get involved in anti-terrorism organizations due to the frustration over National Security Agency surveillance.
"It is very important for U.S. national security interests to keep Europeans on board in view of our joint efforts against terrorism," Murphy, who sits in the Senate for the first time as a Democrat and serves as chairman of the Senate Subcommittee on Foreign Relations and European Affairs, said in an interview in Washington.
In addition, I go to Europe to make it clear that we must continue to work together in counter-terrorism, regardless of their annoyance at NSA programmes.
Media reports that the NSA has captured millions of telephone data in Europe have strained relations with some U.S. allies, even though the head of the intelligence agency this week said the reports were inaccurate and based on a misunderstanding of the metadata collected by NATO allies and exchanged with the United States.
Other revelations cited documents leaked from Snowden, according to which the NSA monitored Chancellor Angela Merkel's cell phone and up to 34 other heads of state worldwide.
The head of national intelligence, James Clapper, defended espionage among allies as necessary, declaring that this was common on both sides.
In all the tumult, Murphy said his authority was preparing the trip of Congress scheduled to take place this year, and hoped the delegation would include members from both parties and chambers.
The names of the other participating deputies are due to be published in the coming days
He said the exact itinerary is still being worked out.
While Murphy emphasized that the purpose of the trip was to improve relationships, he also said that there would be some “serious words.”
He said European governments had to be honest with their people about the kind of espionage programs they themselves used for years.
“Although we can change our surveillance programmes and thus better protect the rights of Europeans, they also have to come to terms with the fact that we are not the only ones spying,” Murphy said.
Meanwhile, John Kerry is planning a trip to the Middle East and Poland over the weekend to deal with the resentment about the U.S. strategy in Syria, Egypt and Iran, as well as U.S. surveillance activities.
A stubborn injury to the ankles threatens to prevent the use of Nicolai Müller in the Bundesliga away game of FSV Mainz 05 at FC Augsburg.
Mainz bangs for a deployment of Nicolai Müller.
He'll try to train with the squad tomorrow.
Until now, he could only do that individually.
"We're going to decide on his stake in the short term," coach Thomas Tuchel said.
The 40-year-old hopes to have his best goalscorer with six goals.
Because the Mainzers already have to do without the injured top performers Niki Zimling, Julian Baumgartlinger and Niko Bungert.
Australiar woman appeals prison sentence in Thailand
A 21-year-old woman from Sydney who was sentenced to 15 days in prison in Phuket for falsely claiming to have been raped by a taxi driver has appealed against the verdict and has been released on bail.
Stevie Rochelle Bamford was initially found guilty by a provincial tribunal in Phuket on June 15 of making a false statement after citing to Thai police that a local taxi driver had raped her in the morning hours on Sunday, June 10, while two other men detained her.
Later, however, the footage from a video camera showed that she had safely entered her hotel after being separated from her Australian boyfriend.
Police in Phuket interrogated Bamford for two days until she confessed to having come up the story.
Until the trial, she was in police custody at the scene.
Bamford was sentenced to a 15-day prison sentence at a low-security detention centre on the outskirts of Phuket, rather than in a prison for adult women.
She is the daughter of Peter Tunks, a former player in Australia's Rubgy league who reached out to the foreign ministry in Canberra asking for help for his daughter.
Tunks explained to the Sunday Telegraph in Sydney that the whole family was “extremely concerned” about his daughter’s well-being and wanted to have her back in Australia.
“Of course we’re very worried, but we hope she’ll be back home as soon as possible,” Tunks said.
Bamford has appealed the verdict and is on a bail of 50,000 baht on the loose.
It has been reported in Australian reports that, meanwhile, she is on holiday at Krabi in southern Thailand.
On the part of the tribunal, it could be heared that Bamford would be represented by a local lawyer in Phuket and had been warned that the appeal could also lead to the imposition of a higher sentence of up to two years in an adult prison.
However, it is also possible that after the recent assassination of travel clerk Michelle Smith in Phuket, Thailand could try to improve its ailing tourist image, and that is why an acquitance occurs.
